        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. 1 Tim EDITOR of " The Examiner" invites corre- spondence on all subjects of public interest. Lomas should be brief and pointed. Their abject must be to impart or seek information. set abuse. Lwerszs must in all cases be accompanied by the name and address of the writer. COMMIINICATIONS received early in the week— except urgent matters--can always be given the best attention.</p>
        <p>A PLEA FOR THE CRIPPLES. 1 Sir,—Would you kindly allow me a little space in your paper to suggest a scheme under which cripples could help themselves more than they do at the present time? If the wealthy people of Warrington would buy hand-made articles, many a cripple could be helped, that is, sucri as hand spinning by the spinning wheel; also fretwork ao4l many ether things they could make by hand. How much good the rich could do by helping ethers less fortunate than themselves. We have a'Guild of Help. Let us have a guild that will heap cripples to keep themselves. Now is the time for the rich to lo3lp. They will be happier for helping others. A WARRINGTON CRIPPLE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0005" n="art0005">
        <head>HOW TO LIVE WELL, WISELY, AND ECONOMICALLY.</head>
        <p>HOW TO LIVE WELL, WISELY, AND ECONOMICALLY.</p>
        <p>- Sir,—The problem of feeding the nation in peace and ii war has been brought to the front during the last week or so as 'being one of the most impoltant things upon which the safety of the country depends. May I therefore take this opportunity of reminding your -readers that the one article of food which may become short and which may become dear is nimal food, whether in the form of flesh meat or of fish? The experience of the centuries has demon- strated that this class of food is not essential to the health and stamina of the race. We must never forget that Ciesar conducted his great campaigns with a corn-fed army, and that to-day some of the finest troops in the world, hardy and untiring, live upon .cereals, dates, figs, oil, and a few vegetables. No one in England need fear for reduction in health or strength so long as the corn ships do not fail us. I would especially like to draw attention to the value of the old 'English dish of Furmenty, which is made by stewing wheat until it is quite soft and tender, with a little spice, and then adding sugar and milk. Recipes will be gladly forwarded by the Order of the Golden Age, Brompton-road, S.W., to all clergy, district visitors, and teachers who want to help the poor people under their care to a )knowledge of how, in this present crisis, they may live well, wisely and -economically. J. OLDFIELD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <head>WAR WORKING PARTIES: A CRITICISM.</head>
        <p>WAR WORKING PARTIES: A CRITICISM.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l shall esteem it a favour if you could insert in your current issue the following, as I believe many well-intentioned people err for lack of information about-these matters. Everyone capable of rendering service is now, in some form or other, making his little-sacri- fice. The spirit so displayed is admirable, yet we think much unconscious harm is being done by this rush of voluntary service in channels where people sell their labour. Women workers particularly are being hit by this introduction of "lady labour," supplying a demand that otherwise would be met through industrial channels. ,It is only necessary to point out that seamstresses and garment- makers are unemployed for the lack of work now being performed by voluntary workers. The women so displaced must in a short time fall on the funds of the Distress 'Committee. It will be seen that instead of assisting poverty we are actually creating it by good-intentioned yet misguided service. If, for instance, our middle class devoted their spare time throwing up earthworks or digging trenches, ,n hilst the ordinary able-bodied navvy or general labourer was. unemployed, the incongruity would be im- mediately detected. Yet it is a parallel case. I venture to suggest that the best service can be rendered by non-combatants in assisting the continuation of ordinary employment as far as possible. It is time manufacturers and shop- keepers spoke out upon this matter. Patriotism, like all other human sentiment, tends to lop- sided development, and unless regulated may easily defeat its object. It is a paradox of economics that when money is urgently needed those who have it grip tight. Even though they now lessen demand by limit- ing purchases, there is no reason why the diffi- culty should be made greater by voluntary sup- plies in a market already falling for want purchasers. Trades and Labour Council.</p>
        <p>CHAS. DUXES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>CHESHIRE INSURANCE COMMITTEE</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE INSURANCE COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>First Meeting.</p>
        <p>The first meeting of the newly-re-appointed Insurance Committee for Cheshire was held at the Crewe Arms Hotel, Crewe, on Saturday afternoon, 34 members being present. The Clerk (Mr. Frank T. West) read commu- nications from the National Health Insurance Commissioners and the Cheshire County Coun- cil notifying the membership of the committee. Mr. J. 0. Brandrith, Northwich, was unani- mously re-elected chairman of the committee. The committee considered the advisability of the admission of the Press to the future general meetings of the committee, and after careful consideration, it was resolved that the Clerk be instructed to issue to the Press an epitome of the proceedings of the future general meetings of the committee. Dr. T. H. Poyton, the county tuberculosis officer, has been called away on active service. and Dr. Meredith Young, the county medical officer of health 'a-as asked to act as medical ad- viser to the committee during the absence of the County Tuberculosis Officer.</p>
        <p>It was resolved that the future meetings of the committee beheld on a Wednesday in April, July, October and January. Mr. Deakin, Northwich, drew the attention of the committee to the difficult y of obtaining cer- tain drugs owing to the war, and asked whether the Clerk had circularised the doctors and chem- ists in the county relati,ve to the matter. The Clerk re plied in flie affirmative.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>CARTONS' WHEAT SEtOS</head>
        <p>CARTONS' WHEAT SEtOS</p>
        <p>New and regenerated breeds of seed wheats yielding over 'ten quarters per acre are fully descrihed in an illustrated catalogue just issued by the well-known firm of scientific farm plant breeders, Messrs. Gartons, Limited, of War- rington. At the present time, when there is a general desire to sow increased areas of land to the culture of wheat so as to strengthen the food supplies of the country, the most profit- able breeds should only be sown. All aggres- sive agriculturists should therefore consult this publication, which may be had free from the headquarters of Gartons, Limited, Agricultural Seed Experts, Warrington.</p>
        <p>One of the things you can't get for nothing is experience.</p>
        <p>a kicker. A mule can beat you at</p>
