      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>BRIGHT PROSPECTS FOR CEREALS. Although the effects of the war have not. brought about "famine" prices for wheats and other cereals, it is an admitted fact that even' at present &amp;gazes, wheat, *Alicia is still King of the Cereals, is commanding prices which make' profitable returns to the cultivator. There is at present every reason to anticipate that pre- sent figures will be fully maintained, if not advanced, for when it is taken into considera; tion that the sowing of wheat this autumn: must, of necessity, be greatly restricted, if not entirely 'cancelled, in those countries where warfare is at present being waged—Russia, France, Belgium, Gerniany, Austria, and pro- bably others—it will at once be apparent that if the war should terminate before the harvest of Lotls, -these countries will be purchasers in- stead of, under normal conditions, being sellers of wheats. It should also be remembered that on account of the disturbance the present har- vest will, in all probability, be to a great extent neglected and destroyed in those coun- tries. These facts should be borne in mind by those who hove opportunities of increasing-their acreages for wheat sowing this autumn, as it is very evident that high prices of wheats will rule during at least the next two or three years. An increased acreage devoted to wheat culture will also prove to be an additional asset to the food supplies of this country. Altogether, the outlook for agriculture in this country is at the present time full a promise.—GartonS' Catalogue for Autumn, 1914.</p>
        <p>LORD MILNER'S WARNING TO BRITISH FARMERS. • A timely warning to prepare against an in- evitable shortage in the world's supply of food- stuffs is given by .Lord Milner, who points out that although the present harvest is abundant, an immense decrease in production in 1915 must result from the fact that .all the able-bodied males of France, Germany, Austria and Russia are now engaged in fighting. Of the 650 million quarters of wheat and rye annually produced throughout the world 350 millions come from these countries, and other producing countries cannot possibly make up the deficiency. Lord Milner predicts that in the latter half of next year, if not before, all nations which live on wheat and rye will be competing fiercely for a share in the diminished supply. He adds We may hope that our own country will be better placed than its neighbours to obtain, at some price or other, a sufficient quantity of wheat to avert famine. But there can be no certainty of this, and in any case, being as we fortunately are, in a better position than other countries involved in the war, to turn our land to full account, it is surely a matter of supreme necessity to use every acre, which can profitably be employed in that manner, for the production of the most necessary of all foodstuffs. Farmers ought, says Lord Milner, to rise to "'the emergency of their own accord. They have it in their power, not only to save the country from an imminent catastrophe, but at the same time to benefit themselves if they will only act with promptitude.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN. HINTS FOR WAR VEGETABLE</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN. HINTS FOR WAR VEGETABLE</p>
        <p>CULTIVATION.</p>
        <p>The Board of Agriculture and Fisheries have issued a leaflet for the benefit of land cultiva- tors, large and small, who wish to bring fresh land under spade cultivation with a view of in- creasing their supplies of vegetables for either home consumption or for sale, during the com- ing months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>To Sow on Cultivated Land.</head>
        <p>To Sow on Cultivated Land.</p>
        <p>. On land which has carried previous crops the soil should be turned over with the spade, dig- ging in a dressing of stable manure if the land was not manured in the spring. On such land the following crops may be sown immediately : Early turnips (white and purple Milan), carrots (early Horn), onions (white Lisbon), lettuce (winter varieties), radish (French breakfast), spinach (winter or prickly).</p>
        <p>For Planting before Mid-September. The following vegetables might be planted from now up to mid-September :—Early cabbage (any early variety), broccoli (winter and spring varieties), borecole or kale (winter and spring varieties), leeks (Musselburgh).</p>
        <p>For Sowing in October and Early November. Sow the following: Broad beans, early peas. Plant all varieties of cabbage and kale raised from seed sown in August. These plants will yield crops in the following spring and early summer, but too late to allow of early potatoes being planted after them. They could, how- ever, be succeeded by onions, broccoli, Brussels sprouts, kale or carrots.</p>
        <p>For Sowing or Planting in February and March. Much land may not be ready for cultivation till the spring; the following crops are suitable for land that has been lying vacant during the. winter, beginning in February or as soon as favourable: Potatoes (early varieties), beans (broad and kidney), peas (early and mid-season). cauliflowers (early), round spinach and spinach beet.</p>
        <p>BREAKING UP GRASS LAND. Many acres of grass land might be utilised, says the circular, for cultivating, especially near large towns. Such land should be treated thus: Skim off the layer of turf two to three inches in depth from a trench 18in. to 2ft. wide. Remove the first spit of soil below the turf, placing it at the end of the plot to be dug. Then stir the bottom soil to a depth of six inches with a fork or spade according to the nature of the soil. Now remove ' the layer of turf from the next trench, and lay it upside down . in the first trench, and the first spit of soil from the second trench placed on top, which will complete that trench. Proceed trench by trench in this fashion until the end of the plot is reached, when the turf and first spit of soil from the first trench ' will fill in the last one. Land of this character is often very fertile, and should give a good yield of potatoes or other crop the following summer. The circular explains how to treat derelict land, which will be useful. Copies of the leaflet (special No. 1) treating as above will be for- warded free of charge and post free on applica- tion to the Secretary. Board of Agriculture and Fisheries, 4, Whitehall Place, London, S.W. The letter of application need not be stamped.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>RASH COVERED BABY'S HEAD.</head>
        <p>RASH COVERED BABY'S HEAD.</p>
        <p>35, Derby-street, Ormskirk, Lancashire, Eng- land.—" When my baby was ,twenty-three days old she was affected with sores up the right arm from her little elbow over the shoulder blade, up the side of the neck, and her face was covered. It first appeared like a red rash, then it broke out into• small white blisters and then these broke and started running matter and formed into large sores. Baby used to start scratching them and her little face would pour blood. The right side all over was a mass of running sores. Also her head was covered with the same dis- figurement. We had to always keep both of her hands bandaged up and I have been compelled to walk the floor night after night with her, not ' ; ',Bina able to see her suffering so much. " After using three cakes of Cuticura Soap and a large tin of Cuticura Ointment we noticed a great change by the sores drying up. She is all. quite aear and clean from any disfigurement at I</p>
        <p>"My eldest girl's head broke out in the same way. Thanks to Cuticura Soap and Ointment she has never had a return of it and she has a beautiful head of hair now." (Signed) Mrs. Sarah L. Riding. January 17, 1914. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold -by chemists throughout the world. Skin book will be sent post free on request. Address post card to F. Newbery and Sons, 27, Cbarterhouse• square, London, E.C.</p>
        <p>. • _ •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 5, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 5, 1914.</p>
        <p>THE CYCLISTS' NOTEBOOK.</p>
        <p>A Three-Speed Gear a Modern Necessity.</p>
        <p>OUR PROLIFIC HARVESTS.</p>
        <p>The sweet song of the robin always reminds us that the end of the recognised cycling season is not far off. It is the only bikd that entertains us with its song now, and it will keep on until the thrushes and blackbirds start again next March. No wonder the robin is England's favourite; the only cry the blackbird makes now is its saucy or angry scolding; and the thrush am! the chaffinch are altogether silent. And yet, barring the flies of various kinds, this is a most delightful period for the cyclist. Cheshire orchards are loaded with apples and pears, and those who ride for an object can set off to Acton village and Crowton, and explore all the (district round there for the fruit-laden trees. They might also take a route through Altrincham to Northenden to see the onion crop. Don't laugh! The sunny gable of every cottage there is hung with this season's onions, drying ready for sale, and they look most appe- tising. What a glorious harvest Britain is hav- ing! It seems as if Providence has foreseen our needs this year, and beginning with the hay harvest and the early fruit, she is cramming our available storehouses with wheat and oats and clover, and soon with apples and pears and Plnms, and the root crops are just as prolific. It is one of the happiest sights the British cyclist can have this year—to see all this plenty being safely housed. And all this just because of twenty miles of blue salt water! Poor Bel- gium; poor France!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>THREE-SPEED GEARS.</head>
        <p>THREE-SPEED GEARS.</p>
        <p>A good many wise folks are talking about next year's mounts. No modern cycle is worth buying unless it has a three-speed gear. Even rides through the town are worth it. No greater help in riding through a tight place in traffic can be afforded to a cyclist than the power of slipping in the low gear of perhaps 50 or 60. The machine is steadied in a moment, its speed is brought down to walking pace, and gives the cyclist the same power over his machind that a motor cyclist experiences when he planks his feet on the ground, and paddles along. Baron Alverstone, the Lord Chief Jus- tice (in his youthful days a University athlete), stated that the 3-speed gear had improved his riding from two to three miles per hour, and that he felt sure a younger man could use it to still greater advantage. Personally, I prefer the Pedersen, whose big cog-wheels give one con- fidence in its strength, and its splendid com- bination of speed ratio-4, 6, 9—are of the high- est value. Thus with eight-inch cranks the middle gear on my machine (a B.S.A. all weathers) is 84. For hill cli*lbing the low is 56, and the high is 126 for a long downhill or glid- ing before the wind, and no one can guess the grand sweep of the cranks when the top gear is in. The great fault I have to find with most 3-speeds is that the ratios are not far enough separated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>PLACES WORTH SEEING. ...</head>
        <p>PLACES WORTH SEEING. ...</p>
        <p>The Roman Bridge near Strines.—Amid the picturesque scenery in the valley of the River Goyt, between Marple and. Strines, is found a stone bridge, which is credited with having been in existence since the time of the Romans. That there is some truth in the statement is evidenced by the weather-beaten stones of the structure. The bridge is a simple arch of stonework situated at the -bottom of a leafy dell. The sunlight glancing here and there on the water of the river, and the presence of a few rocks, worn and polished by the passage of generations of foot passengers, surround it with a setting which helps to make up a beautiful scene. The whole valley abounds in lovely scenery, but no tourist in the district should , pass by without making the slight detour from the main road necessary to visit this bridge.</p>
        <p>CLUB RUNS TO-DAY. Warrington C.C.—Delamere via Frodsham. Wesleyan C.C.—Glazebury. Sankey Bridges W.G.—Dutton. St. George's.—Gateacre.</p>
        <p>LAMP TIME. To-day, 7.55; next Saturday, 7.35. SILVER-STREET.</p>
