      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>NEW OVERSEER.</head>
        <p>A NEW OVERSEER.</p>
        <p>Lively DiEcuEsicn Over the Appoint-</p>
        <p>ment of Councillor Locker.</p>
        <p>HEATED PASSAGE AT ARMS.</p>
        <p>There were some lively, passages over the minutes of the Finance Committee at the meet- ing of the Warrington Town Council on Tues- day. Objection was taken to the appointment of Councillor Locker as an overseer. Councillor PEMBERTON thought the appoint- ment was of sufficient importance to have come before the General Purposes Committee. I am dead against the way, he said, these things go on, and really there seems to be a certain clique in the Council who think they are going to be on everything. lam dead against that sort of thing. It is not a personal matter; but I shall fight to the death against the system which is coming forward now. It has become a perfect public scandal. Men who have been connected with the Corporation for years cannot get these positions. They are filled up at meetings when few people are pres- ent. No regard is paid to the qualifications or claims of old members. It,is a deplorable state of affairs, and it is time it was put a stop to. I move that the minute be referred back. Councillor GOUGH seconded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>EXTREMELY FORTUNATE.</head>
        <p>EXTREMELY FORTUNATE.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS : In the first place I think the Council is exceedingly fortunate in getting the consent of Councillor Locker to take the job at all. It is not an appointment that is worth anyone's trouble to worry about. The respon- sibility rests with the Finance Committee. I do protest most emphatically, at a. time when the country is passing through a situation</p>
        <p>which has never existed before in the whole of its history, that we should fritter away any time at all in making suggestions of mala fides. Surely to God we have far more important work to do than to air our little petty, mean miser- able grievances—(hear, hear)—when we ought to be united as far as we possibly can and let our own indifferences sink absolutely in order that with undivided minds and purpose, and with all the resources at our disposal, we may apply ourselves to something infinitely more important than Councillor PEMBERTON : We are here for Town Council business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>AGAINST PERSONALITIES.</head>
        <p>AGAINST PERSONALITIES.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS : This contemptible, mean and paltry thing: for the first and for the last time I protest emphatically against the intro- duction of personalities such as those we have listened to to-night. The MAYOR suggested that nothing personal had been said. Councillor ROBERTS I beg your pardon. A distinct reference was made by Councillors Pemberton and Gough to the effect that the appointment of Councillor Locker was the act of a clique in the Council. The MAroR : No. One of them asked "Was it?" He was asking a question. Councillor ROBERTS: There are two ways of making an accusation. One is by direct charge land the other by insinuation. I would always prefer the direct charge as being the thing one' can deal with. (Hear, hear.) I say again that there can be no pleasure to any man coming to this Council to try to do municipal work just now if the actions of those who are absolutely above suspicion with regard to appointments of this kind are continually questioned. Refer- ence has been made to the filthiest period in the history of the Corporation. Councillor PEMBERTON : Hear, bear.</p>
        <p>It was</p>
        <p>the filthiest period. Councillor ROBERTS: It was, and you know it, and you were the man who made it. The MAYOR: Councillor Roberts, you must really contain yourself. Councillor ROBERTS It is impossible for one to contain oneself at a time like this. Councillor PEMBERTON : I made nothing out of it. The MAYOR: Order, order!</p>
        <p>Councillor BROADIIIIRkT was orry so much heat had been infused into the debate, bfit for once he had to support Councillor Pemberton, as the way such appointments were made was not in the interest of the best government of the town.</p>
        <p>Finally the anpointment of Councillor Locker was confirmed by a large majority.</p>
        <p>Defined. Teacher: "Can any of you tell me what is the meaning of a 'cursory glance'?" Tommy: "Please. ma'am, I can. It's when your pop looks hard at you and swears."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>METHODIST WORTHIES.</head>
        <p>METHODIST WORTHIES.</p>
        <p>Memorial Tablet Unveiled at Buckley-Street.</p>
        <p>sPIONSERS OF THE CAUSE.</p>
        <p>There were great rejoicings at Buckley-street Methodist Free 'Church on Monday evening, when the congregation assembled to witness Mrs. James Dodds unveil the tablet to the mem- ory of th(e Awnrkm's who had done so much for that cause in the past and whose ardent labours had enabled the church to rise to the important position it now holds in a district where there is ample scope for such sound Christian work as the &amp;A:ley-street congregation performs. The war unfortunately, hadian effect upon the attend- ance, and under more peaceful conditions the congregation would have been treble that which assembled an Monday night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>NAMES.</head>
        <p>THE NAMES.</p>
        <p>The memorial tablet, Which is let into the wall to the right of the pulpit•and platform, is plain, but striking in appearance. It contains a long list of names and there is space for more when additions become necessary, and in course of time this will also be filled as the present gen- eration, who labour for their great cause with as much ardour as did their forebears, are gath- ered to their forefathers. The names on the tablet recall a glorious past, and must act, as one speaker said, as an inspiration to the pre- sent generation. The names and dates of death are :</p>
        <p>At k. ipnslionns,tolBnB,ol. 885. -Sjoahranh Maria Ashton, 1886. John Ashton, 1887. Ann "Siddall, 1891. Eliza Simpson, 1895. William Price' 1895. Richard Siddall, 1897. Joseph Wibberley, 1898. Emily Cook, 1900. 'Mary M. Price, 'l9Ol. Hannah Atkinson, 1905. George Woodward, 1907. Louisa Trow, 1907. Andrew Jenning, 1908. Mary J. Adams, 1913. Sarah Morrell, 1914. Mr. Hewitt conducted the preliminary part of the service, and then invited the Mayor to pre- side.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>MAYOR'S SPEECH„</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR'S SPEECH„</p>
        <p>The May-or ETaiii-lii.s-cOnneetion with Buckley- street Church was rather remote, as he had only been there three 13r four times previously, but at the same time he looked upon the brethren and sisters of 'Buckley-street as the best and most loyal friends he ever had. He remembered once hearing the history of their work, and the friend who gave him that history told of the up- hill fight which resulted in the congregation get- ting that edifice in Buckley-street, then consid- ered a very handsome building. He was glad to know that although the founders had passed away, their work was being continued, and was continued with such success that to-day the Sunday school could not be properly accommo- dated. It was a very healthy sign in these days to find the Sunday school and congregation so large that they could not be accommodated in the church. (Hear, hear.) The people who fought the battle in founding the church and the warriors of old were just as important as the generals on the battlefields of to-day. They had fought the battle of uplift- ing the people of the country, and the good that had been done by that church was sufficient thanks for their efforts. He was glad to learn that the present genera.' tion had not forgotten the tremendous efforts which the old warriors put forward, and that in recognition they had put the names of the old warriors on the tablet Mrs. James Dodds would unveil, so that each succeeding generation would keep them in mind. That tablet ought to be a great inspiration to the generations yet to come.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>TABLET UNVEILED.</head>
        <p>THE TABLET UNVEILED.</p>
        <p>Mrs. J;rn-e-s- Dodds expressed the greatest pleasure at having been asked to unveil the tablet. The history of the church had been a wonderful example a Christian faith and suc- cess. It was largely due to those whose names appeared on the tablet that such success had attended the church—the men and women who had lifted up the banner and kept it flying in days far More difficult than those experienced by the present generation.. The names on the tablet would long remain an inspiration for those still to come. Mrs. Dodds then unveiled the tablet, which she duly dedicated. They would never fully realise how much they owed to the splendid men and women whose names figured on the tablet. During the evening solos were rendered by Mrs. E. A. Sanders. A vote of thanks was accorded to Mrs. Dodds and the Mayor, pro- posed by Mr. A. Taylor, seconded by Mr. J. lirmston.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY. AMATEUR PHOTOGRAI</head>
        <p>CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY. AMATEUR PHOTOGRAI</p>
        <p>War Conditions Discussed at Quar- terly Meeting.</p>
        <p>The 215th quarterly meeting of the War- rington Equitable Industrial Co-operative Society, Limited, was held in the Co-operative Hall on Monday. Mr. J. Upson presided over an attendance including the whole of the com- mittee and 150 members. The President, in moving the adoption of the report and balance sheet, made a brief state- ment regarding the outlook generally. co- -operators generally, he said, were lovers of peace and believers, in the brotherhood of man. The interests of the workers were damned by war, and in every instance the worker paid by far the greater part of the price.</p>
        <p>Business in War Time.</p>
        <p>He referred to the first few days after the declaration of war, and the panic rush that had been made, not only in that society, but generally throughout the kingdom, for stocks of provisions and for the withdrawal of invest- ments. Under the circumstances there were only two courses open to the manager to adopt. One of these was to advance the prices of goods, which, if it had been done, would have meant that the member who had money beyond his weekly earnings, would have been able to take advantage of the goods on offer, and the poorer member would have been left to pay the increased prices when these stocks had gone. The other course which was open was to limit all supplies, so that every member would have an equal opportunity to obtain his goods at the lowest possible prices. It was further pointed out that owing to the keen buying of the manager, especially regard- ing sugar and flour, the society had been able, up to now, to retail sugar at the same prices that had been in force before the war. The only thing that had had, to be done was to limit the quantity to each member to 31b. per week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>Withdrawals Normal.</head>
        <p>Withdrawals Normal.</p>
        <p>Respecting the arrangement that had been made regarding share capital it had been thought best to limit the withdrawal in cash to any one member at a time to ..t5, owing to the rush that had taken place during the first two days, and although all withdrawals asked for had been met since this action had been taken, practically speaking the !withdrawals had been normal. _ .</p>
        <p>----- The actual position was that about .t 1,700 had been paid out during this period in excess of the amount in the corresponding period last year, but the whole of this excess had really gone in the first two days following Bank Holi- day, and since then the demands had been rather less than usual. The President also pointed out that the com- mittee had decided, at any rate for the time being, to hold their hands respecting the laun- dry and dairy, and also the development of the estate at Sankey, and that they would also like the appmval of the meeting to the sugges- tion that they should go slowly forward only at Latchford with the cottage building until they were in a better position to see what the finan- cial outlook would be. _ . _</p>
        <p>The report, which was seconded and carried unanimously, showed that the sales for the half-year had been £170,277, an increase of £7,088 over the corresponding period. The membership stood at 14,082, an increase of 208 on the last three Months. The net profits. after providing for an interim dividend of 3s. 2d. in the „t, were .X 13,644. and again enabled a dividend of 3s. 21. on all purchases to be recommended, together with the usual grant to the education and. thrift funds, and a sum of £350 to reserve.</p>
        <p>The report and balance sheet were adopted practically without discussion.</p>
        <p>To Relieve Distress.</p>
        <p>In dealing with the Educational Fund ac- count, Mr. Sinclair asked the opinion of the members respecting the carrying out or other- wise of the proposed programme of concerts, &amp;c., for this winter, and said that the commit- tee thought it advisable to cancel the whole of the concerts, simply leaving a few of the lec- tures that had been arranged to be carried out, and that the balance of the educational grant ,so saved should he used for the purpose of re- lieving distress. This course was approved by the meeting and carried unanimously. In view of the new circumstance that had . arisen, the President asked the meeting to allow them to defer the question of any grant to the Parr Hall Organ Fund for the time being, and this was carried. The grant of .130 made by the committee to relieve distress in connection with the tanners' strike was also approved. The President then moved, on behalf of the committee, that a sum of ..000 be forwarded to the Mayor of Warrington for the Prince of Wales' Fund, and, in addition, that the com- mittee should be empowered to grant further sums, either to a local fund or to the central 1, fund, should the necessity arise between now and the next quarterly meeting. This was carried unanimously, it being pointed out that by the passim"; of this resolu- tion the committee would have power to deal with any distress among members which might arise locally by special local grants.</p>
        <p>Thanking the Management. The signal evidence of the approval of the members generally of the attitude that had been taken by the managenient in absolutely refusing to advance prices in any direction until corn. pelted by the advances in the wholesale /.narket to do so, was shown by the moving from the meeting that the best thanks of the society should be given to the manager and staff for the way in which the recent crisis had been met and the prices kept down, so that the poorer members had an equal chance with others of obtaining their requirements. This was carried with acclamation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>SUSPENSION OF SINKING FUND,</head>
        <p>SUSPENSION OF SINKING FUND,</p>
        <p>A Scheme to Give Relief During War Time.</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee asked the Council at the meeting on Tuesday to ask sanction from the Local Government Board to suspend the sinking fund and also' to deal with the collec- tion of rates during the war. Moving the pro- posal, Councillor WILK/NSON made an interesting statement. He said: This resolution has been passed to enable those towns where the stop- page of works owing to the war is -Cicely to cause great distress, and the sum, or part of it. which would have been paid in reduction of the debt could be applied in relief works, and the original debt would not be incl'eased thereby. In some towns in Lancashire, where the cot- ton industry is the principal trade, the dis- tress is likely to be acutely felt. The suggestion that this allocation of the whole or some portion of the sinking fund should be only by the sanction of the Local Government Board has been thought advisable, as in some towns it will not be necessary to use the whole of the year's instalment,, whilst in others, where the trade is principally with the Continent, the whole of the amount might be required. If the Local Government Board bring in a Bill to secure this concession, it is probable they will require particulars of the scheme to be submitted to them, with estimates of the cost of same, and they will then give their sanction: in other words, it cannot be expected that they will agree to the suspension of £33,000 here without asking how it is intended to spend it. The resolution re rates does not affect us.. as our improvement and sewers rates are col- lected under a Private ..Act, and the resolution only applies to those towns where the collection is made under the Public Health Acts as a yearly rate, but this will give support in such cases. The proposal was adopted.</p>
        <p>The thrifty Housewife buys Atora Refined Beef Suet. Replaces Butchers' Suet, Lard and Cooking Butter for Puddings, frying and cooking. Goes further, sweet, wholesome. Ask your grocer for ATORA and refuse substi- tuted brands.</p>
      </div>
