      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>CAUSE OF THE WAR.</head>
        <p>THE CAUSE OF THE WAR.</p>
        <p>'ll4wl Z14}31 :4 .4</p>
        <p>The Good Name of This Country. Sir Edward Grey replied, July 30th, 1914 Your telegram of 29th July. "His Majesty's Government cannot for a- moment entertain the Chancellor's proposal that they should bind themselves to neutral- ity on such terms. "What he asks us in effect is to engage to stand by while French -colonies are taken and France is beaten HO long as Germany does not take French territory as distinct from the colonies. "From the material point of view such a proposal is unacceptable, for France, with- out further territory in Europe being taken from her, could be so crushed as to lose her position as a Great Power, and become sub- ordinate to German policy. " Altogether, apart from that, it would be a disgrace for us to make this bargain with Germany at the expense of France, a dis- grace from which the good name of this country would never recover. "The Chancellor also in effect asks us to bargain away whatever obligation or interest we have as regards the neutrality of Belgium. We could not entertain that bargain either." Did we not recognise the paramount import- ance of using restrained language in diplo- matic correspondence at this critical juncture, we should be disposed to say that while Sir Edward Grey's language is weighty, it is not nearly strong enough for the occasion. We have all noticed the efforts that the Germans, through the Press and otherwise, have made to throw the blame of the conflict on to British shoulders. But no attempt has been made, no denial is forthcoming in regard to the definite proposals made to our British Ambassador by the German Secretary of State, who, remem- ber, "had just returned from Potsdam," first that behind the back of France we should consent to give a free licence to Germany to annex the French colonies, and, as Mr. Asquith put it in regard to Belgium, "that we should barter away to the power that was threatening her our obligations to keep our plighted word."</p>
        <p>Belgian Trust in Us. "I make a supreme appeal to the diplomatic intervention of your Majesty's Government to safeguard the integrity of Belgium" are words used in the message of the King of the Belgians to our own King. Our King and country did more. Diplomatic intervention failed, and—be the issue what it may—the power and the might of England is, not for the first time, enlisted on the side of the weak, whose strength and reliance has been, and is, in the plighted word of England. As in 1870, so in 1914, Germany seems deter- mined to turn this war into one of conquest. In 1870 Germany tore from France the two provinces, Alsace and Lorraine, an unnatural diversion which has ever since proved a financial burden to Germany and been an open sore be- tween the two countries. Not only so, but it has brought about an alliance, almost unnatural in itself, between the Anglo-Saxon, Slav, and Latin races. It is significant to note that this was, in part, prophesied in a remarkable letter written after Sedan by Emile 011ivier, Prime Minister of Prance, to the King of Prussia : "If you touch our territory (the letter says) you open a new Thirty Years' War. If you are, disinterested you prepare an indis- soluble alliance of the Latin and Slav races. If you behave as a conqueror you are prepar- ing an alliance of the Slav and Latin races. God punished us for allowing our long pros- perity to sap our virility: Ile will punish your people and your race swollen by victory."</p>
        <p>An Empire that bases its policy alone on force and pays no respect to morality is doomed in the long run to perish. We seem destined to be involved in a strange contradiction, that of using force to resist the power of an' arro- gant military system which has deveroped a Predatory instinct to seize the possessions of a neighbouring State, and to ignore solemn treaties and the rights of small nations. Thus to destroy the reign of force in the world we have to pass through zones 'of fire and through blood and death to arrive at a haven of peace. But to stand aside would mean the triumph of force, the victory of aggression and the triumph of wrong.</p>
        <p>Armaments and Peace. Bismarck, in a memorable speech in the'' Reichstag in 1888, speaking of France, said We will never seek occasion to quarrel. We will never attack France I am neVer for an offensive war, and if war can come only through our initiative, it will not begin. Fire must be kindled by someone before it will burn, and we will not kindle it." This Bill turned Germany into an armed camp, and Bismarck said of it : " This Bill will count for the strengthening</p>
        <p>(Continued In next column.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>FOOD AND PEOPLE.</head>
        <p>FOOD AND PEOPLE.</p>
        <p>How Population Outruns Means</p>
        <p>Of Subsistence.</p>
        <p>DR. MANSON'S ADULT SCHOOL</p>
        <p>LECTURE.</p>
        <p>Dr. J. S. Ala.nson gave a lecturette at the Academy-street Adult School last Sunday on "Food and population."</p>
        <p>GEOMETRICAL v. ARITHMETICAL PROGRESSION. The speaker pointed out that the relation of population to subsistence is the fundamental problem at the basis of all social and political philosophy. Plato, Aristotle, 31ontesquien, Hume, Wallace and Adam Smith all recognised and considered the question, but it remained for Malthus to bring it prominently before the world, and to state it with clearness and preci- sion.</p>
        <p>In passing, proceeded Dr. Manson, it is inter- esting to observe that Malthus was at one time a pupil of the Warrington Academy, and, per- haps, his is the most distinguished name associ- ated with that brilliant but short-lived school. Malthus, in his famous "Essay on the Principle of Population," laid down what is known as the Malthusian doctrine, namely, that population grows in geometrical progression-1,2, 4,8, 16, 32, 64, 128—while subsistence only increases in arithmetical progression-1, 2,3, 4,5, 6,7, 8. From this disproportion between population and subsistence he argued that all the evils and miseries of mankind arise.</p>
        <p>From history and the conditions prevailing among the nations of the earth at the time of his study he grouped two distinct classes of checks which prevented population from out- running the means of subsistence.</p>
        <p>..... ..„. First, the preventive checks drising from ab- stention from marriage, or after marriage from growth of families. Actions of this nature, based on moral restraint, he argued, were per- fectly legitimate, even laudable, but they led readily to vice and prostitution. Second, posi- tive checks, which became operative after popu- lation had outrun its means of subsistence. These positive checks were war, pestilence and famine. Critics of Malthus—and they were numerous—declared that, according to Malthus, war, pestilence and famine were beneficent states. Malthus vigorously denied this infer- ence; all he did was to point out that these evils were the natural consequences of man's folly in allowing population to exceed its food supply. Although, perhaps, Malthus stated the principle of population in relation to subsistence in too rigid and abstract a form, yet his prin- ciple is fundamentally true. He regarded the problem rather from the point of view of individual nations, but to-day we have to regard the problem from that of the whole world. In the days of Malthus famines were localised to comparatively small areas. Nowadays, with our present rapid means of com- munication and transportation, the shortage in one country can be readily met from the surplus of some other country. But this does not affect the relationship of the world's population to its whole power of producing food. Only last Wednesday in the House of Lords, Lord Milner drew the attention of the Government to the shortage in the world's supply of wheat. Since the days of Malthus the world's population has probably doubled itself, and now amounts to about 1,600 millions or 1,700 millions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>Will the Lower Races Prevail?</head>
        <p>Will the Lower Races Prevail?</p>
        <p>Can it be doubted that the present powers of medical science in preventing the spread of great pandemic diseases like plague, cholera, and smallpox, which formerly destroyed great numbers of mankind, will result in the popula- tion of the earth doubling itself again in the next 100 years, and so reaching the enormous total of somewhere between 3,000 and 4,000 mil- lions? How is that number to be fed, even with greatly increased areas of cultivation and greatly improved methods of agriculture, when the time of stress begins to tell? Will mankind adopt the first of the Malthusian checks—the preventive check—and so limit the increase of population to its proper relationship to food supply? It is evident, if this check became operative, and it is only possible to become operative among the higher civilised races—some say it is already operative—that these higher races would eventually diminish in number, and so be swamped by the lower races of mankind.</p>
        <p>If this catastrophe is to be avoided, and the higher races are to maintain and increase their numbers proportionately to the other races, then there must be a continuance of the struggle for existence, for food,and the elementary neces- sities of life, between them and those lower in the scale of civilisation. This outlook does not presage an era of universal peace, however much that end may be desired by the ideals of civilisation itself.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>PRINTER AND BIBLE.</head>
        <p>PRINTER AND BIBLE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rupert Jones gave on Sunday morning, at Sankey Bridges School, a most informing lec- ture on "The Printer and the Bible." In a very lucid account of the invention of printing in its various periods of development. and its application to the greatest of our sacred literature, Mr. Jones mentioned many of the editions of the Bible which have received their popular name through some mistake in transla- tion or printer's error. He also pointed out the great part that various• Continental countries, particularly Germany, had taken in the print- ing and translation of the Bible. At the close members joined freely in the dis- cussion, and the lecturer was invited to con- tinue the subject on a future occasion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>ADULT SCHOOLS AND THE WAR.</head>
        <p>ADULT SCHOOLS AND THE WAR.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Adult Schools are doing their share towards the defence of their country. Already a dozen members are under arms—four from the Bewsey School, four from Sankey Bridges, two from Stockton Heath, one each from Academy-street and Longford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>DANGEROUS DAWN OF WOMANHOOD.</head>
        <p>THE DANGEROUS DAWN OF WOMANHOOD.</p>
        <p>Girls upon the threshold of womanhood of ten drift into a decline in spite of all care and atten- tion. Even strong and lively girls become weak, depressed, irritable and listless. It is the dawn of womanhood—a crisis in the life of every girl —and prompt measures should be taken to keep the blood pure and rich with the red tint of health.</p>
        <p>If the blood is not in healthy condition at this critical stage, the body becomes ill-nour- ished and grave disorders may result. If the health of the body is not maintained by the flow of pure, strong blood, all sorts of evils and weak- nesses follow, and these may lead to fatal disease. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills have saved thousands of young girls from what might have been life-long invalidism or early death. They are a blood-builder of unequalled rich- ness. strengthening Weak nerves and producing the liberal supply of red, healthy blood which a girl needs to sustain her strength. Dr.' Williams' Pink Pills have proved their value over and over again to young women whose health was failing. Any dealer can supply Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People; if preferred, Dr, Williams' Medicine Co., 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, will send them direct for 2s. 9d. a box (six boxes for 13s. 9d.). A book entitled "Plain Talks to Women" will be sent free to any woman who writes to HintAa Dept., 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, for a copy; it contains much valuable information. Business as usual.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>CAUSE OF THE WAR—continued</head>
        <p>THE CAUSE OF THE WAR—continued</p>
        <p>SEPTEMBER 5, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>TYE CYURCHES AND 17)1E WAR.</head>
        <p>TYE CYURCHES AND 17)1E WAR.</p>
        <p>"THE LORD REIGNETH." 'the Rev. 'I% T. James and the Challenge to Civilisation.</p>
        <p>"DARE NOT STAND ASIDE."</p>
        <p>At Wycliffe Congregational Church on Sun- day morning, the Rev. T. T. James, who had just returned from holidays, delivered an in- spiring sermon upon the war, taking for his test the words from Psalm aciii., "The Lord reigneth." The preacher said that strange and startling events had happened since he last stood in that pulpit a few Sundays ago, events so world- shaking that they would not be surprised that he should refer to them as he returned to Wycliffe that day. The conflagration about which for many years we had talked with bated breath, the world war which had often fired the minds of our imaginative writers, had become a fact, and we were in the midst of it. No man knew where it would end. We were dealing with greater things than any of us could comprehend, and we had set in motion revolutionary forces, the results of which it was impossible to discern. We might talk about rolling up the map of Europe and making it a-fresh, but this death-grip of nations would do more than roll up maps; it would go to the very roots of our civilisation, so that we should emerge from it changed beyond all knowledge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>_ War and the Child.</head>
        <p>_ War and the Child.</p>
        <p>From the bottom of his heart he hated war, and he believed it to be an un-Christian thing. It was a cruel, devastating, heartbreaking busi- ness. He saw the troops march out of a town, and had to explain to a little child what they were going to do, and it made him sick. If they want e4I to know how far this thing was from the heart of Jesus, let them try and ex- plain it to the innocence andttrustfulness of a child. It was a tremendous task. He believed it was a disgrace to our civilisation, and that we reproached the Lord Himself and crowned Him again with the crown of thorns, because, after 2,000 years of His presence in the world, we could not settle our difficulties and differ- ences without this murderous appeal to brute force. He said in a Clrristian state of society— if Europe were truly Christian—this thing would be impossible. And yet, things being as they were, when we came to examine the attitude of our own nation towards the present struggle, it seemed impos- sible that we should take any other stand. For very long, doubtless, many of us fought in oar own minds, as well as in every way open to us, against England taking any active share in this entanglement, and even now we could not rid ourselves of the conviction of the barbarity of the whole conflict. Yet there was such a thing as war being thrust upon a people, and there was such a thing as active participation being demanded of as by every consideration of truth and honour.</p>
        <p>The peace that was won by standing on one side while smaller and weaker nations went to the wall, and people were robbed of their inde- pendence by the grim hand of merciless tyranny, was not one that any of us need covet for our country. Neither was it the peace which God Himself would have us seek. He believed this war was at last forced upon us, and that every principle of chivalry and honour compelled us to it in the end. Sad indeed it was that our main foes should belong to that Teuton nation to whom we were itulebted for so much in the realms of thought and scholarship, and whose passion for social betterment had taught us so much of modern methods in dealing with the problems of poverty in our midst. Doubtless the gaping wound that had been made in Ger- man intercourse and friendship would take long, long, to heal, but we should pray that in fairer and better times it would be healed. Days of Stress and Storm. These were days of storm and stress. We were already living in a different world from that we occupied a month ago. It puzzled us to know what would stand and what would go in this conflict of the peoples. Unrestful and anxious we all must be in the midst of such a trial. The hour was big with frightful possi- bilities. And in a time of such uncertainty, in such a period of national crisis, we should indeed lack peace of mind, and fortitude and courage, unless we turned ourselves back and discovered our anchor in the living God, unless we examined the very foundations of our faith and found them to be secure. "The Lord reigneth !" Through some such tumult the man who said these words himself had gone. He had seen thrones totter to their doom. "Thy throne is established of old; Thou art from everlasting." He had seen the enemy sweep like a flood across his land. "The Lord on high is mightier than the voice of many waters." Higher than any mere outward eye could scan, deeper than any mere outward hand could reach, he had looked up and seen that God was sovereign in His power at last, he had reached down and felt a hand which held all human things within its grasp, and cried with a faith that soared above all outward tumult: "The Lord reigneth." Our Heavy Burden. One of the effects of this national crisis should be to turn our minds to a deeper communion with, and a fuller reliance upon the living God. For we should need Him if we were to carry our burden of sorrow and suffering. That the burden would be heavy we had already dis- covered; that the sorrow would be acute was already plain to us. There was a sense, of course, in which we people of England did not understand war, did not realise either its cruelty or horror. Many a year it was since we saw the streets of our cities swim with blood, or listened to the boom of the cannon upon an English battlefield. He prayed God we might still be spared such sights as these. Yet the burden of sorrow and suffering would not be missing. He went last Tuesday into a little Welsh cot- tage, where the only lad had gone to the front. His poor old mother spoke of her love for him, and for all he (the preacher) knew, his bones. might now be bleaching under Belgian skies. There was an old man in the same Welsh vil- lage whose daughter tended the sick and wounded at the seat of war, and every day the old man went in the early morning to meet the train which brought the papers with the news. There were tears in his eyes when lie spoke about her, and his face was written over with a father's anxious love. Everywhere was that story being written, not less in our own town than in other places. We had our own rela- tives and friends, those bound to us by ties of kinship and affection, who had gone bravely to their country's need. The Pinch of Want.</p>
        <p>Heavily lay the burden on the poor of the need of bread, and many who had never known the pinch of want might know it ere the con- flict ended. Where many a year the happy tourist had made his Continental holiday, whole harvests were wasting without those to gather them, and cities which had been graced with international courtesies were the scene of blood and fire. In its devastation of human hearts, in its destruction of human homes, there had been no War like this. And many a weary exile, bereft of home and friends, and many a broken leart mourning for the lost, and many a baffled soul battling with direct need, would be here in this world of ours. Glad would it be for us, and light in the midst of darkness, if still we could lift our eyes above the storm, where striv- ing thrones grappled in the mist, to that clear and eternal throne which would never be vacated, and say with unshaken faith, "The Lord reigneth." The days were with us when the strong failed, and the young grew weary. "They that wait upon the Lord shall renew</p>
        <p>their .strength..!</p>
        <p>A True Ideal.</p>
        <p>- We turned our minds back upon God in such an hour, that they might be purged from all un- worthy thoughts. If we were to reconcile our- selves to thq thought of war at all, it could only be when we felt that we struck a blow for a righteous cause and a true ideal. If we had undertaken the championship of the weak against the strong, we were resting upon a right foundation. But very easy it was for lesser and ignoble ideas to possess us in the sternness and bitterness of strife—mere thoughts of vengeance and lnst of power, and vain glory of material things to reign supreme within us. Some con- ception they must have of how hard it was to preach, with Christian Europe turned into a cockpit. -</p>
        <p>Some idea we must Faye of the unspeakable</p>
        <p>burden laid upon our missionaries in the far corners of the earth, who had to recommend a faith which Europe had embraced. He believed it would take the lives of all the saints for the</p>
        <p>next fifty years to overcome the setback to Christianity which must result in the East from the sight of Christian nations in mortal com- bat. Yet, if it be true that there were times when we could not and dared not stand aside, then at least we could cast ourselves upon God,</p>
        <p>asking that He would purge us from all selfish- ness and cruelty and hatred of men, and pray- ing that He would help us to love nothing but righteousness and justice and truth. An empty jingoism that claimed the support of the God of battles in the chorus of a music hall was the last word in fntility and unman- liness; he 'rayed God He might baptise our people at least with seriousness and thoughtful- ness, that through this hour of danger we might get back to Him, and ask Him to take out of our hearts all that was not fit to stand. Only so should we be brave in danger, patient in re- verse, magnanimous in victory, true to the highest that we knew. Let all our forces be centred upon the triumph of right principles, and might nothing less than that satisfy us., "The Lord reigneth."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Order Out of Tumult.</head>
        <p>Order Out of Tumult.</p>
        <p>We turned ourselves back upon God to pray that out of the medley of striving forces might come the fuller establishment of His kingdom. "The Lord reigneth." The word signified not so much a state as an act. It was a process by which the kingdom was becoming. This singer had seen the wrath of man turned into the praise of God. Out of the tumult had proceeded, a firmer establishment of righteous order. So might we pray in this day of ours. Some of us were already praying that with our own eyes we might see the end of war. Who knew but that by this war God would burn war for ever out of this world of His? As, after some night of storm, the morning came fair and beautiful, so, it might be, our eyes should see a purified and sanctified world, purged for ever of the bar- barity of brute force. For, say what we would, and reconcile ourselves to it as we might, this onslaught of living men upon each other was not God's purpose for His world, nor the mes- sage of His Son to the children of humanity.</p>
        <p>- We've a song to be sung to the nations, That shall lift their hearts to the Lord, A song that shall conquer evil, And shatter the spear and sword. And the song should be in our hearts as well as on our lips at last.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. Unselfishness of the Soldiers.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. Unselfishness of the Soldiers.</p>
        <p>The Wycliffe P.S.A. meeting on Sunday was presided over by the Secretary (Mr. B. Farrar), who asked the brethren to think of, and make every possible sacrifice on behalf of those friends and brothers who had so bravely gone to defend the country and to fight its battles. They should also relieve, whenever they could, the distress caused by the war. In giving the address upon " Soldiers of Christ," Mr. Hampson referred to his own experiences of Army life, and spoke favourably of the soldier. Whilst some civilians thought only of their own selfish interests, the soldier's first thought was of his King and country. In the present war they were fighting for the cause of liberty and honour, and he hoped that, at its conclusion, their country would be bettered, and the name of Christ more honoured. The lesson was read by Mr. W. H. Payton, and Mr. William Beswiek led the prayer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>BLOODY, BRIEF, BLESSED. The Rector of Warrington's Opinions.</head>
        <p>BLOODY, BRIEF, BLESSED. The Rector of Warrington's Opinions.</p>
        <p>Under this heading the Rector (Canon P. W. Willis) writes in his Parish Magazine: "Every- body knew it would be a bloody war, because Germany had been -training for years, and was bent on conquest. It was war to the death, and with the instruments of modern warfare the carnage would be frightful. He hoped and thought that the war would be brief, although that depended upon the number of capable and efficient-soldiers we were able to put into the „field. The trade of Germany and Austria was lit a standstill, their warships were sealed up in their harbours. Under those circumstances could the war last long? Thirdly, it would be a blessed fight. War was of the devil, but war might be righteous, and he believed out of this war there would eventually come a lasting and most blessed peace.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>SUNDAY SCHOOL UNION.</head>
        <p>SUNDAY SCHOOL UNION.</p>
        <p>Rev. William Reid Asks for Co-opera- tion in Intercession.</p>
        <p>The President of the local Sunday School Union, the Rev. W. Reid. strongly urges every , Sunday school superintendent, in his capacity as , such, to co-operate with the leaders of Church ' life generally in the great work of intercession with God at this time. He suggests the following line for continuous prayer:— Confession of our national shortcomings and prayer for forgiveness. Thanksgiving for absence of panic, bountiful provision, spirit of charity, and every national good. Intercession on behalf of our King, Legislators, Army and Navy. Intercession on behalf of every victim of the war in every land. Strong plea for renewed vision of faith, the emancipation of all peoples, and the speedy close of the war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>Warrington Working Men's Association.</p>
        <p>N.A. CENTRE.</p>
        <p>Most of the N.A. anglers of Warrington last week-end were match angling at Aintree, where the Bay Horse Society held their match. Again the contest resulted in a fresh winner. This is the third contest held in this water this season, and in each event there has been a different win- ner. This proves what we said, that the mem- bers have everything to gain and nothing to lose by visiting a fresh place, as it throws the con- test more open.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>CENTRE MATCH.</head>
        <p>THE CENTRE MATCH.</p>
        <p>The Centre's match takes place on September 13th, but where we are not able to say at pres- ent. Newbridge was selected by the general meeting on Wednesday, but there seemed to be some doubt whether the contest could take place in the length mentioned. Two members were selected to go over and view the water and report to a meeting on Monday whether it would be advisable to take a match there. We hear that a roach 7oz. 12dr. has been weighed from Norton this week; also a brace of chub from Melverley. by Mr. Tom Cooper. We were sorry to hear that that fine specimen sheet of water at Ellesmere has been polluted. They say it was caused by the tarred roads close by, but up to the present we have had no confirma- , t ion .</p>
        <p>NOTE. A general meeting will take place at the Glad. stone Arms on Monday, 'September 7th, at 8 p.m.. to hear the report of the deputation sent to New. bridge and to decide the annual match. • A.S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>ANEW MIN ISTER—continued</head>
        <p>ANEW MIN ISTER—continued</p>
        <p>A NEW MINISTER.</p>
        <p>The Rev. 0. P. Pitt Welcomed by Warrington Congregationalists.</p>
        <p>A HAPPY GATHERING.</p>
        <p>Upon the commencement of his Congrega- tional ministry in Warrington, the Rev. C. P. Pitt was accorded a splendid welcome on Mon- day evening at his church in Elmwood-avenue. The Rev. T. T. James, pastor of Wycliffe, the mother church, presided over a crowded meet- ing, and there were also on the platform the Revs. C. P. Pitt, John Yonge, William Reid and A. J. Chessum, Messrs. William Brown, B. Farrar, F. Morrall and W. R. Wallington. After Mr. B. Farrar had led the meeting in prayer,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>RAPIDLY-GROWING DISTRICT.</head>
        <p>A RAPIDLY-GROWING DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said he was sure they were all glad to gather there that night to welcome Mr. Pitt. He was very glad to be there to preside over the meeting, because he felt that their Elmwood-avenue friends had worked exceed- ingly hard under discouraging conditions. How- ever, the new buildings now erected completely justified their efforts. They stood in a rapidly- growing district, where new houses were Con- stantly being built, a district not overstocked with religious edifices. It was not easy to get a minister settled in a new church; it was hard both_ for the minister and for the people. They had, however, been divinely led and divinely helped, and it had resulted in the union of Mr. Pitt and the Elmwood-avenue people. The number of Wycliffe people present that night manifested the deep interest, also, which the mother church had taken in the new mission. He trusted that all Mr. Pitt undertook would be greatly blessed. It was of peculiar interest to him that Mr. Pitt came from his (the speaker's) old college, and would therefore have been brought up in the same traditions as he had been.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pitt commenced his ministry under very sad conaditions by reason of the war, but he believed this terrible thing would strike a great blow at materialism in Europe, and would result in a large increase in church at- tendance. There were better days in front, when their labour would be rewarded. He prayed that God's blessing would rest upon the new pastor in his work at Elmwood-avenue. (Applause.) Mr. FRED MORRALL welcomed Mr. Pitt On be- half of the Primary Department. They at Elmwood-avenue had ;waited very patiently, for some time, to get a minister, and they were deeply indebted to all those who had occupied the pulpit during that period. They were glad to have Mr. Pitt amongst them, be- cause they felt he was the right man in the right place. But what did he think of his people? That was only a fair question to ask. At any rate, he felt they would have Mr. Pitt's support in the work they would have to do with him. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>REV. JOHN YONGE'S REMINISCENCES. The Rev. Joel.; Yore*, who was received with loud applause, said that when they opened that building some time ago they were good enough to ask him to occupy the pulpit first, and they had a glorious time. He supposed that by now the "Old Cockhedgers"—if he might so call them—would be beginning to feel at home in that handsome building. Doubt- less, when they first came to it, they hardly knew themselves. The very name, contrasted with the old one, was an inspiration. (Laugh- ter.) He was very glad that a brother—a I young man with undaunted vigour—had taken up the ministry. He came to them with all the I credentials of an excellent college career. It was perhaps not unnatural on this occa- sion for him to carry them back over 52 years, to a clammy November day, when he first com- menced his ministry in the Isle of Wight, in a Much smaller church than Elmwood-avenue. True, it had four walls and a roof, a few pews, and even upright gas brackets—which he al- ways thought were out of keeping with the rest of the building. (Laughter.) The pulpit was heated by means of a tin bath filled with hot water, which was placed beneath his feet when he was preaching. He also wore a good thick overcoat, and so kept fairly warm. (Laugh- ter.) With their help, Mr. Pitt's work would be successful. He himself would always be reedy to help Mr. Pitt whenever he could. If he came to him at any time, as a young man to an old one, for advice, he would give it willingly. (Loud applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>WYCLIFFE WELCOME.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE WELCOME.</p>
        <p>On behalf of the people of Wycliffe, Mr. WILLIAM BROWN, who acts as the church trea- surer, welcomed the new minister, the Chair- man introducing him as a treasurer who "always had a smiling face and a balance on the right side." Referring to the matter of finance, Mr. Brown said they had all made sac- rifices to get that splendid building, but these they 'did not mind, as anything worth having was worth paying for. (Hear, hear, and ap- plause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>"A HUMBLE PRESBYTERIAN"</head>
        <p>"A HUMBLE PRESBYTERIAN"</p>
        <p>..—...--- ...___.._ ..... The Rev. WILLIAM REID Came, "as a humble Presbyterian," to express the most cordial welcome to their new minister, on behalf of his denomination. He felt glad that the strength of the Warrington Free Church min- istry was to be increased, and if it was ever in his power to help Mr. Pitt. he would only be too glad to do so. He welcomed him also on behalf of the ministers of Warrington, to whom he "sometimes acted as some sort of clerk." He would say one word to Mr. Pitt's people to emphasise the peculiar nature of the rela- tions between a minister and his people. They were unlike any other relations. In business, one could fight in a way, but when difficulties arose that came between a minister and his people, they could never fight successfully. Men had done it, and congregations had done it, and both had been injured. There must be complete confidence and sympathy between the two; a minister should never go about con- scious that ,he missed their sympathy and prayers. , There was always a strain upon his memory, his nerves, and his heart, when lie came before them every week. They would never be just what they ought to be, but let them be pre- pared beforehand, not to be, at any rate, un- duly critical, and not to rest entirely upon what he said. Thus they would enable him to do his work in the best possible manner. They had made a splendid start, and he hoped their endurance would be as excellent as their initia- tive. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>METHODIST CONGRATULATIONS.</head>
        <p>METHODIST CONGRATULATIONS.</p>
        <p>In extending to Mr. Pitt the hearty welcome of the Primitive Methodists of the town, the Rev. A. J. CHESSrM said he wanted to congratu- late them on the beauty of their new building, and also on its fine position. They had been wise to "come out." He welcomed Mr. Pitt as a brother. Although he had himself only been here a short time, he had found Lanca- shire people most kind and generous. (Ap- plause.) At this point there was an interval, during which the audience were served with refresh- ments. Upon the resumption of the speeches, Mr. W. H. WALLINGTON offered a word of wel- come to Mr. Pitt, as one of those who had helped to "keep the old place going" in the absence of any minister. They had worked in the old place for many years in wet and dry— mainly wet—so that they could now appreciate at least some of the advantages they had especially a leader. He would, at the same time, like to pay a well-deserved tribute to those who had worked so hard in the past, whom, he knew, would continue to do so. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>MR. piTT'S REPLY. The Rev. C. P. PITT, who was accorded a most enthusiastic reception, said that he would like, first of all, to answer the question asked dur- ing the evening as to how lie liked his people. From what he had seen of them, he liked them very much (Laughter.) He would like to thank the friends who had referred to him so kindly. He thanked them for their kind . offers of assistance, of which he would avail himself. With both Mr. Reid and Mr. Ches- .. sum he could claim some relationship—with Mr. Reid because the last church he preached in before commencing his Warrington ministry was a Presbyterian one. (Laughter.) Again, not far from their college was another, belong-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <p>(Continued in preceding column.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>ARMY SECRETS.</head>
        <p>ARMY SECRETS.</p>
        <p>Singular Charge at Warrington Against an Ex-Soldier.</p>
        <p>PORTSMOUTH DEFENCES.</p>
        <p>A singular story which centred round the de- fences of Portsmouth, the great naval and mili- tary depot in the South of England, was narrated in the Warrington Police Court on Tuesday to the magistrates, Councillor H. Roberts (in the chair), and Messrs. J. Hepherd, C. Jackson Holmes and T. locker. Harold Dutton. who was described as a bar- man, employed at Widnes and later at Warring- ton, and whose last address, which was not dis- closed, was said to have been in Widnes, was charged with detaining a document or docu- ments, the property of His Majesty's Army, and thus committing a misdemeanour under the Military Secrets Act of 1911. He was described as an ex-soldier, and stood in a military attitude all through the hearing of the case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>PROSECUTION'S STORY.</head>
        <p>PROSECUTION'S STORY.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. R. Ley, of the Public Prosecutor's Office, London, prosecuted. He said while accused was in the Army Service Corps he was stationed at Portsmouth; he was engaged in the office and acted as a clerk to Major Hare, who was the general staff officer then at Portsmouth; was engaged on the defences of that part, and that officer prepared a lecture on the defences, which Dutton had to type. It was an absolutely secret document, and knowing that, prisoner had made an extra copy, which he had retained. The contents of that lecture were such as should have been subjected to the greatest possible secrecy.</p>
        <p>Dutton left the Army Service Corps and joined a Lancer Regiment at the Curragh, and from which he was discharged as medically unfit. At Warrington he got a job as a barman, and while in his lodgings he produced the document and showed it to a fellow lodger named Barker, who said he did not think it was of any good to any- body.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>SUBSTITUTED BY BLANK PAPER.</head>
        <p>SUBSTITUTED BY BLANK PAPER.</p>
        <p>The next day Barker woke up to the serious- ness of the document, took it out of Dutton's pocket and substituted blank paper in the en- velope. Barker then reported the matter to the police, who viewed it so seriously that Supt. Williamson and Detective-Sergeant Holland went to Dutton's lodgings.</p>
        <p>They told prisoner„ that they had come to inquire into some military documents he pos- sessed, but Dutton denied that' he had any ereept his discharge and small book. A searct produced nothing, but prior to that Barker had taken the document to the police. While the police were waiting Dutton went to a girl, who was in the house, handed her an envelope and said: "For God's sake, Cissy, keep this for me."</p>
        <p>AN ALLEGED ADMISSION. At the Police Station Dutton admitted that he had had the document relating to the Ports- mouth defences in his possession, and said he had acted as a clerk to Major Hare, and when typing the secret lecture had taken an extra copy, which he had kept. Major Hare was now on active service, and they could not call him. Prisoner was at Ports- mouth from 1912 until early in the present 'tear. The serious nature of his act could not be described, and one had only to look once at the document to see that it would have been of the highest value to an enemy. Colonel Moggridge, who is at present in com- mand at Warrington, said that Dutton. when in the Army Service Corps, had absolutely no right to make a copy of any document for his own use. The information the document coatained would be of the greatest value to an enemy. John Barker told how he extracted the docu- ment from the envelope in accused's jacket pocket, and substituted blank paper. He took the document, which was marked "Secret," to the recruiting station and they sent him to the police. PRISONER'S EXPLANATION. Supt. Williamson described how he had acted after seeing the document, as described by Mr. Lev. At the Police Station accused said: Well, it is no use denying it now. I did have them, and I gave the envelope and the papers to my young lady when I went for my coat. I was afraid when you asked me about the papers, and that is why I denied having them."</p>
        <p>_ Prisoner was then cautioned, and he ex- plained "I was clerk to Major Hare when I was in the Army Service Corps. He was on the Portsmouth defences, and I used to type out his notes of lectures. When copying that lot I took an extra copy for myself, as I was studying for promotion. I knew I had no right to have it.</p>
        <p>took it with me when I left -the Army Ser- vice Corps. I made no secret of it, and used to show it to the chaps in my regiment when at the Curragh, just to swank about the important work I had been on. 1 never meant to do any- thing wrong with the papers. I showed them to my brother at home, and he told me I ought to burn them. I only took them away with me the last time I was home, and I showed them to Barker in the lodgings. Later prisoner was shown the paper and he said they were what he had had in his posses- sion, and added that he had no more. Prisoner expressed the hope that the case would not be serious, as he never meant to do anything wrong with the, papers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>YOUNG LADY'S STORY.</head>
        <p>YOUNG LADY'S STORY.</p>
        <p>Elizabeth Boardman, a young woman living in Widnes, said on the 12th of August she came to Warrington to see accused, and when at tea the police came. He gave her an envelope to keep for him. When she looked inside she found only blank pieces of paper. A sergeant in the Army Service Corps, whose name was not mentioned, said he was employed at Portsmouth while prisoner was a clerk at the headquarters office. It was possible that Dutton typed lecture notes for Colonel Hare, and it was possible for him to keep copies. Prisoner said he had no intention of doing harm. He had had the documents for 18 months and had shown them to some young fellows in the regiment, and also to a brother, and to Barker. He had no more to say.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>BARKER CONGRATULATED.</head>
        <p>BARKER CONGRATULATED.</p>
        <p>Dutton was sent to the next Assizes at Liver- pool for trial. Councillor Roberts, the chairman, addressing Barker, said : On behalf of my colleagues and myself I ivoish to say we cannot offer our thanks to you as the case has not terminated, but we think you have behaved extremely well and have shown more than ordinary sense.</p>
        <p>The woman who believes everything her hus- band tells her hasn't been married very long.</p>
        <p>;HOLLOWAY'S I:plas OINTMENT</p>
