      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>UNCONQUERABLE.</head>
        <p>UNCONQUERABLE.</p>
        <p>The PRIME MINISTER sounded the right note last night in London, and his words were ably seconded by the LEADER Of the OPPOSITION, Mr. WINSTON CHURCHILL, and Mr. BALFOUR. Mr. ASQUITH'S speech was the political complement of the military pronounce- ment of the soldier, LORD KITCHENER, a week ago. Then we were in sad doubt as to the well-being of our Army in France, and the SECRETARY for WAR, out of the fulness of his accu7rate knowledge, assured us that our men had behaved gloriously, that they had hammered the enemy in a way that would have a lasting effect, and that all was right if we kept our eye stead- ily on the •end in view. After several days of further doubt and German advance, the PREMIER, without giving away any information that might be of use to the enemy, assures us that in regard to the general situation at the seat of war, in his judgment, in what- -ever direction we looked, there was abundant ground for pride and com- fort. There could not be much room for comfort if the advance of the Ger- mans really meant disaster, however much pride we took in the stubborn resistance our men had offered ; so we must take it that Mr. ASQUITH wishes to imply that the military situation, as he knows it, is satisfactory. It is the vindication of our cause, however, that marks the value of the PREMIER'S pronouncement. He told his London audience, and they cheered 'him to the echo, that the Government and the people of this country would have been wanting in their duty had , they in defiance of their plighted and solemn obligations not done their best to prevent and to avenge the intolerable outrages of these buccaneering adven- turers in a political and a military sense. The sacking of Louvain, with its splendid buildings and pictures, its unique library, and unrivalleg asso- ciations, he described as a shamelew holocaust of irreplaceable treasures lit up by blind barbarian vengeance. Mr. ASQUITH said what we all feel—that we might as well be wiped out now by force as to have been wiped out for , moral cowards. "Sooner," he said, " than be a silent witness which meant,l in fact, a silent accomplice of this tragic triumph of force over law and freedom, he would rather see this , country of ours blotted out of the pagel of history." This was only half the ' message he sent to us, our Allies—and our enemies. Nearly 300,000 men have already answered LORD KITCHENER'S call, and the Empire is sending suffi- cient -men to ensure that we shall, in the end, prevail. He quoted the words of Pitt on another grave occasion in our history----" England has saved her- self by her, exertions, and will save Europe by her example." Our armies are intact, our conscience is clear, our Spirit is unbreakable; and in such cases the burning of defenceless cities, with all its attendant misery, will go a very short way indeed towards making amends for the serious manner in which the German time-table has &gt;'.one astray.</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>-.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 5, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 5, 1914.</p>
        <p>Recruiting at the Police Station. Numerous iecruits have been enlisted at the Police Station this week, the Chief Constable acting as recruiting officer.</p>
        <p>Food Prices Still Norr alnal• .0 Dor' Food prices in Warrington kill rein_oo mal. Bacon is still high, but there st': tendency towards a reduction.</p>
        <p>Warringtonian Injured in Satt!%, P.C. Skinner, of the Warrington Polic," was called up to join the Coldstream ysth of wounded in an engagement an A ugust. He is now in a London hosPl</p>
