      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>PASSING WEEK,</head>
        <p>PASSING WEEK,</p>
        <p>TERRITORIALS IN FINE FORM.</p>
        <p>Yesterday we received the following gratify- ing 'account of the way in which our local " Terriers " are enjoying themselves at their temporary home in the North : "The town here is on a hill, situated in pleas- ant country. All have found comfortable quar- ters, despite the fact that there are several thousand other Territorials needing accommo- dation. A bad cold went round our Battalidn before it got here, but they are now quit of it, and look very fit.</p>
        <p>"They work hard, starting each day with an hour's physical drill in the park, from 7 till 8. Then may come company drill for three hours, and a route march in the afternoon, :varied by night marching, staff rides, and, of course, bath parades on regular days, and church parade on Sunday. Indeed, when we arrived for the day last Sunday week, we found the Warrington Territorials filing into a cinema palace for their service, so crowded was the old town!</p>
        <p>"The officers are in great spirits at the splen- did news that their chaplain and others have secured the requisite 300 men to bring their unit up to full strength for foreign service. They all wish most strongly that Mr. Brace- camp could be with them. _</p>
        <p>"The tramp of companies goes on all day down the main street, and the High4and Light Infantry pipers sound fine with their skirling music, as they swing round a corner."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>THEIR EQUIPMENT.</head>
        <p>THEIR EQUIPMENT.</p>
        <p>When the Territorials were embodied, £2,000 was collected in a few hours to complete their equipment, and put the men on a war footing. Since then an appeal has been made for £3OO to enable the friends of the Battalion to provide the men with such comforts as sleeping hel- mets and thick socks for the winter. So far— in, say, a week—£23B 15s. Gd. of that sum has been given, the amounts received having in- cluded £lOO from the Hon. Colonel of the Bat- talion (Colonel J. Reid Walker), £lO 10s. from Messrs. Thos. Whittle and Sons, Ltd., £5 from Councillor 11. Roberts, and £3 from the Misses Wilson-Patten. Seeing that 350 extra men have been enrolled during the week for foreign service, more funds will be required than the committee originally expected. We feel sure , the appeal for the little extra help has only to be made to be met.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>GUARANTEED.</head>
        <p>GUARANTEED.</p>
        <p>We have undertaken as a community to see that the wives and other dependents of our Territorials who may be killed or injured in the war shall not want. That is, if the nation does not make proper provision—which is an inconceivable proposition—the town will; so that the men can go to their duty in the double assurance that those they may leave behind will be cared for.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>NO POLITICS.</head>
        <p>NO POLITICS.</p>
        <p>Lord Kitchener,- wise man that he is, has asked the political parties if they would jointly agree that their organisations should co-operate in the work of securing the men needed to fill up the ranks of the great army he is raising. That does not mean that isolated action is to be taken by any political party which would at once give the idea that it was sought to make political eapital out of a season of national storm and stress. Last night the Prime Minis- ter and the Leader of the Opposition, standing on the same platform in London, opened a cam- paign which is destined to spread itself through the Empire, and make all sides one in such a way as has never before been known. It is in accordance with this scheme that the Mayor has decided to call a town's 'meeting to decide upon a line of action, the suggestion being that we should raise a Civilian Army and a complete Battalion for Kitchener's Army. The meeting was to have been held to-night, but as the prin- cipal speaker, Lord Derby, is unable to be pres- ent owing to illness, it has been postponed. We were informed yesterday that the Conserva- tive Club Corps, which had already been formed, would fall in with the arrangements as made at the Maybes meeting, when that meet- ing takes place.</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR'S FAITH IN WARRINGTON. On Thursday the Mayor wired to Lord Kitchener :—" I believe that I can readily raise a Battalion of one thousand men to serve in Lord Kitchener's Army. I have asked for, but cannot obtain, any advice from the Military Authorities here, for the reason given that they are without instructions. Is it desired that I Should proceed to enrol the men, and if so, I Shall be glad to receive instructions as to how I must proceed?—Peter Peacock, Mayor of War- rington."</p>
        <p>The War Office replied : "Mayor, Warrington. Army Council offers sincere thanks for your patriotic offer, which will be submitted to the Secretary of State._ Secretary; War Office." The Mayor's optimism seems well founded, for recruits have found the officials more work than they can do, and the pressure is still main- tained. Some of the works have lost hundreds of men, and ,hundreds of others are ready.</p>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL BUSINESS "AS USUAL." Wars may come and wars may go, but "scenes" at the Warrington Town Council go on for over. Business was "as usual" on Tuesday evening, when several violent scenes took place—not very creditable at this juncture. We ought to be at peace, if we possibly can be, internally. With this end in vle,w the Mayor asked that the political parties in town and other organisa- tions would consider the advisability of avoid.. mg Municipal ConteSts at the coming November elections.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>INTERCESSION. •</head>
        <p>INTERCESSION. •</p>
        <p>" BEFORE HONOUR IS HUMILITY."</p>
        <p>(Proverbs xv. 33.) Lord God of Hosts! Almighty God, Whom Thou loveth Thou doth chasten, 'Neath the smart of Thy chast'ning rod We plead that Thou would'st hasten To hear Thy people whilst they pray On bended knee before Thy throne; Spare, Lord, the rod! Help us to-day To seek Thine aid and Thine alone.</p>
        <p>Lord God of Hosts! Great Judge of all, Within the hollow of Thine hand Thou holdeth nations—their rise and fall— The fate of man in every land. Our cause is just—in mercy hear An Empire's supplicatory prayer— Save, Lord, our men, men without fear, In Jesu's name to ask we dare.</p>
        <p>Lord God of Hosts? "The great I AM," Be Thou their stay in battles' blast, Stretch forth Thine arms and let their span Protect them as in ages past. And may the shadow of Thy wing Be over all-L-on land or sea— That ev'ry man a song might sing, A song of faith and trust in Thee. Lord God of Hosts! Omniscient King. Thou knowest we for peace have striven, 'Teas not to be, for man would bring With anger filied and malice riven A hardened heart. With stiff-necked pride His lust for power he would not cease. We pray Thee, Lord, such will o'er-ride, Be Thine the Will—and send us peace.</p>
        <p>Lord God of Hosts! Thy Spirit graft With blessings to our King and Queen, Let Thy minist'ring.angels waft</p>
        <p>The Word which in the past hath been Their guide, our strength, in sorrow's grief, The source of Greater Britain's might: In Thee we trust, in full belief, That Thou wilt keep, and guard the right. R. OSSULSTON-RIGHE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>WAR AND FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>WAR AND FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Warrington Committee Recommend Suspension of Season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>DECISION NEXT TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>DECISION NEXT TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>It is, as yet, doubtful lvhether the Northern Union fixtures for the present season will be carried out. Matches arranged for to-day will be played, but a general meeting has been called for Tues- day in Manchester, at which it will be decided what steps shall be adopted in view of the pres- ent state of affairs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>WARRINGTON F.C. TAKE ACTION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON F.C. TAKE ACTION.</p>
        <p>Last Saturday a meeting of the Warrington F.C. Committee was held, and Mr. T. P. Brad- shaw, the hon. secretary, was requested to write to Mr. Platt, secretary of the Northern Union (who, in the absence of Mr. Houghton, is also acting secretary of the League), to recommend a " suspension of the football season, as my com- mittee were of opinion that sport should be discontinued. "Very strong protests have been made by our members, asking the committee to take this course, and a very powerful local appeal, backed by influential residents, has been issued, asking people not to take part in or support football for the time being. This appeal is receiving considerable support, and is growing daily. . "We therefore request that a meeting of the Northern Union Committee should be called to consider the suggestion. We will, of course, abide by the decision, but the general opinion here is that, for the moment, football is a minor consideration, and that the players and football enthusiasts, or at any rate a inajority of them, could be better em-</p>
        <p>ployed by'resionding to the nation's call." That letter was sent on Sunday, and on Tues- day, September 1, Mr. Platt replied :</p>
        <p>_ "1 am in receipt of your favour of the 30th ult. Will place the matter on the agenda of the Union meeting for next Tuesday." Mr. Bradshaw, after consultation with the Chairman and Treasurer, telegraphed "Committee think matter serious, and should be decided before season commences, and request you call your committee together immediately to consider our letter, as we will not take responsibility playing on Saturday without definite instructions." Mr. Platt wrote September 2nd : "I am in receipt of your telegram of to-day's date. At a meeting Of the League Manage- ment Committee it was decided to call a general meeting of club representatives for Tuesday next, at Manchester, to consider the question of the war and its effect upon foot- ball. In the meantime they expect football fixtures to be kept. I cannot call a meeting of the general committee of the Union for the present week, but the matter is on the agenda for their meeting also on Tuesday next."</p>
        <p>These meetings will accordingly be held in Manchester on Tuesday afternoon to consider the question. The Warrington Committee, it will be seen, endeavoured to get the point settled before the season began, but were unsuccessful. They in- tend to fulfil to-day's engagements, as per in- structions, and await the result of the meeting of the authorities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>PLAYERS AND COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>PLAYERS AND COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>We understand that the players have ex- pressed their willingness to meet the committee in any way they can, and have intimated that, if play is decided upon, part of their pay shall be devoted to the war fund.</p>
        <p>BARROW CLUB STOPPED. The military authorities at Barrow have stopped the Barrow Club playing football at Cavendish Park or away, and though every effort was made to get the ban removed the authorities were obdurate, and for the present, at any rate, the Barrow Club fixtures are aban- doned. Barrow ought to have played Hull Kingston Rovers at Cavendish Park to-day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>A welcome return visit is paid to the Royal Court Theatre this week by Mr. J. Newton Cowling's company, in "The Collier's Lass." The play presents a special attraction to local people, since it includes some typical scenes of homely Lancashire life. Whilst of a pathetic character generally, the play contains an appre- ciable amount of comedy. The title-role is feelingly portrayed by Miss Violet Somerville, and Mr. Stanley Radcliffe makes a vigorous Tom Fielding, her lover.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>" Australian Nell's" Visit,</head>
        <p>" Australian Nell's" Visit,</p>
        <p>- - "Australian Nell," another of Mrs. F. G. Kimberley's cleverly written dramas, occupies the stage at the Royal Court Theatre next week and is sure to attract large audiences. The story opens in Australia, where Arthur Car- rington, a scion of the British nobility, marries Nell, a girl of the Australian backwoods. For a time they are happy, but the arrival of Laura Carrington, a former sweetheart of Arthur's, creates doubts, and eventually. Laura, with the aid of Spanish Jake, succeeds in parting hus- band, and wife, the former returning to Eng- land. After a lapse of years, he again returns to Australia, and exidanations remove all doubts. Miss Leah Corenters, who interprets the title-role, is an actress of exceptional ability. Mr. Alfred D. Adams enacts the role of Spanish Jake; Arthur Carrington is played by Mr. Sid- !ley Monckton; and the intrigues of Laura are interpreted by Miss Gladys Morris., Mr. Nor- ton Shields is Jack Richardson, Miss Ethel Masterson the Kiddie, and Miss Evelyn Sey- mour is Phcebe. Some capital comedy inter- ludes are introduced by Mr. Fred Wright as Sambo and Miss Louie ireathfield as Arabella.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>lIIPPODRONIE.</head>
        <p>THE lIIPPODRONIE.</p>
        <p>The excellent programme presented this week at the Hippodrome includes a screaming absurd- ity, "The New Detective," which shows Sam Snapper (Mr. Arthur White) as a "cool" detec- tive, and, his excitable clients (Clairette Ruane and J. M. Harper) to advantage. Gaby Revette's exhibition of tableaux representing famous pictures is of wonderful finish. Emer- son and Baldwin are mirth-provoking jugglers hailing from America, who perform their tricks with great dexterity. The Stowe Brothers' equilibristic performance is a popular turn, whilst Barry Mills and Kitty Mack entertain with splendid patter. Clinton and Rice, dancing comedians, are also much appreciated.</p>
        <p>Strong Brogramme Next Week. Fred Karno's company in the absurdity "Teeth" tops the bill. It is a travesty on a dentist business and provokes plenty of mirth. A superb musical treat is promised by the talented Child Trio. Other turns will include: Dave Bryant, mimic; La Freya, in her charm. jug poses; and Tom Gott, a Yorkshire dialect comedian.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>STAR KINEMA.</head>
        <p>STAR KINEMA.</p>
        <p>The chief pictures in next week's Star Kinema House programmes are: "King in name alone" (first three days) and "Beauty and the barge" (last three days).</p>
        <p>" LOEWEN." [The same word, "Loewen," in the German language, stands for "Lions" and for the name of the city of Louvain.]</p>
        <p>_ LOEWEN: THE LIONS LOEWEN: THE LOU. OP THE SEA. VAIN OF THE GER- "It must be admitted MANS. that the British without " The only means of attempting to consider preventing surprise at- their own danger, sent tacks from the civil out lifeboats in order to population has been to save our men."—The interfere with unrelent- Kaiser's War News of ing severity, and to the Battle of Heligoland create examples which by Bight, by Wireless. their frightfulness would be a warning to the whole" country. . . • Louvain has been bit- terly punished." The Kaiser's War News, by .; Wireless.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>War and Sale of Work.</head>
        <p>War and Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>_ At a recent special meeting of the Council of the Parish Church it was unanimously agreed to abandon the parochial sale this year on account of the war.</p>
        <p>The Church Bell's Meaning. The St. James' Church bell rings for a few moments every day at noon, and townspeople are asked to offer a prayer on behalf of the soldiers and sailors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>More Transports Pass.</head>
        <p>More Transports Pass.</p>
        <p>Another stream of motor transports hurried through Warrington during last week-end. Most had been commandeered from Scottish firms and were on their way South.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Cigarettes in Socks.</head>
        <p>Cigarettes in Socks.</p>
        <p>Warrington la-dies have knitted a considerable number of pairs of socks for the use of the sol- diers at the front, and not a few have placed packets of cigarettes in each pair. Tommy will thus receive a doubly welcome gift.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Rev. E. C. E. Carleton at Home.</head>
        <p>The Rev. E. C. E. Carleton at Home.</p>
        <p>The Rev. E. C. E. Carleton and Mrs. Carleton arrived safely back in Warrington this week, after their prolonged sojourn in Switzerland. They got through without any untoward inci- dent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Literature for the Wounded.</head>
        <p>Literature for the Wounded.</p>
        <p>Mrs. H. G. Lawson-Johnston (la, Queen's Gate, London, S.W.) desires to thank the many readers who have responded so generously to her appeal for magazines and other literature for the wounded.</p>
        <p>A Bit of Realism.</p>
        <p>Wax figures of Red Cross nurses standing beside hospital beds form very realistic dis- plays in the windows of some Warrington shops. These are used to boom the sale of garments and materials for the use of soldiers at the front.</p>
        <p>Keep Working.</p>
        <p>"The war, of course, absorbs all our interests," says the St. Tames' Parish Maga- zine, "yet it is a plain duty to perform our daily tasks as cheerfully and promptly as ever; old engagements must be kept, and, as far as possible, there must be no dislocation of busi- ness or routine."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>" Specials " Sworn In.</head>
        <p>" Specials " Sworn In.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Tuesday four special constables were sworn in for duty in the rural district around Warring- ton. The Chairman (Mr. F. A. Frost), in thank- ing the men for their services, hoped they would not be called upon for any serious task.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>"Itch, the Terriers, Too."</head>
        <p>"Itch, the Terriers, Too."</p>
        <p>One of the German prisoners at the Work- house read that the British bulldogs .(the sol- diers) had now got to work. "Ach, and the Terriers are going out, too," he said. Evidently the worried German did not know that the word "Terrier" did not mean a dog, but ap- plied to our gallant Territorials.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Reservist in Trouble.</head>
        <p>A Reservist in Trouble.</p>
        <p>At the, local Policed Court on Saturday Ed- ward O'Donnell, 74, Stanley-street, St. Helens, was charged with being drunk and disorderly on Central Station. The prisoner said he was going to join the National Reserve at Liverpool, and, owing to a soldier friend, he missed twa trains. The prisoner was dismissed and pro- mised to go immediately to Crosby, Liverpool, where the headquarters were situated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Grey Horses Galore.</head>
        <p>Grey Horses Galore.</p>
        <p>The commandeering of horses for warfare has resulted in a large number of white and grey horses, not .suitable for modern fighting, mak- ing their appearance in Warrington. More light-coloured horses are now said to be seen on the streets of the town than ever before. Merchants are also said to have bought up as many cab horses as possible, and owners were said not to be sorry to sell, as the taxi cabs have driven horse cabs out of fashion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>First Timber Ship Arrives.</head>
        <p>First Timber Ship Arrives.</p>
        <p>The first timber ship to arrive in the Man- chester Ship Canal since the declaration of the war was berthed at Walton lay-by on Tuesday with a cargo for Messrs. R. A. Naylor, Ltd. It is the Ulfos, from Christiania, and the captain reported that he did not encounter any German warships in the North Sea, but was stopped by a British man-of-war, the captain of which, after inspecting the ship's papers, allowed the Ulfos to proceed. No timber is yet available from the Baltic. Messrs. Naylor informed "The Examiner" representative that extra in- surance had been paid on the cargo to cover the war risk. Other vessels were on the way, and it was thus hoped to keep, their staff employed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>" Corporation Soldiers."</head>
        <p>" Corporation Soldiers."</p>
        <p>In the minutes of the General Purposes Com- mittee, which were considered at the meeting of the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday evening, a resolution stated that any officer or servant of the Corporation affected by the mobilisation would be allowed leave of absence during the period of his service, and that he would be reinstated on his return without loss of position or anything else. Certain fears were quieted when the Town Clerk explained that no mention had been made of Lord Kitchener's Army when the resolution was passed, but he wished it to be understood that all officers and servants of the Corporation joining that Army would be included in the resolution.—The min- utes were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Coping With Distress in District.</head>
        <p>Coping With Distress in District.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Warrington Rural District Council the Chairman (Coun- cillor William Furness) gave his report of a meeting at Earlestown dealing with relief dis- tribution, already published. He added that a further meeting had been held, when it was decided that the Council should be a sub-com- mittee with power to add to its numbers until a total of twenty was reached. He suggested that the sub-committee should have a broad ' basis and include representatives of manufac- turing conceras.—The Rev. A. M. Mitchell asked if the world of labour could not have representation, and was informed that anybody could be recommended. There was a little scene between the Rev. A. M. Mitchell and Councillor Knowles, which the Chairman char- acterised as being "six of one and half-a-dozen of another." It was ultimately decided that for the present the sub-committee only consist of the members of the Council, the Rev. A. M. Mitchell voting against.</p>
        <p>Three Sons in the Army. A Warrington mother, all of whose sons, three in number, are serving in the Army, is Mrs. Ellen Ridyard, of 17, Barnard-street, San- key Bridges. David, the eldest of the three, is 34 years of age, and was employed at the Museum. Ile is married and has three chil- dren, the family living with his mother. He has belonged to the II Company of the Terri- torials for some years, having seen active ser- vice in South Africa. His last letter home in- formed his relatives that lie was in the North, but was expecting to leave any day. John. aged 27, who served his two years in the Ai•my Service Corps about six years ago, has been called up as a reserve. He is a married man, and lived at 21, Marsh-street, being employed as a labourer at Messrs. Rylands Bros.' He first moved, with his regiment, to Bradford, and thence to Aldershot. It is expected that by now he will be in France. The youngest son is Arthur, aged 21, who is employed in the L. and N.-W. Railway engine sheds. He only enlisted three weeks ago, joining the same company as that to which his brother David belongs, since when he has moved with the battalion. Three of the four sons of Mr. and Mrs. Gibbs, 26, Wilderspool-road, Warrington, belong to the Army. The eldest is Thomas Knox, aged 28, an employe of the Manchester Ship Canal Company, who is a member of the Royal Field Artillery. When he last wrote home they were at Aldershot, but expected to move immediately. William, aged 26, an employe of Messrs. Rylands i Bros., has joined the Territorials, and s at pres- ent training at Warrington. He expects to leave i the town shortly. Denis, the youngest, who s also employed at Mesirs. Rylands Bros., has joined the King's Scottish Borderers, with whom he is now at Portland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>RED CROSS</head>
        <p>RED CROSS</p>
