      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>– HELPING THE DEPENDENTS. Numerous Warrington Cases Speedily</head>
        <p>- HELPING THE DEPENDENTS. Numerous Warrington Cases Speedily</p>
        <p>Relieved.</p>
        <p>The work of relief to wives and dependents of sailors, soldiers and reservists and Territorials has been carried on throughout the past week. Mrs. Venables, the honorary secretary of the Soldiers' and Sailors' Families' Association, and a band of willing workers, have been engaged daily at the Parr Hall relieving any necessitous cases. Up to yesterday about 700 cases have been dealt with, and those who are found to be still within the rules of the society will con- tinue to be assisted.</p>
        <p>Attempts have been made to take advantage of the society by unscrupulous people, but careful investigation is being made, and it is as well that it should be made known that any attempt to obtain money by false statements from any source whatever will be severely dealt with.</p>
        <p>It must be distinctly understood that if father or son is on active service this is not of itself an absolute claim on the society for assistance. The society is helping deserving cases, but there have been some cases where no attempt has been made to make proper use of the re- sources which are within the reach of the appli- cant, and it cannot be expected that any chari- table organisation can give assistance which would only be misused in such cases. The office hours of the Soldiers' and Sailors' Families' Association are from 10 to 1, and 2.30 to 4.30 daily.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>FOR TERRITORIALS' FAMILIES.</head>
        <p>FOR TERRITORIALS' FAMILIES.</p>
        <p>- • The relief relief of the wives and dependents of Territorials has been carried out by the Bor- ough Treasurer at Bank House, but on and after Monday net this work will be trans- ferred to the Parr Hall. The office hours for Territorial cases will be from 10 to 12 and 2 to 4.30 p.m. daily. Another work which is being carried out at the Parr Hall is that of general registration of all cases of charity of whatever sort, and to all those who are in touch with these information will be given which will prevent overlapping.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>TIIE NEW POPE, Cardinal della Chiesa Elected.</head>
        <p>TIIE NEW POPE, Cardinal della Chiesa Elected.</p>
        <p>Cardinal della Chiesa was on Thursday elected Pope and will be known as Benedict XV. For the past seven years he has been Archbishop of Bologna and was only raised to the Cardinalate in May of this year—at the same time as Cardinal Gasquet. The new Pope's election is a great surprise. His name had never been mentioned among those likely to succeed Pius X., and his com- parative youth—lie is only 59—and the fact that lie had so recently been made a Cardinal seemed to render his election most improbable. Giacomo (James) della Chiesa was born at Pegli, the well-known seaside resort near Genoa, on November 21st, 1854, and was ordained priest on December 21st, 1878. He spent the five years 1883-87 in Spain as secretary to the Nuncio, and in the latter year was appointed secretary to Oardinal Rampo fPapal Secretary of State. In 1901 he became Consultor to the Holy Office, and on December 16th, 1907, was appointed Archbishop of Bologna and was con- secrated by Pius X. himself a week later. It is a curious fact that his predecessor in the archbishopric, Cardinal Svampa, was consid- ered at one time as very likely to be Pope. The new Pope's choice of his title is explained by the fact that the celebrated Benedict XIV. (1740-58) was also Arohbishop of Bologna. Benedict XV. will be the fourth Archbishop of Bologna to become Pope.</p>
        <p>Preachers for To-morrow. Bewsey-rogcl Wesleyan—Morning, Arr. j. Upson; evening; Rev. Williamson. Legh-street Primitive Methodist—Morning and evening, Rev. A. J. Chessurn. Wycliffe Congregational (40th Dedication Anniversary)—Morning and evening, Rev. T. T. James. Golborne-street Baptist—Morning and even- ing, Rev. H. Ogden. Cairo-street Chapel—Morning and 'evening, Rev. Irvine Lister. Latchford Baptist—Morning and evening, Rev. J. S. Chedburn. St. John's Presbyterian—Morning and even- ing, Rev. W. Reid. Padgate Wesleyan Church (Choir Anniver- sary)—Morning, Rev. W. Thompson Derry; evening, Rev. Arthur I. Morton.</p>
      </div>
