        <p>Scarlet Fever Spreads. There were 93 cases of scarlet fever in the Warrington Hospital last week. Thirty-three new cases have been reported.</p>
        <p>Warrington Charities' Festival. The annual festival of the Warrington Charities will be held at Whalley, near Black- burn, on September 21st. The war will not cause the abandonment of the enterprise.</p>
        <p>Church Sympathy With Catholics. The present is a fitting time to express our deep sympathy with our Roman Catholic brethren," says the Parish Church magazine, on the loss they have sustained in the death of Pope Pius X.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Births and Deaths.</head>
        <p>Births and Deaths.</p>
        <p>—.-- The births in Warringtoa last week numbered 43, giving a rate of 30.5, compared with a rate of 23.2 in the same week last year. The deaths numbered 19, giving a rate of 13.1, compared with 17.5 last year.</p>
        <p>Church Lectures Postponed. The Committee of the Warrington Church Society have decided to postpone the lectures on "Papal claims," which they had asked Father Puller to give at the Parish Church in Novem- ber. They feel that at the present time all con- troversial subjects are best avoided.</p>
        <p>40144401444.1440.14,14•1q4•14•14•144•1014014, "NOT WAR ./4 BUT ASSASSINATION."</p>
        <p>II The following letter appeared in the -4; Paris edition of the "New York 014 Herald" under the above heading: 4; + 4 (By TELEGRAPH.) 4 014 Antwerp, Wednesday. i To i tahme Ew di ti ti o rt hoef Lhaed the Zeppelin slaughter. "aHnderwalodu'n'd—ed of. 3: sThe Germans 'attacked a sleeping city like a thief in the night, murder- + ing helpless women and children. q, In the name of civilisation let * + America protest. This is not war, ,T. but assassination. A, I appeal especially to the Peace 144 Society. SEAMAN, PP Surgeon-Major United States I Army Reserve Corps. 014 PP 011+++++40101"14+4401444+444+40R1</p>
        <p>Woman's Trying Experience. The story of what was described as a serious assault was related at an Occasional Court held in Warrington on Tuesday, before Mr. W. Furness.—Michael Burns, of no fixed abode, was the accused. The evidence showed that on August 24th two ladies were walking across a field in the Padgate district when the prisoner rushed at one and knocked her to the ground. In the subsequent struggle the ladies beat the man with their umbrellas, which were both broken in the fight. A Warrington youth's at- tention was drawn to the scene by hearing the woman's screams and he gave chase. The pri- soner ran towards Paddington. Locks and the young man's cry of "Stop that man!" brought the lockkeeper to the scene, and he, with gun in hand, threatened to shoot tire prisoner if he attempted to run away. The police came and prisoner was later identified as the man who had committed the offence.—Prisoner was com- mitted to the Quarter Sessions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Bible or Music Hall?</head>
        <p>Bible or Music Hall?</p>
        <p>"Almost everybody in England professes to have a great veneration for the Bible," says the St. James' Parish Magazine, "but not a great number of people read it methodically and regularly. Why? Because much of the Bible is very difficult and needs patient study and careful explanation." .The clergy tried to make the explanations, but at the Wednesday evening services at St. James' Church the con- gregations numbered between ten and twenty. " We think," continues the Magazine, "that more people might and should attend. How many persons make a point of going to the Hippodrome, the Theatre, a picture palace once a week—and paying to be admitted! It will be retorted Yes, but the variety entertain- ment, or the play, or the pictures, are very interesting, and the study of the Bible must be very dull.' Now people who argue in this way are as a mile those who do not know much about the Holy Scriptures, because, as a matter of fact, they are full of profound interest, an interest, we admit, that must be acquired. But it is well worth the while to try."</p>
        <p>• A Popular New Postmaster. The appointment of a new Postmaster for Warrington, a position resigned a short time ago by Mr. J. Wyard, who had been promoted to a similar post at Middlesbrough, has now been made. The choice falls upon Mr. Harry Chambers, who has commenced his new duties during the week. Warringtonians will hear of the appointment with pleasure, as, to all in- tents, the new Postmaster is one of them, by virtue of his long connection with the town, both officially and socially. Mr. Chambers was educated at Leeds Grammar School, and, upon leaving this institution, entered the General Post Office. 'There were prospects, however, of the telephone system developing consider- ably, for which reason he commenced later in the telephone department. His labours were attended with great success, for he has since held appointments at Birmingham, York, in Cumberland, and in this county. From 1900 to 1912, Mr. Chambers occupied the position of Warrington district manager of the National Telephone Company, and upon the Government taking over the telephone system ,he was trans- ferred into their service, and appointed to the Bolton district. His experience will prove valuable to Warrington, since the work of the General Post Office is very closely connected with that of the telephones. As a matter of fact, the whole of the local telephone service will shortly be worked from the Post Office. Mr. Chambers and his wife resided at Padgate during the first eight years of their local life, afterwards removing to Springfield-street. They took a keen interest in Christ Church, Padgate, and Mr. Chambers was 'an enthusiastic sup- porter of the Dramatic Society, which the vil- lage boasted at the time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>AN IRISH SOLDIER'S TOUCHING LETTER HOME.</head>
        <p>AN IRISH SOLDIER'S TOUCHING LETTER HOME.</p>
        <p>Among the many letters from British soldiers is this simple and touching one written by Private P. Arc Glade, from "somewhere in France" to his mother in Ireland:—</p>
        <p>We were all beat up after four clays of the hardest soldiering you ever dreamt of . . . I am glad to say we accounted for our share of the German trash, who area poor lot when it comes to a good square ruction in the open. We tried hard to get at them many a time, but they never would wait for us when they saw the bright bits of steel at the business end of our rifles. Some of them squeal like the pigs on killing day when they see the steel ready. Some of our finest lads are now sleeping their last sleep in Belgium, but, mother dear, you can take your son's word for it that for every son of Ireland who will never come back there are at least three Germans who will</p>
        <p>never be heard of again. Before leaving Belgium we arranged with a priest to have masses said for the souls of our dead, and we scraped together what odd money we had, but his reverence wouldn't hear Of if, taking our money for prayers for the repose of the brave lads who had died so far from the Old Lane to rid Belgian soil of the unmannerly German scrubs. When we got here we sang "Paddies Ever- more" till we were hoarse, and then we were off to chapel to pray for the souls of the lads that are gone. You will probably hear all sorts of strange stories at home, but you may be sure that we are in good heart, and only want to get at the Germans again. Some of them don't understand why Irishmen should fight so hard for England, but that just shows how little they know about us, and what a difference it makes with the Home Rule coming.</p>
        <p>It ,is comforting to think we're as old as we feel, but the stubborn fact remains that we're as old as we are.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, sy,Tsm.py4 5, 1914. IF CONQUERED?</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, sy,Tsm.py4 5, 1914. IF CONQUERED?</p>
        <p>Magnificent Response to the Call to</p>
        <p>Duty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>What Would be the Workers' Fate Under German Rule?</head>
        <p>What Would be the Workers' Fate Under German Rule?</p>
        <p>ATTITUDE OF THE I.L.P.</p>
        <p>BY "A WORKER."</p>
        <p>_ If the Germans succeeded in conquering Britain, what would be the position of the workers? This was the question discussed lamongst a group of my fellow-workers one evening this week. I was far from satisfied with the conclusions some of them reached, and perhaps this may have been in a degree due to the fact that too much has been deducted from the resolutions passed by the .executive of the Independent Labour party. At a conference earlier in the week that body passed a resolution in which they decided not to take any part in the present recruiting boom, on the ground that they could not assist militarism. To those who know the constitu- tion of the I.L.P. this must come as no surprise. The executive would have to swallow some of its most important constitutional clauses to have arrived at any other decision.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>RANK AND FILE VIEW.</head>
        <p>RANK AND FILE VIEW.</p>
        <p>_ The rank and file of the however, take a different view, and it is roughly estimated that 25 per cent, of the members are now on active service, either with the Regulars, with Kitchener's Armies, or with the Territorials. The rank and file have found it much more easy to swallow the constitution than have the executive.</p>
        <p>Therefore too much should not be made of the I.L.P. resolution, as unfortunately has been done in some quarters. In Warrington alone I know of scores of 1.L.P.-ers who have gone on active service, with the promise of more ready to go when required. There is not a trade union but what has lost members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>CONDITIONS MADE WORSE.</head>
        <p>CONDITIONS MADE WORSE.</p>
        <p>But I have got away from the subject of what would be the position of workers if the Ger- mans conquered this country. One argument was put forward that the workers would be no worse under German rule than under Eng- lish rule. Anyone who believes that is pin- ning his faith to a fallacy. It has been ray lot to meet, from time to time, German trade unionists, and they, after a careful study of the condition of our workers, were unanimous of the opinion that we possessed privileges and conditions which could never prevail in Ger- many.</p>
        <p>In the Fatherland the workers have not a tithe of the political power we possess, they are ground down under the heel of autocracy, and even if they have won victories at the elections, the landed, Church and nobility interests are all-powerful in their Parliament. The result is seen in the long hours the workers have to labour; they have no half-day on Saturday, and except in a few model factories—the show places for foreign visitors—the conditions are very bad.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>AN INTERESTING COMPARISON.</head>
        <p>AN INTERESTING COMPARISON.</p>
        <p>Dusseldorf is a town in close resemblance to Warrington, yet while our iron workers here have about a 50-hour week, the workers of Dusseldorf put in 60 hours and upwards for a wage below the standard rate in Warrington. The food is worse, the housing is worse, and the people have not a fraction of the freedom we Possess in Warrington. In only one respect can the general conditions be said to be equal, and it is that Warrington and Dusseldorf each possesses a Labour Exchange designed to find work for the unemployed. Other German towns are much worse.</p>
        <p>Suppose we were conquered, we would find that Mr. "Smith" would lose his factory and his business would be taken over by Mr. "Schmidt" (the German c:quivalent, of "Smith") and worked on German lines. All hope of a 48-hour week would go, 'so would the half-day on Saturday. A heavy tariff to fight the Americans and other unconquered countries would be imposed. The food would certainly cost more, and autocratic rule would make short work of trade unionism.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>NATION OF CONSCRIPTS.</head>
        <p>A NATION OF CONSCRIPTS.</p>
        <p>_ _ ..__.... If the German war lord ruled us from London conscription would be certain; he would have still other worlds to conquer, and the Britons would have to fight his battles. It is not so many years since he spoke about the hordes of China as " the yellow peril which would have to be removed," and with British-pressed soldiers he would soon be able to attack what has been a bogey with him for the last ten years.</p>
        <p>More could be written, but it is not neces- sary' it should. Suffice it then to say that the reason why trade unionists—tanners, gaswork- ers, labourers, engineers, every branch of workers in Warrington—are now rolling up to join the colours is that, apart from the love for their country, they fully realise what a miser- able life would be theirs under German rule.</p>
        <p>A SOCIALIST'S EXAMPLE. Henry Ball, the assistant secretary to the Carters' Union, who is well known to Warring- ton carters and who was engaged here when the carters were on strike over a year ago, is a typical Socialist, who has set an example to his fellows. He is among those who have enlisted this week.</p>
        <p>Speaking to a friend at the railway station, prior to his departure on Thursday, he said : "In the labour struggles of the past few years I have fought with the boys through thick and thin; now I am going out to fight side by side with them in the ranks."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>WYCLIFFE MEMORIAL HALL. Appeal Against the Assessment Dismissed.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE MEMORIAL HALL. Appeal Against the Assessment Dismissed.</p>
        <p>An appeal against the assessment of the Wycliffe Memorial Buildings was made by the Wycliffe Trustees at the•monthly meeting of the Assessment Committee, held at the Workhouse yesterday morning. Mr. W. Furness presided.</p>
        <p>The buv ildings -were assessed at IS lOs gross and ..t7 rateable.</p>
        <p>Mr. P. T. Winskill, the treasurer of the church, wrote apologising for his absence. He stated that the buildings, erected 13 years ago, were long desired, because of the unfitness of the day school premises for the necessary meetings held in connection with the church and Sunday schools. "It came as a very great surprise," he wrote, " that after being exempt from rates until now, we should be asked to pay on a building where meetings are held on exactly similar lines to those which, prior to 1901, were held in the schools or in a room under the church organ.</p>
        <p>"These Meetings consisted of week-night ser- vices, ladies' sewing classes, weekly meetings of young people for discussion of papers, and meet- ings of other organisations connected with the church. There were other outside organisations which used the buildings, buethe entire income from all sources for the last nine complete years averaged £2 10s. per annum, against which was the cost of lighting, cleaning and warming." The use of these buildings was quite different from others considered to be of a similar kind. There were no billiards, card parties, whist drives, dances, or anything of that nature, the officers of the church having declined, on many occasions, to allow the use of the rooms for such purposes. Mr. Woodcock said the buildings were cer- tainly rateable, and therefore the only question to consider was whether the assessment was a reasonably nominal one under the circumstances and considering the small income. That church institution was little used by outsiders:</p>
        <p>The committee decided to.make no change in the assessment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>ALL ANXIOUS TO FIGHT MILITARISM.</head>
        <p>ALL ANXIOUS TO FIGHT MILITARISM.</p>
        <p>A CALL FROM THE RANKS.</p>
        <p>ARMY.</p>
        <p>Warrington Sergeant's Advice.</p>
        <p>A Warrington sergeant, •now at the front, sent the following message to his townsmen, by means of a letter home, and we publish it to show the spirit in which Tommy Atkins is approaching his task, and the hopes he enter- tains of getting a sufficient backing in the way of reinforcements:—</p>
        <p>Dear Brother and Sister,— During the lull I find time to drop you a line. Here we are still getting fit for the fateful day, which will decide for a long time the question of the hour—England, Home and Beauty, or Boastful Militarism of Germany? What's it to be? will be decided on the Con- tinent by the youth of England. • Dear , People do not yet realise the serious peril confronting England as a nation. The youth and manhood will have to assert itself pretty quickly, or we are done for, which God forbid. We want men, men, men! Our troops must be reinforced every week. No gaps must be allowed, and in six months' time we will march triumphantly into Berlin, and woe to the German Emperor on this day! But six months is not now, and we are going to be sorely tested before we can assume the offensive in action. The offensive must always remain the birthright of the British Army. Send us the men, for the women and children's sake, and we can play our part, as our forefathers played it before us. Good God! Football in such a time, when hundreds of our brave lads are being cruelly shot down every day! It is not right to watch I the game, much less play. The only game now is the National one—War—and we are playing away every time. Do your best to make chaps see this point, dear Pro- bably the next casualty list will make the people of England realise the awful tragedy being enacted in Fair France. I am still at ---. I shall soon be on my way, to help the brave lads there. Was ever British pluck more manifest than In the recent fighting? Outnumbered seven to one, we contest every inch of ground, and before our troops retire the ground is deluged with Prussian blood. The memory of our fore- fathers spurs our noble lads on to feats of courage and endurance never equalled before. These events will be writ large in history pages. British pluck and valour! How sweet the sound! How noble! How heroic! It is magnificent to be British! Forsooth, football! The final will be played in Berlin_ Roll up in your thousands! No admission! Free trip and refreshments provided on the way! Put your names down early. Be in time for the kick-off! The Allied Forces will commence their march in about three weeks. That's the Idea! Send us best wishes. An armed nation at home, valiant soldiers abroad! We'll stand the shock, but, by the living God, we'll beat the Prussians yet, and woe to the vanquished. Pray God speed the day. The nation of football spectators must be turned into Army operators, and all will be well.</p>
        <p>Recruiting, locally, nationally and imperially, is making satisfactory progress, a great wave of enthusiasm having kept the Military Authorities exceedingly busy. There are indications that this wave will not have spent Itself until it has carried the Kaiser and his Huns back to Berlin, and somewhere beyond.</p>
        <p>'MAYOR PROPOSES A CIVILIAN</p>
        <p>Town's Promise to Territorials on Active Service.</p>
        <p>Warrington is promised a Civilian Guard, and perhaps a regiment, which may bear the title of " The Warrington Regiment," that may form an important unit in Kitchener's Second Army. The scheme was outlined at the meet- ing of the Town Council on Tuesday by the Mayor (Alderman Peacock), and it was enthu- siastically received by the members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>LOYAL WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>LOYAL WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A Rich Harvest of Recruits.</p>
        <p>Trains packed with loyalists who have joined the Kitchener Army at local depots, have left the town almost nightly, and crowds—in some cases reaching extreme proportions—have gath- ered outside the entrance to Bank Quay Station to give them a send-off. The recruits are jolly fellows, who seem to have taken a leaf from the notebook of their comrades already on the Continent, for they sing "It's a long, long way to Tipperary," and other popular songs with a gusto, which, if not exactly harmonious, gives an index to the healthy state of their lungs. Numbers of these men, and those who have responded to the telegram received by the Rev. W. Bracecamp for 300 more for the Territorials, have been shouldering their arms and executing drilling movements on the children's recrea- tion ground in Bank Park, which at times very much reminds one of a barrack square—that is, if the eyes fail to observe the green trees and the grass! In addition to the drilling there it is also being carried on at Orford Barracks, where many enthusiastic scenes can be wit- nessed in the course of the day. The large num- ber of able-bodied men who have come forward is very remarkable, and so great has been the pressure upon the recruiting officers and the medical examiners that undoubtedly they will feel somewhat relieved when the supply is ex- hausted! In the town's main streets one can- not move without coming into contact with a merry party whose talk indicates that they "have passed the doctor," and that they would immediately like to be transferred to the seat of the war to have a "pop" at the villainous murderers of helpless men, women and children. Well done, Warrington! Loyalty has made your men happy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>CIIAPLAIN'S APPEAL. Rush to Join the "Terriers."</head>
        <p>CIIAPLAIN'S APPEAL. Rush to Join the "Terriers."</p>
        <p>The telegram which the Chaplain (the Rev. W. Bracecarrip) had received from the Terri- torial Headquarters in Scotland, and which ran "Three hundred recruits urgently wanted to complete battalion for foreign service. Recruit- ing open at the Drill Hall," was read in a number of churches on Sunday evening. At St. Paul's Church, the Vicar (the Rev. W. Bracecamp), after disclosing the words to the congregation, called out: "Now lads, what's it to be?" Compulsion was in the air, but he wanted St. Paul's. lads to volunteer and to be at the Drill Hall the following morning. _ _ _ . _</p>
        <p>• At the Parish Church the Rector, after read- ing the telegram, said: If the 300 survivors of that noble band of 700 who faced death the other day return to England, what will they think of the physically fit young men who are playing tennis, cricket and football, and who refuse to give assistance?" The youths of Warrington were not slow in responding, and the officials at the Drill Hall were given a busy time all day on Monday and again on Tuesday. The officers, who work all day and well into the night, are Lieutenant Robertson, the Rev. William Bracecamp (chap- lain), and Drill-Instructor Russell, and they have received assistance from a number of well- known civilians.</p>
        <p>The required 300 could have been obtained several times over.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>PRIME MINISTER LEADS THE WAY.</head>
        <p>PRIME MINISTER LEADS THE WAY.</p>
        <p>The Prime Minister has addressed to the Lord Mayor of London, the Lord Provost of Edinburgh, the Lord Mayor of Dublin, and the Lord Mayor of Cardiff a letter, in which he says that the time has come for combined effort by all parties to stimulate and organise public opinion and public effort in the greatest con- flict in which our people have ever been en- gaged. The Premier proposes as a first step that meetings should be held without delay in every district throughout the United Kingdom, at which the justice of the war should be smile plain, and the duty of every man to do his part should be enforced. He himself is ready, as far as the exigencies of public duty permit, to render such help as he can, and he would be glad with that object to address meetings in the , four capital cities. The other political parties agreed to join in the effort, and the first of the meetings was called for London last night, Mr. Boner Law, Leader of the Opposition, having arranged to speak along with the Premier.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>MAYORAL SCHEME.</head>
        <p>THE MAYORAL SCHEME.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said: Gentlemen,—The circum- stances of the war justify me in introducing at this stage a statement as to the steps I propose to take on behalf of the borough. It is my intention to get together the men to form a Civilian Army and to see that they are drilled and trained in the use of arms so as to be ready for any emergency. I have been approached on the subject from several quarters and I shall be glad if all the rifle and other similar associations will join with me in form- ing one strong body of men so that they can all be trained and work together. (Hear, hear.) All men desirous of joining should send in their names to the Acting Executive Committee at the Parr Hall, where their names can be enrolled and a start made in drilling and train- ing. , What I have outlined will in no *ay inter- I fere with the enlistment of men for Lord Kitchener's Army, of which I am most anxious a local battalion should be formed. With this object of forming a local battalion I have in- vited Lord Derby to address a public meeting of the men of Warrington in Parr Hall on Saturday qvening at eight o'clock. lam glad to tell yod that Lord Derby has expressed his willingness to attend—(applause)—and I shall be glad of the support of the members of the Council on the platform. Assistance for "Terriers'" Dependents.</p>
        <p>There is one other matter to which I should like to refer, and that is that during my absence from town on Saturday last the Deputy Mayor received a telegram from Colonel Fairclough, the Commanding Officer of our local Terri- torials, asking if, in the event of casualties among his men during active foreign service, their wives and other dependents would be looked after by the town. The Deputy Mayor, with my entire approval, and, I am sure, with your approbation and support, sent the follow- ing telegram to Colonel Fairclough "Mayor unfortunately away, but we, on behalf of the inhabitants of Warrington, here- by undertake, should the country fail in its duty, to look after and adequately provide for , the wives and all other dependents of your men in the event of casualties to any of them on foreign service." (Hear, hear.) I have not the slightest doubt that the coun- try will do its duty, and that the wives and other dependents of those who suffer by the war will be amply and properly provided for. (Hear, hear.) All I can add is that speaking on behalf of our Warrington lads, should the country fail in its duty, we will see that their wives and children do not want. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>QUESTIONS NOT ALLOWED.</head>
        <p>QUESTIONS NOT ALLOWED.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT said he had a question to ask.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: I really cannot now allow a ques- tion.</p>
        <p>Alderman SmsTarnsT I don't know who is responsible, but this afternoon I saw one of the doctors who was examining the reCruits, and</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: I cannot allow a discussion on the subject. Alderman SMETHURST : But the men are leav- ing the town, and are going back to their own homes in Earlestown and other places in search of work.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: The authorities are getting on with the recruiting as fast as they can. I don't think the patriotism of the men will fail. Alderman BENNETT : I saw in the papers on Saturday that a body of special constables, numbering 150, had been formed in Warrington in view of the emergency, and the idea I have in my mind is that they might form the nucleus of a corps. The MAYOR: I shall call a meeting, and it can decide what it likes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>MEETING POSTPONED.</head>
        <p>THE MEETING POSTPONED.</p>
        <p>Illness of Lord Derby.</p>
        <p>It is announced that the meeting which the Mayor had decided to hold in the Parr Hall this evening has been postponed, Lord Derby,. who was to be the chief speaker, has been taken ill, and in the circumstances it has been decided to abandon the meeting for the time being, but it will be held at an early date when his Lordship has recovered from what is a slight indisposition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Mg CMG GUARD.</head>
        <p>Mg CMG GUARD.</p>
        <p>Following upon the statement by the Mayor that lie intended to form a Civic Guard, several Rifle Clubs and the Preparatory Training Corps inaugurated at the Conservative Club this week have placed themselves in communi- cation with the Mayor, offering to join his corps, steps for the Organisation of which will be taken as early as possible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>RECRUITS FROM THE WORKS. A Splendid Rally to the Colours.</head>
        <p>RECRUITS FROM THE WORKS. A Splendid Rally to the Colours.</p>
        <p>From the larger works in Warrington there has been a splendid rally of the workers to serve gallantly their King and country, and, in addition, the smaller works have sent their quota. Accurate numbers it has been impos- sible to ascertain at present, but approximately it was estimated that between 1,800 and 2,000 of Warrington workers were enlisted within the past week, a most noble response for a town of the size of Warrington. Last night " The Examiner" representative learned that eight of the largest works alone produced about 1,680 men, all smart, soldier- like chaps. The smaller works combined pro- duced between 350 and 500 equally serviceable men. The approximate figures last night were : Crosfield's Pearson. and Knowles</p>
        <p>Rylands' Whitecross</p>
        <p>Fletcher, Russell's Monks, Hall St. Helens Cable Co. ' Richmond's</p>
        <p>1,680 Other works</p>
        <p>Total 1,830</p>
        <p>This does not include the men who enlisted previously, and the figures are believed to be below the actual numbers. With the Reser- vists, Territorials, &amp;c., Warrington must now have between 4,000 and 5,000 men in the Army, or equal to four battalions at war strength. There are also a number of Warringtonians in the Navy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>PATRIOTIC SCHOOL TEACHERS.</head>
        <p>PATRIOTIC SCHOOL TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>All the male teachers at the Beamont Council Schools, Warrington, have offered themselves for military service at home or abroad. Five of the teachers have been accepted for service and haVe joined the Army Ordnance</p>
        <p>Corps and leave Warrington on Tuesday. This leaves the Headmaster, Mr. W. Mason, who is over the age limit, with only one teacher, and she a lady, as far as the boys' department is concerned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>700 MEN PASSED AT POLICE</head>
        <p>700 MEN PASSED AT POLICE</p>
        <p>STATION.</p>
        <p>About 700 recruits have been passed at the Police Station, which has been turned into a recruiting office during the latter part of the week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>POLITICAL PARTIES JOIN FORCES TO OBTAIN RECRUITS.</head>
        <p>POLITICAL PARTIES JOIN FORCES TO OBTAIN RECRUITS.</p>
        <p>State Committee. The crisis is so gave, and the need for more recruits, in addition to the force of 100,000 just raised, is so vital to the interests of the country, that Lord Kitchener has inquired of the leaders of the great political parties if they would jointly agree that their organisations should give their full co-operation in securing the enlistment of more men.</p>
        <p>To this request there could be but one answer, which was given unhesitatingly and unani- mously, and a joint Parliamentary Recruiting Committee has been created to carry out Lord Kitchener's wishes, and, being formed to unite the official party organisations, is constituted of the Leaders and Whips of the parties and their chief officials.</p>
        <p>Two distinguished officials of the War Office have also consented to serve on the committee.</p>
        <p>The head office is at 12, Downing-street, S.W., with the branch offices of the joint honorary secretaries, who are: Sir Jesse Herbert, 21, Abingdon-street, Westminster, S.W.; Mr. John Boraston, St. Stephen's Chambers, Westmin- ster, S.W.; Mr. Arthur Peters, 28, Victoria- street, S.W.</p>
        <p>Assistant honorary secretary, Mr. R. H. Davies, C. 8., 12, Downing-street, S.W. The committee will appeal to the political associations throughout the country to give general assistance to the work of recruiting; will enlist the sympathies and help of peers, Members of Parliament, ilnd Parliamentary candidates; will assist the County Recruiting Committees in obtaining Parliamentary speak- ers for public meetings; will issue and circu- late suitable publications in leaflet and pamph- let form, and will in every possible way work in conjunction with the recruiting agencies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>TRADE UNIONISTS' WARNING.</head>
        <p>TRADE UNIONISTS' WARNING.</p>
        <p>- - The Parliamentary Committee of the Trades Unip!i Congress issued on Thursday a manifesto approving of the co-operation of the Labour party in the House of Commons in this cam- paign. The committee expresses the opinion that .the voluntary system of military service is on its trial, and that, if it fails, the demand for a national system of compulsory military service may prove to be so persistent and strong as to become irresistible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>MISS CHRISTABEL PANKHURST TO SPEAK.</head>
        <p>MISS CHRISTABEL PANKHURST TO SPEAK.</p>
        <p>In view of the German menace to national safety and British citizenship the Women's Social and Political Union has organiiied a cam- paign of public )neetings with the object of rousing the country to an even stronger defence against the enemy. Miss Christabel Pankhurst, who has during the last two and a half years lived in Paris, has now returned to London, and will address the first meeting at the Lon- don Opera House on Tuesday, September Bth, at eight o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>TO NONCONFORMISTS. Powerful Appeal by Sir W. Robertson</head>
        <p>TO NONCONFORMISTS. Powerful Appeal by Sir W. Robertson</p>
        <p>Nicoll. A powerful appeal to young Nonconformists, by Sir W. Robertson Nicoll, editor of the " British Weekly," is given : I call on all Nonconformists who can fight to se down their names in this hour of crisis, and to enlist without delay. That Nonconformists are neither cowardly nor incapable when called to a righteous war the glorious name of Oliver Cromwell sufficiently attests. That this is a most righteous and necessary war is a proposi- tion which cannot be contested. For—</p>
        <p>(1) It is a war that was none of our seeking. We strove for peace to the last minute, if not the last second, of the last hour, and we strove in vain. The contention was forced upon us. (2) We went into this war because it involved the keeping of the most solemn and sacred obligations. We were bound by treaty. We went forth in the cause of the smaller nations, and especially in the cause of Belgium, which has kept troth with us so bravely. (3) We entered the war because, as increas- ingly appears, it was a war against barbarism of the most evil and remorseless kind—a war for freedom, civilisation, and Christianity. Christian law has been treated by our oppon- ents as of less than no account, and their code of conduct is infinitely baser than that of savages._</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>." OUR LIFE AS A NATION."</head>
        <p>." OUR LIFE AS A NATION."</p>
        <p>•- • . -•-• (4) We are now fighting for our very life as a nation. If we are subjugated by Germany we have no higher future before us than the life of a tributary province harassed and hum- iliated at every point—a life so intolerable that death is infinitely preferred. (5) In particular, this is a war for the people. They have seen it. They perceive that the very existence of democracy as it has flourished in this country is threatened with a death-wound. If we could imagine the war-lords flushed with so stupendous a triumph as a prostrate Europe at their feet, no one would fail to see that the essential elements of honourable, happy, ?elf- respecting life would be at an end. (6) We are fighting .for our children as -)ur fathers fought for us. When this war is over and peace arrives, the world will enter on a day bright with promise for those who are to follow us, (7) The result of this world-war may depend on a very slight preponderance of force on either side. Every man who can fight is wanted, and wanted at once. Hitherto there has been no attempt to drive the people, be- cause it is felt that they can be led, and that when the issues are set clearly before them they will abhor as infinitely worse than death the stain of cowardice. For the Order of the White Feather there will soon be no room in our land.</p>
        <p>OVER QUARTER OF A MILLION RECRUITS. Everything shows that the people of Great Britain and the Dominions have fully realised the need for a supreme effort. In the British Isles 260,000 recruits for general service have joined the colours sinee the war began.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>IRISII VOLUNTEERS.</head>
        <p>IRISII VOLUNTEERS.</p>
        <p>The Ulster Volunteers and the National Vol- unteers have largely volunteered for active service. •</p>
        <p>Sir Edward Carson, in Belfast yesterday, headed the first batch of a thousand Ulster Volunteers to the recruiting offices. Three shiploads of arms and ammunition have been landed in Ireland within the past few days and have been distributed among the National Volunteers. The larger number were sent to the North of Ireland, and every county in the province has received its quota. Mr. Redmond, it is said, has entered into negotiations for a large additional supply of modern rifles and am- munition. Mr. T. M. Kettle and Mr. John O'Connor, were responsible for all the arrangements connected with the landing of the gu4s. and ammunition.</p>
        <p>FROM THE EMPIRE. In the Dominions the enthusiasm of the people rises daily. Canada is prepared to send far more than the 20,000 men already promised, and the other Dominions have no need of this example to spur them on. So anxious are the Canadians to get to the front that over 6,000 more than have been called for have gone to Valcartier at their own expense, hoping to be enlisted. American offers to serve are pouring in by the thousand. The Minister of Militia on Thursday received the offer of a regiment of Virginians 1,000 strong, and all officered. According to Colonel Hughes, Minister of Militia, 60,000 citizens of the 'United States have offered to enlist in the Canadian Expeditionary Forces. Of course, no Americans could be en- rolled.</p>
        <p>Canadians from the United States are return- ing by hundreds to join their regiments. Colonel Hughes has estimated that 10 per cent. of those already at Valeartier are returned Canadians. Sir Donald Mann was an interested specta- tor at Victoria, 8.C., of the departure for</p>
        <p>' '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <p>active service of the contingent from the sth Regiment. He was much impressed by the demonstration by the people.'</p>
        <p>Ten thousand citizens at a mass meeting at Winnipeg determined, as Canada is an integral portion of the Empire, to do their full. rart in its defence, and urged the Dominion Govern- ment, in addition to keeping the Militia at full strength, to raise an additional force of 100,000 men in order to be enabled to send suezessive divisions to support and augment the Army in the field.</p>
        <p>Canada's great gift of 98,000,0001 b. of flour to the people of Great Britain has been quickly followed up by the offer of 4,000,0001 b. of cheese by the Province of Quebec. Dairying prevails on a very large scale in this Province, the total yearly yield of the in- dustry being approximately A:6,000,000. Quebec cheese has obtained the highest prices on the Canadian market.</p>
        <p>The planters of Mauritius have offered' 1,000,0001 b. of sugar to, Mr. Churchill for the Navy, and a similar amount to Lord Kitchener for the Army. The directors of the Canadian Pacific Railway Company voted .t 20,000 to the National Patriotic Fund. The Royal Bank of Canada has given £lO,OOO to the Dominion Patriotic Fund, and the Dominion Bank £5,000 to the Toronto and York Relief Fund.</p>
        <p>A message from Wellington says that the Prime Minister estimates that the total offers to the Patriotic Fund, including the value of horses and motor cars, amount to £150,000.</p>
        <p>INDIAN PRINCE'S SPLENDID GIFT. It is announced that the Maharajah of Mysore has offered 50 lakhs of rupees (.033,333) for the Indian forces; which the Viceroy has accepted. The Maharajah has also offered his troops, which the Viceroy has promised shall be called upon should occasion arise.</p>
        <p>FALL IN! • 4, What will you lack, sonny, what will you lack, When the girls line up the street, Shouting their love to the lads come back From the foe they rushed to beat? Will you send a strangled cheer to the sky And grin till your cheeks are red? But what will you lack when your mate goes by With a girl who cuts you dead? Where will you look, sonny, where will you look When your children yet to be Clamour to learn of the part you took In the War that kept men free? Will you say it was naught to you if France Stood up to her foe or bunked ? But where will you look when they give the glance That tells you they know you funked?</p>
        <p>1 , -i.:i''.. ~...'. ... 1 1</p>
        <p>How will you-fare, sonny, how will you fare In the far-off winter night, When you sit by the fire in an old man's chair And your neighbours talk of the fight? Will you slink away, as it were from a blow, Your old head shamed and bent? Or say—l was not with the first to go, But I went, thank God, I went?</p>
        <p>Why do they call, sonny, why do they call For men who are brave and strong? Is it naught to you If your country fall, And Right is smashed by Wrong? Is it football still and the picture show, The pub and the betting odds, When your brothers stand to the tyrant's blow And England's call is God's? —Harold Begbie in the " Daily Chronicle."</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>FEEDING HUNDREDS.</head>
        <p>FEEDING HUNDREDS.</p>
        <p>Beamont Scholars' Thoughtful and</p>
        <p>Kindly Action.</p>
        <p>PILES OF PROVISIONS.</p>
        <p>Somewhat of a miracle, on the lines of the loaves and fishes, was performed by the scholars attending the Beamont Council Schools on Wednesday. Through their thoughtfulness and kind-heartedness, over 700 mon waiting to enlist in Kitchener's Army were given "a right square meal," as one Tommy put it. Not only was food given, but even such luxuries as cigarettes and tobacco were dispensed freely, and, again to quote Tommy's phraseology, the guests "were done proper." There was a great influx to the vicinity of the Orford Barracks on Wednesday of would-be re- cruits, many coming from Wigan and other places in Lancashire, and one man was said to have come from South Wales. The rush of ap- plicants was so great that many had a long wait before they could undergo the necessary medical examination.</p>
        <p>DELUGE OF FOODSTUFFS. Fearing that the men might feel the pangs of hunger during their long wait, Mr. W. Mason, the head master, suggested that some food should be collected for the use of the men, and he sent his scholars home for this purpose. He was not prepared for the deluge of foodstuffs which, in less than an hour, was pouring in upon him. Such a great regard have the residents in the vicinity of the Barracks for Tommy Atkins, that ere long crowds were assembling with food. Children came laden with loaves, sandwiches, fruit and a wide collection of other articles. Mothers came with huge jugs of steaming hot tea. coffee and cocoa. Others brought dainties, and in a short time there was nearly sufficient to feed a battalion for a week gathered in the school buildings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>IMPROVISED CANTEEN.</head>
        <p>IMPROVISED CANTEEN.</p>
        <p>A large table was provided as the centre of this improvised canteen, and from behind it the elder school girls, assisted by a number of women, anxious to make themselves useful, dis- pensed food, tea, &amp;c., fruit, and even tobacco to the men. It was estimated that between 700 and 800 men got ,a generous meal. When the canteen closed at six o'clock there was a large stock of provisions in hand, and it was then pro- posed that the feeding process should be re- peated on Thursday. _ _</p>
        <p>Meanwhile later on Wednesday evening, "The Examiner" representative was informed the officer in command at the Barracks got in touch with Mr. Mason and requested that the feast should be stopped, as complete arrangements had been made for the feeding of the men in the Barracks during their time of waiting. RESULT OF A CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>On Thursday morning the Mayor (Alderman Peacock), Mr. J. Moore Murray, Director of Education, apd Mr. W. Mason. the head master, met the officer in command, and afterwards "The Examiner" representative was informed that the officer had given the Mayor an assur- ance that no one would be turned away without food during the period of waiting between his enlisting and passing the final examination. On this assurance it was decided to abandon the temporary canteen, and re-distribute the food amongst the donors. The prompt way in which the public responded to the head master's request indicated how sympathetic they are to the lads in khaki called up to fight for King and country.</p>
        <p>"WORK FOR .WOMEN" FUND. The Queen's Appeal. In the following wortia the Queen inaugurates a fund to provide -work for women during the war : In the firm belief that prevention of distress is better than its relief, and that employment is better than charity. I have inaugurated "The Queen's Work for Wgmen Fund." Its object is to provide employment for as many as possible of the women of this country who have been thrown out of wort by the war. I appeal to the women of Great Britain to- help their less fortunate sisters through this fund. MARY R. To collect funds for,the work which forms the subject of the Queen's appeal a committee has been formed with the Queen as president, Lady Crewe as chairman, and Mrs. Pearson as hon. treasurer. Among the other members of the committee are Mrs. Asquith, Lady Askwith, Lady Bryce, Lady Derby, the Duchess of Wel- lington and Lady Rothinere. The activities of the committee will be de- voted to collecting funds to be spent on schemes devised by the Central Committee on llnem- odmoenna.tions, however small, plsuoymbsecnrtipAtimonosnganWd should be sent to Lady Roxburgh,, Foley House, 8, Portland Place, W. Lady Roxburgh is act- ingas hon. treasurer in Mrs. C. Arthur Pear- son's unavoidable temporary absence.</p>
      </div>
