      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Coroner and Jurymen. Prom the account of last week's inquest on the victim of a motor accident I infer that Coroner Brighouse generally CaIISPS more than " twelve good men and true" to be summoned for service on such occasions. Apparently, but for the circumstance that two of those who had been called to sit at the recent inquiry had meanwhile enlisted in the Army, the temporary deadlock which arose would have been easily avoided. I have known other coroners who habitually took the precaution to summon 14 or 15 jurors in order to avoid the possibility of such inconvenience as occurred here last week; one of these, indeed, almost invariably swore the lot, and once when I ventured to ask the reason of this, he explained that the plan was calculated to ensure unanimity every time—in other words, whenever one or two obstreperous dissentients threatened to make trouble, he was in a position to ignore them, being assured of the necessary round dozen of signatures to the verdict. Another whom I knew years ago re- quired the attendance of twelve only, and of course it happened now and again that the full number did not promptly appear. Once when that was the case I was called upon to fill the gap, and when the belated juror hastened in with a clumsy sort of excuse to the effect that a business engagement had detained him, he was sternly rebuked and fined. On the very next day, most unfortunately for himself, the Coroner was late at an inquest in the same district. He hurriedly muttered an apology in which he was understood to plead that his train had been delayed owing to races. Certainly, when the ' reports of these striking contrasts appeared under appropriate headlines in the week-end newspapers, the coroner did not shine particu- larly, and the penalised juryman must have felt to some extent compensated. A Lucky Prisoner. So the Germans in the Workhouse are on No. 2 diet. As I read it in the report of the Guardians' meeting, I thought of ray old friend the Cockney tramp. He had been telling me some "spike" experiences, and after mentioning how he encountered an organist among the casuals at one Workhouse, he went on to say there was also one in the Warwick "red jug," serving five years for forgery. "Ali, ah," said I. "so you have been in gaol, too, eh?" and he hastened to explain—" Well, yer see, it was like this: I was a-sittin' by the road-side a-gettin' me tea quitt's a lamb when a bloomin' copper kern along an' punched me can over, so I caught it before it was spilt, an' I up an' chucked it in 'is face. Wouldn' you ha' done the sime? 'Course I'd got to go along wiv 'im, an' I got a month. But I was all right, bless yer. When they arst me wot me tride woe, an' I arnser'd I woe a stownmison, they said I woe jeas the blowk they wos a-witin' for. I woe set to re- pair a wall wot had given wy, an' they woe so set up abaht it that I woe put on C diet strite avry—C diet, mind yer! They found me a I lybrer, an' the job larsted jess the month aht, an' I lived like a bloomin' fightin' cock all the while, no error."</p>
        <p>An Adventure in Holland. The story at the foot of last week's "Children's Corner" reminded me of an experience of two or three personal friends of my own at the con- clusion of a tour in Holland. They had barely allowed themselves tithe to reach their boat at Rotterdam, and, jumping on to the wrong tramcar, found themselves far from their objec- tive with only ten minutes or so to spare. Notic- ing that an inspector who boarded the car was wearing the five-pointed green star, one of the party accosted him in Esperanto and made known their quandary. He conducted them by a short cut through the docks, and so they were enabled to catch the boat. They have con- tinued to correspond with the friendly tram inspector, and through him have got into touch with other mesehers of the llotterdam group, one of whom has since been over on holiday, al- though bespoke no English. The same party,, by-the-bye, went to Paris for the Esperanto Congress which was to have been held there, but because of the outbreak of war were ad- vised to return at once, and had only 24 hours on so in the French capital. United Methodist Church. I suppose by this time the 173 ministers of this Church who are changing this year will have' settled in their new circuits, the rule being that they occupy their fresh pulpits on the last Sun- day in August. Quite one-third of the number are superintendents or have charge of single stations, the remainder being second or third ministers. By the way, a Yorkshireman of that denomination,who is on the local preachers'plan, set out for an appointment one Sunday morn- jag when another who was driving that way in- vited him to "a lift." The pair somehow got ' into a rather hot theological discussion, which the owner of the vehicle speedily and effectually ended by saying: If that's th' sort o' stuff thou'rt going to preach on yonder, get out and walk, thou'll be there soon enough." The Wesleyan " Flitting." • By the time this appears in print 800 Wes- leyan ministers will have removed to pastures new. The triennial system must cost the Con- nexion ,810,000 to £12,000 per annum in trans- ferring so many of these peripatetic preachers and their belonging e to and fro. It is the busi- ness of the circuit stewards and their wives to welcome them and make them comfortable in their new homes, and as a rule there is lavish hospitality at such times. The larder and not infrequently even the coal cellar are well sup- plied beforehand by the generous-hearted offi- cials, who are also required to get from a sani- tary expert a certificate that the drains are in perfect order. The railway companies"- agents eagerly tout for the conveyance of these minis- ters and their baggage. In the cases of some of the middle-aged married men, their olive: branches have so run over the wall that re-1 served compartments are placed at their dis- posal by the competing companies; nay, more - than one has been known to requisition a saloonl for their removal. The reference brings to my mind an amusing ' story which Sir Francis Lycett, the great Metho- dist benefactor, used to tell of his experience as , a circuit steward. At Highbury, they were about to take a third minister on to the circuit, and the steward was anxious to get the right man, so he instructed the superintendent to wire him from the conference when the appointment was made. The superintendent wired Sir Francis accordingly, and, to his amazement, received the message: "Twelve young sergeants," whereupon Sir Francis, who was very wroth, wired back, "We won't have him at any price." It turned out the clerk made an error in transmitting, and the message should have read, " Tweddle Young Sergeant." meaning the Revs. Wm. J. Tweddle, Robirt Newton Young, and W. Harlow Sar- geant—three foremost Methodist preachers. What really alarmed the circuit steward was the intimation, so he understood, that the third minister's name was Siargeant, and that he had twelve children. As the Highbury circuit paid ' a liberal children's allowance the steward trem- bled for the circuit exchequer, and "twelve young Sergeants" was a terrible prospect. , Not long ago I came across a Baptist minister with 14 children, but, of course, his conditions were different from those of his Methodist bre- thren; his stipend was "planked down" in the lump, so to express it, and he had everything to pay out of it, just like any ordinary mortal, so that he had experimental knowledge of the struggles and difficulties of his working class congregation. In Former Days. I have just had a dip into Wesleyan history, and I read that Wesley permitted the preachers to receive gifts of food and clothing, but at first no money from those they served. Hopper said, "In those days we had no provision made far preachers' wives, no funds, no stewards. He that had a staff might take it, go without, or stay at home." Of John Jane, who died in 1750, it is pathetically recorded that "all his clothes, linen and woollen, hat and wig; are not thought sufficient to answer the funeral charge, which is one pound, seventeen shillings, and three- pence; all the money he had was one shilling and fourpence." Two years later £l2 per annum was allowed to preachers for necessaries. Previously the society stewards supplied their bare needs and travelling expenses. Some circuits divided the love-feast money among the preachers, "which was very little indeed," says Miles, speaking of the Norwich Circuit. York ap- pealed to the Manchester Conference "against the large sum of .1;12 a year" for the preachers, as just named; but it was confirmed and made universal. In its records the quaint entry is also found—" 7s. Gd. for turning the assistant preacher's coat, and making it fit the second preacher." Now it is said the Wesleyans pro- vide everything for their ministers, "down to the dishcloths."</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER. ..„3 Conducted by UNCLE JACK. )c:2,</p>
        <p>My dear Children,— In these times we are all patriots, and that gives me the idea for our next competition. The subject is: "Why I should be loyal." I hope to receive a large number of interesting letters. The closing date is 26th September. This week we have to give a cordial welcome to the following new members:— HORACE HIMS WORTH and LILIAN HIMSWORTH, 15, Fitzherbert-street. EVELINE COTTON, 123, Forster-street. I Your loving • UNCLE JACK.</p>
      </div>
