      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>IN THE AGGREGATE.</head>
        <p>IN THE AGGREGATE.</p>
        <p>_ Sammy was not prone to over-exertion in the classroom; therefore his mother was both sur- prised and delighted when be came home one day with the announcement, "I got 100 this morning." " That's lovely, Sammy !" exclaimed his proud mother, and she kissed him tenderly. " What was it in-?" . . .</p>
        <p>-- ----. " Fifty in reading and fifty in 'rithmetie."</p>
        <p>RIDDLE. You all give me honour, And me you obey, I'm first in the battle, And lead all the way. But part of your freedom</p>
        <p>Take then from me, I'm last in the line, My slow step you see.</p>
        <p>A SAILOR'S CHANT. Ranzo was a sailor, 0 Ranzo, boys, 0 Ranzo; Ranzo was a sailor, 0 Ranzo, boys, 0 Ranzo.</p>
        <p>He shipped on board a whaler, 0 Ranzo, boys, 0 Ranzo; He shipped on board a whaler. 0 Ranzo, boys, 0 Ranzo.</p>
        <p>He died on Tuesday morning. 0 Ranzo, boys. 0 Ranzo; He died on Tuesday morning, 0 Ranzo, boys, 0 Ranzo.</p>
        <p>But well lower him down with a golden chain, 0 Ranzo, boys, 0 Ranzo; We'll lower him down with a golden chain, 0 Ranzo, boys, 0 Ranzo.</p>
        <p>A COUNTRY WALK. One afternoon I went for a walk round C—, a small country village. Starting out along the canal bank, which at the time I write of was very picturesque. I heard a chug-chugging, and on looking round I became aware that these sounds proceeded from a motor boat. I was greatly interested in the way in which it was lowered from the higher to the lower level. I crossed the canal at the locks, and turned up a nice quiet country lane. It was rather steep, but when I reached the top the magnificent panorama amply repaid me. Straight ahead I saw the roadside dotted with farms and cot- tages, while behind I law the silvery thread of water gleaming in the sunlight. Passing over a bridge the road led between hedges which nearly formed an arch overhead. The sun was shining out from a clear azure sky, and I was glad of the shade afforded by the hedge. The road hitherto had been deserted save for a toad or two, but now it began to get busy. 'The first person I met was an old farmer,' who asked what the time was. He was clad in striped grey and black tweed trousers and coat, and was mopping his forehead with a bright red duster, presumably a handkerchief. The next person I met was a boy on a bicycle, who nearly beheaded two fowls that ran in front of his wheel. Aft' •-1 the</p>
        <p>After passing a few more people I turned to right, and then to the left, and saw the targets where the Territorials practise shooting. In the distance I saw an old castle, the oldest inhabited one in Scotland. It is of peculiar construction. The windows are small. Round the top is a parapet with embrasures and loop- holes, suggestive of the troublesome times when the chiefs of the flans fought with one another. After going along the road for about half a mile I returned by another route to my starting point, passing a moor of heather on the way.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>CLAY-BRODIE.</p>
        <p>A pretty wedding was conducted by the Rev. Wm. Reid on Monday at St. John's Presby- terian Church, Wilderspool Causeway, when Miss Annie Thorburn Brodie, of Whitefield- road, Stockton Heath, second daughter of Mr. James Brodie, of Musselburgh. Scotland, be- came the wife of Mr. Walter Adnah Clay, son of Mr. George Clay, of Park-Buildings, War- rington.</p>
        <p>The bride, who was given away by her brother-in-law, Mr. George Crawford. of Ranee- gunge, Bengal, looked charming in a dress of satin charmeuse and crepe de chine, embroid- ered with pearl trimmings, lace and orange blossom. She carried a sheaf of lilies. The bridesmaid was Miss Bessie Brodie, sister of the bride. She wore a dress of tussure silk, trimmed with brown, and hat in pink and brown to match. Her bouquet was of pink carnations, and she wore a handsome brooch, the gift of the bridegroom. The duties of best man were discharged by Mr. G. E. Clay, of Greenmount. Gravesend, the brother of the bridegroom. After a reception had been held at the Winmarleigh Cafe. Mr. and Mrs. Clay left for Pwllheli, where the honeymoon is being spent. The bride's travel- ling costume was of navy blue, with black vel- vet hat, trimmed with ostrich feathers.</p>
        <p>. - Many useful aud valuable presents have been received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>BACKACHE NOT A DISEASE</head>
        <p>BACKACHE NOT A DISEASE</p>
        <p>But a Symptom, a Danger Signal Which Every Woman</p>
        <p>Should Heed.</p>
        <p>Backache in a woman is a symptom of organic weakness or derangement. If you have backache don't neglect it. To get permanent relief you must reach the root of the trouble. Read these words of a sufferer.</p>
        <p>"I suffered two years with female all. ,as very bad and I had a continual backache which was most dis• tressing. I could not stand on my feet but short time on ac- count of the pain in my back, and I would have such dragging sensations I could</p>
        <p>hardly bear it. I had soreness in each side, nd could not endure</p>
        <p>tight clothing. On ad- vice I took Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound and am enjoying good health. It is now more than two years and I have not had an ache or pain since. I do all my own work, and never have backache how. I think your medicine is excellent and I praise it to all my neighbour&amp; If You think my testimony will help others you may publish it." Write to-day for a free private book (ro women only) to the Lady Manager, the Lydia E. Pinkham Medicine Co., 47...1 Hampstead Road, London, N.W. For 2s. 9d. she will send you a bottle of the Lydia E. Pinkham Vegetable Compcund, or for 4s. 6d. a much larger bottle. Any chemist will supply it at these figures.</p>
