      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>THE</head>
        <p>THE</p>
        <p>COUNCIL SCENE.</p>
        <p>Lively Skirmish Over an Insurance Committee Appointment.</p>
        <p>" POLITICS " ALLEGED.</p>
        <p>There was a lively skirmish in the Warring-, ton Town Council on Tuesday evening. The " affray " arose over the recommendation of the General Purposes Committee to appoint Mrs. Mary Roberts, of 2, Kendrick-street, a well- known social worker, to the vacancy on the Insurance Committee occasioned by the resig- nation of Mrs. Simpson, who has left War- rington. Councillor CHRTMES set the ball a-rolling by moving the recommendation back for further consideration. He had nothing personal in the matter and his sole object was to protest against the method in which the vote had been taken in the committee. He had attended many meetings and conferences, and had never seen votes taken in the way they were upon the , three candidates before the General Purposes Committee.</p>
        <p>Councillor LASRFORD: It was quite unfair the way the voting was taken. Councillor ROBERTS was amazed at the oppo- sition now raised, but he could quite understand that it was now raised for political purpose!. The lady was very competent to fill the posi- tion.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR BROADHURST'S OUTBURST Councillor BROADHURST : This lady has been very autocratic. in her inquiries about the dependents of those now fighting. (A Voice: "Rot!") Councillor ROBERTS: Does Councillor Broad- hurst know this from his own knowledge, or has he heard it from some second person? If he will state a definite case I shall have it sifted to the very bottom. Councillor BROADHURST : It has been told me in my own house.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS: I say the whole opposi- tion has been arranged. Councillor BROADEIrRST : I resent that. If we worked politically we would be to-day a much stronger political party.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>WOULD NOT WITHDRAW.</head>
        <p>WOULD NOT WITHDRAW.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS said he was not going to withdraw. He knew that the Conservative members had been carefully and sedulously canvassed for the sole object of defeating the appointment of Mrs. Roberts. The whole opposition was for political and no other pur- pose. It was not right. For 20 years Mrs. Roberts had maintained herself respectably and honourably, and there was not one present who could bring one jot or tittle against Mrs. Roberts's character. (Applause.) _ _</p>
        <p>, She is not a blood relative of mine, added Councillor Roberts, and I think it is contemp- tible that such objections should now be made to her appointment. They are trivial and arel for political purposes, and if the business is carried on upon these political lines no candi- date can be successful, even if they get the , 'majority vote.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>LIVELY SCENE.</head>
        <p>A LIVELY SCENE.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON (warmly): I dispute the charge made by Councillor Roberts. .Every- body in this Council who does not agree with Councillor Roberts are liars.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS: You are a double-dyed one.</p>
        <p>Councillor PENtaurroN : You are!</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY MAvoa (Dr. Joseph, who presided in the absence of the Mayor) : Gentlemen, avoid</p>
        <p>personalities!</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : Then you must give me protection. I will not have his insults! +Councillor ROBERTS: You will have to. Councillor PEMBERTON : I will not. The DEPUTE MAYOR (ringing the hell): Order, order, please! Councillor PEMBERTON (excitedly): She sent a a letter asking for the position before we knew there was a vacancy.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR v. TOWN CLERK. The DEPUTY MAYOR: The Town Clerk says the vacancy was announced in the local papers. Councillor PEMBERTON : The Town Clerk has no right to say so, and The TOWN CLERK: I am not contradicting you, but Mrs. Simpson in June sent in her resignation to the Insurance Committee. It was accepted and this was stated in the papers. Councillor PEMBERTON : I say we did not know of it here.</p>
        <p>The Tows CLERK: I did not say you did. COB/1611OP PEMBERTON : You implied it. The TOWN CLERK (firmly): No, I did not. The DEPUTY MAYOR: I do not see any reason for this indignation on the part of Councillor Pemberton. I gave you the information I got from the Town Clerk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>STILL OBJECTING.</head>
        <p>STILL OBJECTING.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON :-I am objecting to the Town Clerk interfering. What has he got to do with it? The DEPUTY MAYOR: I asked the Town Clerk for information. Some of us may not have known the matter was in the papers. Councillor PEMBERTON : Councillor Roberts did not take part in the business of the General Pprposes Committee until he got up to propose this lady's appointment. Councillor GOUGH : You are wrong. He did not 'propose it. I did. (Laughter.) Councillor ROBERTS: I took no part in the debate in the committee until it was nearly finished. (A Voice: "That's right r) Councillor PEMBERTON : If there was any.poli. Heal move it was not on the Conservative side.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>"I FLATTER YOU."</head>
        <p>"I FLATTER YOU."</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSO.N interjected a remark that they had had enough from Councillor Pemberton to last a month.</p>
        <p>COUlleillOr PEMBERTON : We object to this ap- pointment. Councillor WILKINSON : Who are the "we"? Councillor PEMBERTON : Me and my friends think ---</p>
        <p>Councillor WiLKINSON : You are the one and they follow you. Councillor PEMBERTON : That is very flattering to Dl2.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON (sarcastically) : I say I do flatter you. The DEPUTY MAYOR : Order, order!</p>
        <p>SPELL CLIQUE.</p>
        <p>COURCiIIOP PEMBERTON Some respect should be paid to the public feeling outside. It is becoming the custom for a certain clique in the Council to co-opt whom they like. Councillor ROBERTS; Can Councillor Pember- ton spell "clique"? The DEPUTY MAYOR: This is not a spelling school.</p>
        <p>Councillor PUIBERTON I will leave you to spell it. Mrs. Roberts has been twice rejected. Councillor JENKINS said that the number of votes given to Mrs. Roberts was more than that given to Mrs. Hall. (Cries of " You have let the secret out," and laughter, during which the Councillor sat down.) On a vote, Councillor Chrimes' amendment was defeated by a considerable majority, and Mrs. Roberts's appointment was confirmed-.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Painting at the Rallis.</head>
        <p>Painting at the Rallis.</p>
        <p>Objection was taken at the meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday to the Town Hall. Parks and Baths Committee letting a contract for 129 4s. 3d. for Painting the central hall at the baths without the tenders' having been advertised.—Councillor Lashford moved that the tender he not accepted.—Councillor Pem- berton objected to that method of doing busi. ness, as it was against the Standing Orders.— The Borough Surveyor explained that the work WWI an addition to the • contract and it was thought that it was not necessary to advertise tor tenders.—On a vote. Councillor Lashford's amendment was defeated.</p>
        <p>A man's heart is frequently touched through sympathy, his purse through flattery.</p>
        <p>~ SATURDAY, SE</p>
        <p>INSURANCE WORK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>Success in Aiding "White Scourge"</head>
        <p>Success in Aiding "White Scourge"</p>
        <p>Vi_ctims.</p>
        <p>CONIREITTEE'S ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>Inter-mixed with a maw; of statistics in the annual report of the Warrington Insurance Committee, which has just been issued, are to be found many items of interest. For instance, we are told that from the inception of the com- mittee it was possible to give sanatorium treat- ment. In the first year there were 54 applica- tions for such treatment; last year the number had mounted up to 93. Every case received in- stitutional or domiciliary treatment. The average duration of institutional treat- ment of those discharged from the sanatorium daring the past twelve Months was 74 days, whilst the average cost per case amounted to .617 198. 5d., or 345. per week—the figure provi- sionally agreed upon between the Insurance Committee and the Health Committee. The longest period of institutional' treatment during the past twelve months was 136 days and the shortest 61 days, the latter figure not including nine cases which.left the sanatorium before the expiry of treatment, contrary to the recom- mendation of the medical adviser, and also ex- cluding the persons who died in the institution. The average waiting period before applicants were admitted to the sanatorium was 32 days, but it is stated that this period will be reduced when the proposed extension to the building has been completed.</p>
        <p>Extra nourishment was granted to 52 persons lunder domiciliary treatment during the last 1 twelve months, this taking the form of two pints of milk and two fresh eggs daily. Cost of Domiciliary Treatment. The average cost of domiciliary treatment (including panel doctors' fees, drugs, medicines, &amp;c.) for the six months ended 11th January, 1913, was 16s. 6d. per case, and for the twelve months ended 11th January, 1914, £ll Is. 6d. per case. The difference was due to the fact that for the first-named period the doctors were paid at the rate of 2s. 6d. per visit and ss. for each report, and for the last-named period the " domiciliary sixpence" came into operation, i.e., in consideration of a capitation fee of 6d. per head for the number of persons on his list, each doctor undertook to give whatever domi- ciliary treatment was required to any of his insured patients suffering from tuberculosis. The highest number of applications for treat- ment in any month was received in February this year, when 21 were submitted, and the lowest number was one in November last year. Where the Cases Came From. Orford Ward supplied 15 patients—the high- est number from a municipal division—two- thirds of whom were males. The returns for the other wards are as follow: Town Hall, Fairfield, St. Austin's and Latchford, eight each; Bewsey, ten; and Whitecross, St. John's and Howley, twelve each. Of this total, 13 were between the ages of 25 and 27, and a further 13 between the ages of 37 to 39. Twelve cases were between 22 and 24, whilst there was one between 53 and 60.</p>
        <p>Trades Affected.</p>
        <p>The following list gives occupations affected, with the number of cases: Black- smiths' strikers, 1; bookbinders, 3; carters 1; charwomen, 1; clerks, 1; crane-drivers, 1; fitters, 2; galvanisers, 4; gardeners, 1; grocers' assistants, 1; hawkers, 2; insurance agents, 1; iron puddlers, 3; ironworkers, 3; joiners, 1; labourers (general), 30; moulders, 3; painters, 3; printers, 1; rollers (mill), 1; saddlers, 1; shunters and brakesmen, 2; signal fitters, 1; taxi-drivers, 2; tubemakers, 1; typists, 1; van drivers, 1; velvet cutters, 2; weavers, 5; window cleaners, 1; wireworkers, 11; wool-sorters, 1.</p>
        <p>Disease Arrested. It is interesting to observe the results of the treatment. In seven cases the disease was apparently arrested; in eight institutional cases great improvement was effected, whilst eleven institutional (one advanced) cases and ten domiciliary eases were improved 80 much that they .were reported to be "fit for work." The disease was stated to be advancing in eight instances, and in seven the report reads "disease in statu quo." Death removed four patients from the sanatorium, and 16 who had been trelted at home. Only in one case at the sanatoriiim was the disease said to he advanc- ing on July 15 this year—the date the report ends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>Medical Benefit.</head>
        <p>Medical Benefit.</p>
        <p>The administration of this benefit is the most important work undertaken by the committee. The panel system and payment by capitationl fee was adopted in Warrington (says the report) as in all other areas throughout the kingdom, excepting Manchester and Salford, who adopted the system "payment per attendance." The fact that 27,250 insured persons have chosen their panel doctor or approved institution, and; that no complaints by insured persons against panel doctors have had to be considered by the Complaints (Medical Service) Committee, points to the satisfactory working of the system in Warrington, as does also the fact that only 196 persons asked to be transferred to other doctors at the close of last year. The number of doctors who came on the panel in January, 1913, was 19, and this was increased to 25 during the three months following. The whole of these 25 doctors stilj remain on the panel. In addition there are two approved in- stitutions in the town, viz.: The Warrington Amalgamated Friendly Societies' Medical Asso- ciation and the Post Office Medical System, the latter being open to Post Office servants only. The policy adopted in January, 1913, whereby each practitioner undertook to accept on his list all insured persons who presented them- selves for acceptance, has been adhered to up to the present, and has given general satisfac- tion.</p>
        <p>No Drug Deficiency in Warrington. For the supply of drugs, medicines, and ap- pliances to persons who selected panel Practi- tioners, a panel of 14 chemists, &amp;T., was formed, and this portion of the work also appears to have been carried out satisfactorily, inasmuch as no complaints have been received. In many areas throughout the country (more particu- larly in Lancashire) there was a deficiency in the drug fund and drug suspense fund to meet the chemists' accounts in full. Fortunately this did not apply so far as Warrington was concerned, there being a surplus of ~t7O in the drug. suspense fund after settlement of the chemists' accounts.</p>
        <p>Deposit Contributors.</p>
        <p>The number of persons who actually became deposit contributors proved to be considerably less, all over the country, than was anticipated. The first count of the Warrington register in April of last year revealed the fact, that only 339 had joined, and the highest number in the borough at any period during the past 18 months was 415.</p>
        <p>_ Some 185 sickness claims (from 76 male and 10 female contributors) had been dealt with by the committee, and a total of 1:70 18s. 2d. paid in respect of same. Although still incapa- citated, the sickness benefit of 36 contributors ceased, owing to the amount standing to their credit being exhausted. In respect of 5 sick- ness benefit claims, no payment could be certi- fied for, owing to the number of contributions paid by the applicants being less than 26.</p>
        <p>IPTE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>. -, . , .. . ..... , Jr, DISTRICT MEWS ::::1 ~...........__ .... _ ... ..,-,......t......, _</head>
        <p>. -, . , .. . ..... , Jr, DISTRICT MEWS ::::1 ~...........__ .... _ ... ..,-,......t......, _</p>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>The Council Offices.—The Lymm Urban Dis- trict Council have decided to place their offices at the disposal of the National Relief Com- mittee.</p>
        <p>New Gas Manager.—At a recent special meet- ing of the Lymm District Council, Mr. George West, of Broadbottom, was appointed gas manager.</p>
        <p>Members at the Council Meeting.—At the meeting of the Lymm Urban District Council on Monday evening there were present Councillors T. H. Warburton (chairman), Lockyer (vice- chairman), Gotobed, Ingham, Cowper, Critchley, Kershaw, Parker, Groves and Holt, and Messrs. W. Mullard (clerk), Mort (surveyor), and Barnes (water engineer).</p>
        <p>What Pricer—At the meeting of the Lymm District Council on Monday it was reported that at a recent meeting of the Roads Com- mittee the Surveyor produced a plan showing the building line round by the mill in the vil- lage, and Councillor Kershaw reported on the interview with Mr. Clarke, the agent for Sir William Lever. The committee unanimously agreed that the plan be approved, and the Clerk was instructed to write Mr. Clarke and ask what price and on what terms Sir William would sell the quantity of land for widening the corner as indicated on the plan produced by the Surveyor, The Council endorsed this.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>WHEN DISTRESS COMES.</head>
        <p>WHEN DISTRESS COMES.</p>
        <p>What the Lymm Council Intend To Do</p>
        <p>Steps are being taken to provide relief work</p>
        <p>in the Lymm district during the present crisis. At a recent meeting of the Roads Committee a letter was read from the County Council deal- ing with this matter, and it was decided that the Surveyor should confer with the County Surveyor at Chester and report at the next Council meeting. The report was given at the Monday evening meeting, when Mr. Mort said the County Sur- veyor was quite unable to give him any infor- mation beyond the fact that the Road Board would on the recommendation of the Local Government Board make grants towards ap- proved schemes in districts where distress occurred. It was imperative, however, that a list should be prepared at once, and after con- sulting with the Chairman of the Roads Com- mittee, Mr. Mort reported that he forwarded the following list to the County Surveyor, with the approximate amounts:—</p>
        <p>Local Schemes.</p>
        <p>No. 1. Main —Road, Section I.—Ditchfield Bridge, widening of roadway, exclusive of con- struction of bridge (this scheme has already been approved by the Road Board, A:2,500). No. 2. Main Road, Section lll.—Widening of Mill-lane and Burford-lane, near under-bridge, £5,000.</p>
        <p>No. 3. Widening dangerous corners at junc- tions of Brookfield-road and Eagle-brow, £2OO. No. 4. Widening dangerous corner at junc- tion of Church-road and Brookfield-road, £5O. No. 5. Widening dangerous corner of Lymm Mill, £l5O. _</p>
        <p>, No. 6. Taking up, breaking, ballasting and re-metalling about one mile of boulder paving in various parts of the district, -tl,OOO. This brought the total estimate to .g 8,900.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>Land to be Acquired.</head>
        <p>Land to be Acquired.</p>
        <p>All the schemes, with the exception of No. 6; necessitate the acquiring of land, and as re-. garde the widening of the four lane ends the excavations at this point would come in very useful for filling in at the old mill if the schemes could work together. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The Council...liad a -plan for widening Mill- lane to 3'6ft., but. the County Surveyor thought the width hardly sufficient. If any of the sug- gested widenings were to be included in appli- cations for Road Board grants next year nego- tiations as to the purchase of the necessary land must he put in at once.</p>
        <p>The Council gave the Surveyor instructions to make the necessary arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>TO PREVENT CONTAMINATION OF WATER.</head>
        <p>TO PREVENT CONTAMINATION OF WATER.</p>
        <p>All Eyes on the Waterworks.</p>
        <p>COUNCIL'S ACTION.</p>
        <p>_ see that your water supply is not contaminated by the Germans. There is the danger, and the local councillors, realising it, have taken steps to minimise the risk. At their meeting in the Council Offices on Monday a letter was read from Major P. Mal- colm, the chief constable of Cheshire, in reply to a communication from the Council. "I beg to inform you," wrote the Chief Constable, " that if the management of the waterworks will select watchmen for the protection of their property the Superintendent of the division will take steps that the men selected shall be visited periodically during the day, and at fre- quent intervals during the night, by one of the regular constables, who, I may add are already paying attention to the matter.</p>
        <p>Every Necessary Step Taken. "As regards the enrolment of suitable per- sons to protect the waterworks and private pro- perty in the district, I trust that the Council will accept my assurance that every possible step that is necessary has been taken and is being taken. Names of several thousand men all willing to act in emergency have been regis- tered in the county, and these men, together with those who may hereafter volunteer, will, in the course of a few days, be organised into parties or companies, with local leaders, under the supervision of the Superintendent of each division.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>No Undue Haste.</head>
        <p>No Undue Haste.</p>
        <p>"I deprecate any TMC-I;rial;a;iein this organisa- tion," concluded the letter, "as it is liable to create unnecessary alarm and defeat the object in view."</p>
        <p>The Clerk also read a letter from Supt. Suther- land, who wrote: "In view of the possibility, however remote, of an attempt being made by alien enemies to interfere with the water supply, it would be advisable, in my opinion, for your Council to place one or two watchmen on the waterworks at night during the present crisis. You will understand that the police are and will be fully occupied, and cannot give to every point the attention they would wish. but they would keep in touch with your watchmen and visit them occasionally during the night."</p>
        <p>BOY SCOUTS OFFER THEIR SERVICES. A letter from Mr. W. G. Jones on behalf of the Heatley Boy Scouts was also read. It stated that the troop was "ready and willing to offer their services in any way they could." They would do all they possibly could, and their tent and ground sheets were at the disposal of the Council. Councillor Gotobed said he had been ap- proached by a good many ratepayers with regard to the ater question, and these ratepayers viewed it in a very serious light. They had alien enemies in their midst, and many rate- payers felt it would be quite easy for the water supply to be contaminated, and thus do a great deal of damage. It seemed to him to be im- perative to guard the waterworks without delay —whatever be the nature of the guard. They had no special constables in the district—at least he had heard of none—and they had no watchmen, so naturally some arrangement should be made. He moved that watchmen be placed on the works pending futther arrange- ments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>LYMM OUGHT TO HAVE SPECIALS.</head>
        <p>LYMM OUGHT TO HAVE SPECIALS.</p>
        <p>-- Councillor Cowper said they had special con- stables in other places, and ought to have them in Lymm. He was willing to be one, and take his, turn on night d,uty. Some corner boys from Warrington came to his house, but his language sent them away, but they went to a lady's house and demanded food. The Chairman (Councillor Warburton): I take it it is not with regard to molesting people, but just as a protection for the waterworks. We have had an offer from the Boy Scouts, who could guard during the day time. Their tent could also be used by those on night duty. Mr.</p>
        <p>Farrar has some men who have made an offer for night duty.</p>
        <p>NOT AN EASY MATTER.</p>
        <p>Councillor Groves said he would not like it to go forth through the Press that it was a very easy matter to contaminate their water. It was not, and he would like that to go forth. Councillor Gotobed : It is a matter of opinion. Councillor Groves intimated that it woula take a lot of trouble. A Member: Not very much. . _</p>
        <p>Councillor Gotobed said just as well to face the truth. It was no use hiding things. They would have to face the position manfully. Councillor Groves said the statement was in- viting aliens to contaminate the water. Councillor Gotobed: It is inviting us to do the right thing. Councillor Parker seconded Councillor Goto- bed's resolution.</p>
        <p>- - - Councillor Kershaw said it was useless having one man. He must have help of some sort, and they would not have to be on duty too long. Councillor Gotobed : My resolution says "watchmen." Councillor Kershaw, continuing, said the ques- tion was were they empowered to pay watchmen? With regard to the Boy Scouts, they were very worthy chaps, but not very much good. It Nyas certainly a job for a man. The Clerk intimated that they had the power to pay. The Chairman said that one man would not be a halfpennyworth of use, because there was the tower, the reservoir and the machinery, all of which would want looking after.</p>
        <p>Councillor Kershaw: Yes:. It was ultimately decided to obtain watchmen for the night and the Boy Scouts during the day time, and the Clerk was instructed to write to the Superintendent of the division making in- quiries when the special constables would be ready.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>NO BATHING IN. DAN!. Lymm Council's Decision. "BATHING PROHIBITED."</head>
        <p>NO BATHING IN. DAN!. Lymm Council's Decision. "BATHING PROHIBITED."</p>
        <p>This is the order with reference to the Dam— for this year, at any rate. The matter was again discussed at the meet- ing of the Lymm Urban District Council on Monday, when Councillor Kershaw moved that the resolution as to bathing in the Dam be rescinded.</p>
        <p>He thought it was the opinion of most of them that it was undesirable to allow the bathing before eight o'clock in the morning, or at any other time. He believed the resolution allow- ing it was rather rushed. His reason for bring- ing the matter forward was the extreme danger owing to the sloping banks, and from the fact that there was no supervision, and, further, be- cause they had no dressing accommodation. It was advantageous for young people to bathe and swim, and the Council looked forward to the day when they could allow it, but the time was not yet. Councillor Groves: As one of the opponents of bathing I have very great pleasure in seconding this proposal. Councillor Gotobed : As one of those in favour of bathing I also support Mr. Kershaw. It is near the end of the season and bathers will not lose mech. I shall not be content until the Council provide some sort of accommodation for the bathers in this district.</p>
        <p>Councillor Cowper, in supporting. said they would all be delighted when they could find them with mixed bathing. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>This was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>IWFOC.</head>
        <p>IWFOC.</p>
        <p>!KTON_ HEATH</p>
        <p>Special Constables.—Nine special constables have been sworn in to help the Stockton Heath police should the necessity arise. Their work will chiefly be the guarding of railways and bridges.</p>
        <p>War Stops Show.—The autumn flower show of the Stockton Heath Garden Society has been abandoned on account Of the war.</p>
        <p>A Civilian Guard.—lt is proposed to form a Civilian Guard for the protection of Stockton Heath. Councillor Mclntyre, chairman of the Parish Council, has called a special parish meeting to be held in the Church Room on Monday evening. The objects are to form a Civilian Guard, and to encourage recruiting in Kitchener's Second Army.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>WAR FUND. A Steady Sum Being Raised.</head>
        <p>THE WAR FUND. A Steady Sum Being Raised.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath, Latchford Without and Wal- ton Inferior has been formed a sub-area of the rural area of Runcorn for, the raising of money to the Prince of Wales' War Fund, and the following committee has been appointed : Messrs. George Pilling (chairman), John Hal- lows, William Weir, Thomas J. Lee, H. J. West- brook, Walter Banks, John McDerrnid, John H. Fletcher, Joseph H. Clarke, Fred Pearson, W. Bainbridge, William Silcock, James Richardson, William Roberts, the Rev. J. Collins, the Rev. G. Curtis, and Mr. William Mclntyre (lion. secretary). It is hoped all will help, and it is also desired that where practicable weekly sub- scriptions should be collected in works and establishments where labour is employed, and any assistance in this direction will be appreciated by the committee. Tile committee hope that necessitous cases will be reported to any of the members who will bring forward any applications for relief, which will be fully investigated by an Executive Commit- tee and dealt with at the earliest possible moment- •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>SUBSCRIPTION LIST.</head>
        <p>SUBSCRIPTION LIST.</p>
        <p>The following is a list of Tl;;;ubseriptions already paid or promised :—Stockton Heath Forge (Caldwells Limited). 1:25; Messrs. Peter Caldwell, .40; W. Bainbridge. £5; J. H. Clarke, .25; T. J. Lee, 1;5; George Pilling, .65; J. 11. Fletcher, ; Fred Pearson, .12 25.; William Mclntyre, ~t 2 25.; William Roberts, IA Is.; Wil- liam Sileock, Is.; W. Banks, ..t1 is.; Fred Broady, Is.; W. A. Timperley. ..t1 Is.; John Hallows, 10s. 6d.; Jonathan W. Welsby, las.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Wife As Deputy.—At the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday, Frederick Thompson, roadman, Bank Gardens, Penketh, was fined 10s. and costs, with the alternative of 14 days, for being drunk and disorderly. He was represented by his wife.</p>
        <p>A Successful Student.—Miss Dorothy Higham, the eldest daughter of Mr. William lligham,"of Farnworth-road, Penketh, has obtained a well- merited honour by winning the Junior Oxford certificate. She was a former pupil of Arpley- street School, Warrington, where she won a scholarship, but through residential/ reasons was unable to claim it. At present she is going through a ,three years' course at Widnes Sec- ondary School. ••,..v mr—VX•WSINIPMS Kitchener's Young Men.—The young men of Penketh have awakened to their country's need and have joined various branches of Kitchener's Army. Several have gone to Chatham for the Engineers, some to Preston as Field Artillery- men, and the rest to Edinburgh as members of the Lancashire Regiment. The Penketh Tan- ning Company gave their employes to under- stand that in the event of any members being called upon for military service their names would remain upon the firm's books, and their places kept open for them upon their return. A I scheme is being drawn up to assist the families of such as occasion necessitates.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Pastor's Farewell.—The Rev. Harry Middlewood on Sunday preached his last sermon at the Wesleyan Chapel before a good congrega- tion, who came to bid him farewell before his departure to Tavistock, in Devonshire. His sermon was taken from Acts xxvi. 29: "I would to God that not only thou, but also they that hear me this day were both almost and alto- gether such as I am, except these bonds."—At a meeting arranged the previous week the chapel stewards, on behalf of the chapel members, presented Mr. Middlewood with a suit case, and Ml.s.,Middlewood with a case of fish knives and forks.—Their departure is generally .regretted, for during their term of office they have en- deared themselves to the people.</p>
