        <p>he Iva ed Families. te tip:44l4e many families owing to mar- t.Cii or land a birth, and most the kllsee of Europe are affected in this aktile liaiser is first cousin to King George, nezarina, Whose only brother is the B 4 of e }lease, and presumably nowefight,,- N, cierrnan Empire. The Queen of Pged of p ari Belgium was before her,mar- Princess. The only surviving 'Boerr,,,nlcess Christian, who lost one son in "Tair, is heir to the Duchy of Schleswig iS a major in the Prussian Army; il-ieen called upon to fight against th 'thet The Duchess of Albany's With Prince of Waldeck-Prymont, is also FYI ton the army of the Kaiser, and her - kNlt' R the reigning Duke of Saxe-Coburg give his aid to the German forces. ICOViI "is our Princess Alexander of LleatiPe husband is going to the front. Tl Ihter Illirleek, married to the Kaiser's only anwiother British-born prince fighting Zialliltia„in' Many other families find them- -4'4 cf ' saki position, and instances are re- fighting against sons, whilst :111101a of many British-born women are _l'4l" of the German or Austrian forces.</p>
        <p>probable that the Austrian velours hats for autumn and winter wear will not be so popular this year. In shape these hats are mc,t becom- ing, as well as neat and smart, and large quan- tities were sent over before the war broke out. They are likely to be offered at cheap prices, owing to the boycotting to which they will be subjected. Most of our big houses had also made their selection of French dress goods before the bolt from the blue, with its dislocat- ing effects on trade and everyday life. War has always affected women's dress, but as yet it chiefly shows itself in a certain restraint in keeping with the general feeling of the gravity of passing events. There is, however, no reason why women should cease to be smart, and fortu- nately present modes are rather helpful than otherwise to inexpensive dressing.</p>
        <p>"Nothing German." Women do not fight in battle, but they can do their part in numberless ways at home. One of these consists of husbanding resources by practising a wise economy in their households, and supporting British industries in preference to buying foreign goods. The Germans have had many advantages over this country, for when laying down machinery and plant they were able to avail themselves of the newest labour-saving devices. Many British firms have not been in a position to discard old machinery and to set up the new required for keeping in the van of commerce. It seems that the country is now being awakened to the opportunities the war affords of entering the markets from which it has been driven, and women can help in this matter more than the other sex, because they do the major part of the shopping where-food, dress and household requisites are concerned.</p>
        <p>The Pique Hat. The white hat seems to be more popular than ever, and in pique is undeniably the smartest wear with navy serge, suiting, or covert coating tailor-mades. At the seaside and in the country it is most simply trimmed and sailor in shape. A deep cross-way band of pique round the crown and an unpretentious mount is the usual trim- ming. Sometimes a cluster of small apples in black silk is placed at one side in preference to a mount. The shape is generally worn at an angle and well over the face. A new bat of</p>
