      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>MEMS. FOR MOTHERS.</head>
        <p>MEMS. FOR MOTHERS.</p>
        <p>The Children's Lessons.</p>
        <p>, Until a definite arrangement is made regard- ing the present occupation of our schools, I would like to suggest that parents can do a great deal by preserving some kind of discipline among the children, who appear at this time to be running wild, and getting beyond all bounds of order. Without wishing to give offence, it would be well for mothers in the more crowded districts to be a little more strict in the way of cleanliness. The unusual feeling of excitement should be no excuse for dirt and neglect, and during some part of each day boys and girls who would otherwise: be at school should be kept within certain bounds and be encouraged to behave well, and to devote at teat an hour to quiet reading or study of some kind. Before the long holidays were over, the chil- dren had become weary of so many weeks of idleness, and that would have been the time for parents to arrange some better plan than the present state of things. The children, from the date when school usually opens, might be washed and dressed with the same care, and a simple lesson or two should be regarded as a regular thing; all could have been done without any trouble or friction, and the little ones would glide naturally into ways of order and good con- duct.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>Sold everywhere in</p>
        <p>Pries WI (36 pills)a, 219(168 pills).</p>
        <p>Warrington v. Newton-le-Willows.</p>
        <p>Played at Warrington on Saturday. Scores: Warrington.—H. G. Hodgson b Harrison 7, H. Hawthorn b 'Lindley 6, G. Woodcock run out 4, Hannam run out 0, B. Silcock b Waywell 53, Rev. J. M. Buckmaster h Cooke 3, S. L. Par- tridge b Cooke 2, T. Wright b Cooke 50, Blomley not out 0, W. Richardson and W. Bennett did not bat, extras 2; total (for eight wickets) 127. Newton-le-Willows.—W. Dudgeon b Hannam 1, F. Harrison b Hannam 0, H. Waywell c Rich- ardson b Bennett 1, Marsh (T.) b Hannam 2, C. A. Bodner b Hannam 1, T. Cook b Hannam 0, J. H. Lyons c Silcock b Bennett 0, A. Smart c Woodcock b Hannam 13, H. E. Daniel b Han- nam 0, C. W. Hindley b Bennett 8, T. Wilkin- son not out 3, extras 2; total 31.</p>
        <p>Warrington 2nd v. Newton 2nd. Played at Newton-le-Willows on Saturday. Scores:— _</p>
        <p>Newton-le-Willows.-H. Braddock c Holbrook b Turton 16, G. Appleton c Howard b Entwistle 12, F. Bowyer Howard 18, R. Bradshaw c Howard b Whitlow 8, H. Martin lbw b Howard 0, 11. Beaman b Entwistle 7, E. Alldred b How- ard 7, H. Butters b Howard 0, M. Sutton at Holbrook b Entwistle 3, H. Gee b Entwistle 0, D. McCawley not out 0, extras 6; total 77. Warrington.-H. Partridge b McCawley 24, A. D. He c Butters b Sutton 46, G. Whitlow b Appleton 43, J. Mclntyre c Butters b Sutton 4, T. Williamson not out 26, W. V. Turton Butters b Bradshaw 1, W. Howard at Martin b Alldred 13, W. Entwistle 1) Alldred 8, extras 4; total (for seven wickets) 169. • "SPOONER " CUP CONTEST. Conservatives v. Rylands'. On Rylands' ground on Saturday. Scores :- Conservatives.-F. Ritchie b McGowan 3, W. Hunter c McGowan 1) Waywell 3, J. Constable b McGowan 13, F. Summers b Carter 14, B. Green b Jolley 5, W. Bell b Jolley 1, J. Haywood c Waywell b Carter 20, F. It C. Tole lbw b H. Waywell 1, F. Lemon run out 17, W. Brooks not out 11, W. Barrow b Jolley 5, extras 4; total 97. Rylands'.-E. Sanders b H. Green 6, H. Bram- hall b Green 0, G. McGowan b Green 0, J. Way- well c Tole b Green 9, S. Carter b Bell 6, T. Horrocks c and b Barrow 3, F. Jolley c Hay- wood b 11. Barrow 2, T. Newton b Barrow 0, T. Knight b Bell 1, G. Williams not out 3, H. Booth retired 0, extras 6; total 36.</p>
