      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>44. 4,4 +0 OMb</head>
        <p>44. 4,4 +0 OMb</p>
        <p>.. • 4fr</p>
        <p>coarse straw, with a dull surface is of a curious shade that resembles nothing so much as newly- sawn wood. The lining of the brim is velvet and straw, the shape close fitting, and the trim- ming generally of soft feathers of a tale brown shade, finishing in a high mount at one side. Giant cocks' tails are seen on some of the autumn hats, and very handsome they look. Shot straws in dark contrasts, such as crimson and black, brown and orange, have been pre- pared for early autumn wear.</p>
        <p>Lawn and Linen Blouses. Stripes are still in favour, and are taking the Place of checks, with plain coats for autumn tailor-mades. The colours are softer in suit- ings, cloths, &amp;c., than in summer materials, the coat being generally of the predominant shade used in the stripe. Beige always makes its ap- pearance in the early autumn, and this year is no exception. It is a useful colour that goes very well with a number of furs, and can be worn well into the winter. Linen and lawn blouses will be used with tailored suits, even during the cold weather. The contrasting sleeves in the case of evening blouses will be seen throughout the autumn, and makes for economy, since it permits of the use of short lengths of materials secured during the sales. For shirts and plain blouses, sleeves will be modelled in one with the body, or they may be set into a deep armhole, with a clear shoulder line.</p>
        <p>Major Richardson's Bloodhounes. Four very clever bloodhounds have been taken to the front by Major Richardson, their master and trainer. When he left the Army, this officer took up the breeding of dogs as a hobby, and beccaning much struck by the animals he saw attached to the French regiments, determined to turn his attention to training his dogs for 1 similar work. Two of ,his collies went to Russia at the time of the Russo-Japanese War, and ren- dered such valuable service that the Czar pre- sented the Major with a war medal and a mag- nificent watch and chain. Our authorities evinced for some years an utter indifference to the value of these dogs in time of war, but of late have shown a disposition to avail them- selves of their intelligent devotion to duty, which almost equals that of human beings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH. Dangers Caused by Flies.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH. Dangers Caused by Flies.</p>
        <p>Common house flies are well known to be car- riers of disease germs. The presence of large numbers of flies indicates filth in the vicinity. Their chief breeding-places are accumulations of manure, but decaying vegetables or animal materials and filth of any kind also afford them breeding-places. Their food, however, is not limited to filth, and it is this reason which ren- ders them dangerous, for after feeding on garb- age of the most offensive description, tliewill convey that filth to foodstuffs used by human beings, and so contaminte that food. The mis- chief done by flies is not confined merely to the conveyance of filth; they also carry disease germs from persons suffering from infectious disease to healthy persons. Flies are specially attracted to patients suffer- ing from infectious sickness. To avoid danger all house refuse should be burnt or placed in proper receptacles if kept covered. These recep- tacles should be frequently emptied completely. Food of all descriptions should be carefully screened with muslin or wire covers. Keep all rooms, especially the kitchen, free from dirt. Keep flies away from food, and kill every fly in the house; his body is covered with disease germs. Sinks and gullies should be well flushed with water, and the drainage system kept in good order. Washing round with Jeyes' fluid is a great help.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>I N.U.INTERMEDIATES. 1</head>
        <p>I N.U.INTERMEDIATES. 1</p>
        <p>The New League Starts Operations on 19th September.</p>
        <p>YOUNG PLAYERS KEEN.</p>
        <p>" EXAMINER " SPECIAL.</p>
        <p>It is with a marked amount of keenness that the members of the Intermediate N.C. League anticipate the opening of the season, which pro- mises to produce quite a series of interesting games. The date of commencing the matches is the 19th of September, and already the players have. gone into practice. The League is composed of 17 teams from 13 clubs. Owing to a shortage of grounds, each the tomW'n ill ipslarey'plre6 represented tcehebsyonolne or more teams,.. Every part of and great rivalry is anticipated and many "Derby" matches will be played . Heathside Bewsey 0.8. will look forward with delight to their encounter with Wycliffe 0.8. St. James' 0.8. are now talking of re- venging their 1911 defeat at the hands of Bolton Council 0.8. Parochial and St. Mary's have ever been keen rivals, and the same may be said of Heathside Frogliall and St. Barnabas'. Most of the matches will be played at Victoria Park, where four Rugby fields are now being marked out. Last Saturday the Park presented an animated appearance; no less than ten teams were enjoying a robust, healthful game of Rugby..</p>
        <p>„ A match will be played at Wilderspool each week, and the boys will no doubt give of their best when playing on the historic sod on which their seniors have played such notable games.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>GAME SHOULD GO ON.</head>
        <p>GAME SHOULD GO ON.</p>
        <p>_ Whatever opinions may be expressed about the advisability of abandoning senior football, I think all will agree that the Intermediate League should go on, and that the boys should be encouraged to "play the game." If the boys can learn to play the game on the "Rugger" field they will do so when their turn comes in the workshop, and even on the battlefield. The League Committee has an enthusiastic chairman in Councillor E. Osborne, who, with Mr. T. Barnes, represents the Warrington R.F.C. The Schoolboys' Rugby League is re- presented by Messrs. S. J. Price and E. Hawkes- worth, and each competing team has one repre- sentative on the committee. The Mayor (Alderman P. Peacock) has been asked to become the first president, and several local gentlemen have been invited to become vice-presidents..</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Malcolm tHeathside) is the honorary treasurer, and Mr. E. Jackson Mee (St. James') the honorary secretary. The committee are'looking forward to a suc- cessful season and ask for the support of all Rugby enthusiasts. E. J. M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>BOWLING:-</head>
        <p>BOWLING:-</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>Rylands' Recreation V. Parish Church. 'Played at Gorsey-lane, and resulting in a win for the Parish Church by the • narrow margin of one point. Scores, Parish Church names first :-- R. W. Antwis (captain), 11; W. Dutton, 21. Walter Dennett, 21; C. Taylor, 20. S. Tilling, 21; B. Jolley, 17. E. Baston, 21; W. Gallop, 16. R. Done, 4; J. Meakin, 21. T„ Chidlaw, 13; W. S. Barker, 21. T. J. Hopkins, 21; A. Duckworth, 13. Substitute, 21; H. Farrer, 13. T. Wilkinson, 20; J. Mulvey, 21. J. R. Squire, 21; T. Plevin, 10. Total—Parish Church, 174; Rylands', 173.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>SEPTEMBER 3 , 1914.</p>
        <p>THE ADVENT OF FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>N.U. PLAYERS AT WORK.</p>
        <p>The Practice Game Promises Well for the Team. LEIGH MATCH SHOULD BE WON.</p>
        <p>BY "SCRUM."</p>
        <p>There was plenty of hustle in the practice game played at Wilderspool on -Saturday, and if combination was not much in evidence its absence was due more to the hustling tactics than to the lack of ability to combine. Clock- work passing is never expected in a trial match —it will come once the men shake down for the season's game. It was a ding-dong engage- ment, and in the end the second side, which was composed mainly of "A" men, were routed, but the score—well, there is no need to divulge it.</p>
        <p>First honours fell to the "A" men, who opened with splendid dash and gave Jolley a hot time before the first try was forthcoming. Then the Seniors took matters in hand, and the "A's" had to fight on the defensive the greater part of the time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>"VERY SATISFACTORY."</head>
        <p>"VERY SATISFACTORY."</p>
        <p>To use a war phrase, the wio.;li.o.r; both sides was "very satisfactory." The Senior side may not have been so strong as what it was last season, but the material is there, and with a few good, stiff games it should be soon licked into shape. The war has been responsible for the withdrawing of some well-known players, the forward and half-back departments suffer- ing the most. Despite this, Warrington should be able to turn out a couple of sides capable of holding their own with any teams they may meet, either in the League or the Combination. The side which played for the,Seniors was : Jolley; Baker, Johnson, Brooks and Law; Young and Dukes; Thomas, Tranter, Fearnley, Critchley and Green. The "A" team was composed of more than the usual thirteen and consisted of: Webster; Taberner, Dainteth, Hammond and• Cherrett; A. Brown and Allison; Harmer, Pouncey, Bevan, Logan, Mather, Murphy, Thornton and Worrall.</p>
        <p>As will be seen, many new men figure in the club list, and at the close of the game. I learn, all of these signed on.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>FIRST PLAYERS.</head>
        <p>THE FIRST PLAYERS.</p>
        <p>_ Coming to the players individually, I think Jolley is showing quite as good form as in the past, and only wants a game or two to time him up to concert pitch. I expect there will have to be some experiments made with the threequarter line. Brookes was, if anything, the smarter man in that quartet. Law does not seem to have shaken down yet, but when he does he and Brookes ought to make a very useful couple. The other pair were hard work- ers, but did not impress one so much as did the Brookes-Law wing..</p>
        <p>Young is scrum worker and he gave a good display in that position, but he wants a quick man as partner. The forwards did well, al- though Chesters and Skelhorne, now on active service, were missed. Clare was an absentee, but the Thelwall lad, who did so well last season, is expected soon to take his old place in the forward rank. Thomas is as "weighty" as ever. Tranter and Fearnley can "foot it" in the old style, and Green and Critchley seem worthy of their positions. Of the "A" men one cannot say much, except that they will give a good account of themselves in the Combination games.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>LEIGH MATCH TO-DAY. 'arrington Send a Strong Team.</head>
        <p>THE LEIGH MATCH TO-DAY. 'arrington Send a Strong Team.</p>
        <p>All circumstances considered, Warrington are sending a strong team through to Leigh this afternoon to play the opening fixture of the season. In playing personnel there has not been such a great change in the first side as it was at one time feared the exigencies of was would occasion.</p>
        <p>'Of last season's team Nicholas, Chesters and Skelhorne are the most notable absentees. All are on active service, Nicholas with the Welsh Territorials, and Skelhorne with our local lads now in Scotland, while Chesters was last heard of at Devonport. To fill their places, Dainteth, 'Planter and Critchley have come into the senior side, and thus the team is very similar to that which did such good work last season. I am hopeful that they will still do better in the season which lies before us. The selected team ought to give a good account of them- selves, and I am hopeful to see them return home to-night with their two first League points. CALIBRE OF THE TEAM.</p>
        <p>Taken all round, the side is one of well- balanced calibre, and should in this respect be quite equal to the majority of the N.U. teams this season. Jolley, doubtless, will be as sure at back as ever. The threequarter line looks a powerful quartet, and I am hopeful of seeing good work by them. Waywell is in his old position, and should find Brookes a sound part- ner. Laws, the newcomer, is inside man to Baker, and this wing should give us home dashing play. Young, who will work the scrum, and Pain- teth, as stand-off, ought to render useful ser- vice at half-back, while I expect the forwards to give us plenty of their characteristic foot- work.</p>
        <p>Brookes is this season's captain and he should be able to get the best out of his men. Jolley figures in the position of vice-captain. A special train will leave Bank Quay to-day at 2.15, arriving at Leigh at 2.50. The excursion returns at six o'clock, and the return fare is is., so a good crowd should accompany the team.</p>
        <p>The men will line out at 3.30 as follows:</p>
        <p>Warrington. Jolley. Waywell, 1' ,okes, Laws, Baker. You Dainteth. Cox, Clare, Few nley, Tranter, Thomas, Critchley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>Cartwright,</head>
        <p>Cartwright,</p>
        <p>Coffey, Darwell, Winstanley, Boardman, Marsh, _Cain, Booth. Roberts, Keegan, S. Johnson, T. Johnson.</p>
        <p>Clarkson. Leigh.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A" v. LEIGH "A." The Opening Match at Wilderspool.</p>
        <p>- - Warrington "A" men should give a good account of themselves against the Leigh "A" side at Wilderspool this afternoon, and I am anticipating that they also will gain two Com- bination points. There are several new faces in the team and the newcomers arrive with a good reputation as junior players. The kick- off is timed for 3.30 and the men will line out as follows:</p>
        <p>Warrington.—Bramliall; Taberner, Nichols, Hammond, and Cherrett ; A. Brown and Alli- son; Harmer, Bevan, Green, Mather, Thornton, and Pouncey.</p>
        <p>Leigh.—Hilton; Ainsworth, Higham, Carter, and Donohue; O'Neil and Clarke; Gallop, Jones, Gatheridge, Grant, Hurst, and Dooley.</p>
        <p>TEAM FOR HUDDERSFIELD.</p>
        <p>Warrington are due at Huddersfield on Mon- day, when they play their annual , match, which, owing to the Feast Holidays; always attracts a great crowd. The Warrington Side will be selected after the Leigh match. The train leaves Bank Quay at 10.40 on Monday morning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>SOCCER GAME</head>
        <p>THE SOCCER GAME</p>
        <p>War Calls Depleting the Junior Clubs.</p>
        <p>A START MADE TO-DAY. BY "LINESMAN."</p>
        <p>On Tuesday the football season of 1914-15 was ushered in by the English and Southern Foot- ball League Clubs, but it is the first Saturday of the month of September that is generally accepted as the opening day of the season, and to-day the willow will have to be laid aside to allow the big ball to take its place in the field for the winter game, and our local League will commence oh its term of office.</p>
        <p>Owing to the present war crisis many of the local ,clubs find some difficulty in organising a team, and in a few instances clubs will have to cancel their membership owing to their mem- bers having joined the Army. At the time of writing there has not been anything definitely settled respecting the First Division of the League, and it is being kept open for late ap- plications, but the Second League is completed and the fixtures arranged, and some of the clubs are engaged to-day. The membership shows an increase on last season, and covers a wider area. It is comprised of the following clubs: St. Bar- nahas', Bank Quay Recs., Fairfield A.F.C., Fairfield United, Earlestown St. John's Mission, Earlestown Y.M.C.A., Newton Congregational- ist, Hollins Green, Elmwood Avenue Cons., Pad- dington, Latchford Baptists and Orford White Star. ,</p>
        <p>It will be seen by the above list that there are only four of last season's members again compet- ing. Warrington Excelsior (last season's win- ners) have disbanded for several reasons, and Orford St. John's have joined the First Division Amongst the new members, the St. John's Mis- sion. Club were members of the St. Helens Sun- day School League last season, and occupied the fifth position, and should do well in the competi- tion. The Y.M.C.A. paid several visits last season amongst our local clubs, and created a good impression by their displays and the New- ton Cons. arid Hollins Green had good records last season. The remaining clubs are well known locally, and form the nucleus of what should prove to be a strong combination of clubs and provide some keen and interesting games for their supporters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>ST. BARNABAS.'</head>
        <p>ST. BARNABAS.'</p>
        <p>At the genera-1 meeting of the above club the following were elected as officers for the corn- ing season: Patrons, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Mr. A. H. Crosfield and Capt. G. Crosfield; presi- dent, Mr. A. Shuttleworth; treasurer, Mr. J. W. Hind; hon. secretary, W. T. Shaw, 311, Liver- pool-road, Warrington; with a committee of nine. The secretary reported the club to be in a very satisfactory position, and last season in the League they played 13 matches, which re- sulted as follows: Won 5, lost 7, drawn 1. They also reached the final stages of the Widnes and District and Warrington and District Minor Medal Competitions. The committee are anxious that their club should improve on last season's performances, and have signed the fol- lowing players: Shaw, Long, Spruce, Appleton, Foster, Glover, Ryan, D. Vernon, Challinor and Bevan, of last season's team. Neil and Crozier (of the Excelsior) have also signed for the club. But they will not have the services of Shuttle. worth and Hunt, both these players being mem- bers of the Territorial Force and having been called up.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>_FAIRFIELD CLUB.</head>
        <p>_FAIRFIELD CLUB.</p>
        <p>This club 1;a;-1;g-a-i;11.7;i;;Nrtifieleague for the coming season, and are making efforts to build up a strong team, and have obtained the follow- ing signatures; H. Monks, T. Howard, T. Webb, Osborne, Jackson, Appleton, Bates, Bolton, Broadhurst and Hanablett, last season's players, and Hy. Challinor, S. Johnson, Townley, Law- less, and J. Howard are new players for the club. The secretarial duties will be carried out by Mr. C. E. Taylor, n, Marshhouse-lane, and a very strong committee has been elected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>FAIRFIELD UNITED.</head>
        <p>FAIRFIELD UNITED.</p>
        <p>The above club has again entered the League, and the members have elected Mr. H. Clayton, 50, Padgate-lane, as treasurer and hon. secretary, with a strong committee. Most of last season's players are again available, and they will have the able assistance of W. Hunter, of Warring- ton Albion fame, and the younger Woodhouse. who is only 15 years of age and gives a very good exhibition of defensive play.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>EARLESTOWN Y.M.C.A.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN Y.M.C.A.</p>
        <p>The above is one of the new recruits to the Warrington League, and this is their first season in League football since the club was re-formed about four seasons ago. During that time they have been engaged in friendly encounters with many clubs in the Warrington district, and have met with their share of success. At their general meeting they decided to again take part in League contest. They can rely on the majority of last season's players to turn out again, and have also signed on several new players, who have shown much promise during the practice games. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>EARLESTOWN ST. JOHN'S MISSION.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN ST. JOHN'S MISSION.</p>
        <p>This club i; one of the most promising mem- bers of the League, having previously been con- nected with the St. Helens Sunday School League, and always occupying fifth or sixth ppm_ tion in the League chart. From a playing point of view they were fairly successful last season, and the playeors are very enthusiastic over their successful application for membership into the Warrington League. They have got good dress- ing accommodation for visiting teams in the Mission School, and an enclosed ground near by, also several promising players for the coming campaign. PADDINGTON CLUB.</p>
        <p>The following have been elected as officers for the season: President, Mr. Ed. Gorton; vice- presidents, Mr. W. Jump and Mr. A. Hughes; chairman, W. B. Smith; treasurer and lion. secretary, Hy. Range, Highfield-terrace, Pad- dington. The club-house and dressing-rooms are at the Dog and Partridge. The secretary's report showed the club to be in a very satisfac- tory position, and last season their team played 20 matches, winning 16, lost 2; and drawn 2, with 52 goals for and 25 against. The club have joined the Warrington and District League. and have retained all last season's players, and are looking forward to making a good position on the League table.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>LATCHFORD BAPTISTS.</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD BAPTISTS.</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptists at their general meeting elected the following to office: President, Mr. I. Wilkinson; vice-presidents, Messrs. J. Uns- worth and F. Grounds; treasurer. Jos. Wilkin- son; hon. secretary, W. T. Wilkinson, 550, Knutsford-road, Warrington. The secretary's report for last season was very satisfactory, and showed the team to have played 36 matches, having won 23, lost 4, and drawn 9, with a goal average of 2 against 1 in the club's favour. For this season they have been elected to member- ship of the Warrington and District Leagne, Second Division, and the following players who played last season have signed on again for the club. Wright, Albinson. Noden, Hardy, Peers, Clarke, Hardy, jun., Blease and E. Jackson. Dunn and Rushton are amongst the new mem- bers and should strengthen the team. Ken- wright, last season's captain, will be missing, as he has been called up with the local Territorial force.</p>
        <p>ELMWOOD AVENUE CONGREGATIONAL.</p>
        <p>Only formed last seasonl. this club played friendly matches with very satisfactory results, and have joined the Warrington and District League, Second Division. At the general meet- ing the following were elected as team's com- mittee: Messrs. W. Evans, H. Farrar, H. Hamil- ton, R. Wallington; hon. secretary, John Pluenix, 82, Dalton-bank, Warrington. The players, who performed so well for the club last season have all been retained, with the excep- tion of two. New players include Wells, Ash- ton, Haze Mine and R. Knight, the latter keep- ing goal last season for Fairfield United. The club officials are hopeful of obtaining the ser- vices for outside left position of a player who has had Scottish Junior Cup experience. Jas. Phenix. who made himself very popular last season, has been elected captain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>ORFORD WHITE STAR.</head>
        <p>ORFORD WHITE STAR.</p>
        <p>At the general meeting the following were elected officers for the coming season: President, Mr. J. Ashton; treasurer, F. Eaton; lion. secre- tary, T. Turner, 37, Forshaw-street, Warring- ton; committee, Messrs. Gleaves, Buckley, Rudge and Jones. It was decided to join the Warrington and District. League, Second Divi-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <p>I</p>
        <p>sion. Amongst the players signed on are Picker- ing, G. Redford, J. H. Berry, .Hatton, J. Red- ford, Day, Caldwell, Gregory-, and Miller. HOLLINS GREEN.</p>
        <p>The members are new aspirants to League honours. The club was only inaugurated last October, and a list of friendly matches was played, and 22 matches were decided, of which nine were won, eight lost, and five drawn, 42 goals against 37 being scored. The officials for this season are: President, the Rev. F. A. White; vice-president, Mr. H. Atkinson; trea- surer, Mr. F. Perrin; hon. secretary, Mr. Jos. Arstall; committee, Messrs. J. Cleary, J. W. Perrin, H. Perrin, J. Blackburn, G. Adams. J. Tongue, and W. Arstall; and the club mem- bership is 30. All last season's players have signed forms and are anticipating a successful season in their new sphere.</p>
        <p>AMotor-cyclesand doceenorlee. Repairs by competent men. Petrol and tyres. W. KNIGHT, STOCKTON HEATH GARAGE, LONDON ROAD. Telephone 63 Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>NEWTON CONGREGATIONALISTS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON CONGREGATIONALISTS.</p>
        <p>The members are entering upon their first season of League football, having previoasly participated in friendly games. The playing members of the club have plenty of confidence in their abilities to give their opponents a good game, and have a strong club memisn'sfiii, to support them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>LONGFORD UNITED.</head>
        <p>LONGFORD UNITED.</p>
        <p>The club was admitted to membership of the League at a special meeting. Last season they played friendlies and created a good im- pression by their sound display in the Widnes and Warrington Medal Competitions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>WARRINGTON CENTRAL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CENTRAL.</p>
        <p>This club is looking forward to the season, and the members are expecting, by their amal- gamation with St. George's Mission, to place a strong team in the field. The latter were members of the Minor Competition last season. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. First Division Abandoned.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the League was held on Tues- day night at the Lion Hotel, when Mr. R. Garner presided. Theit was a discussion as to what course the League should adopt, as several clubs have lost many of their players owing to the war. There were only six dubs represented for the First Division, and Latch- ford, Grappenhall, Weaver Refiners and Cros- field's could not guarantee to be able to place full teams in the field, and Orford St. John's were doubtful, Great Sankey being the only club under the present conditions to play a good team. The meeting decided to abandon the First Division for this season.</p>
        <p>It was decided to carry on the Second Divi- sion, and after hearing applications from Long- ford United and Warrington Central they were admitted to the League, making the number of teams entered to 14. This was agreed to by the Chairman's casting vote. A letter was read from the Secretary of the Warrington Athletic Club, asking for the sup- port of the League in the formation of the Warrington Athletes' Volunteer Force. It was derided that the clubs should place the matter before their respective committees and report to the next meeting. MATCHES FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Hollins Green v. Earlestown Y.M.C.A. ; J. T. Hadkiss. Elmwood-avenue Congregationalists v. Fair- field A.F.C.; W. Dennett. Fairfield v. Orford White Star; D. Howell. Warrington Central v. Earlestown St. John's Mission.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Padgate United play Risley with: P. Gleave; Warburton and Sankey; Hodkinson, Gilbert, and Critchley; James Houghton, John Hough- ton, Garnett, Higginson, and S. Hardman (captain). Reserves, Yates and Llewellyn.</p>
        <p>ELMWOOD-AVENUE CONGREGATIONAL. Help for the Territorials.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this club the members decided to give their strongest support to the special appeal which is being put forward by Mrs. W. Roberts to provide some winter clothing for the Warrington Territorials who are engaged in the war. The receipts which are to be taken at their two home engagements at foot- hail and the proceeds of a concert which the lady members have arranged are to be devoted to the Appeal Fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>'MER</head>
        <p>'MER</p>
        <p>63 LONG ACRE. LONDON</p>
