      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>EXAMINER, S ATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 5, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, S ATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 5, 1914.</p>
        <p>PROGRESS OF THE VYAR.</p>
        <p>IN BELGIUM. LOUVAIN WIPED OUT. German Savagery At Its Worst.</p>
        <p>FRIGHT, ARSON AND MURDER.</p>
        <p>The Germans have destroyed Louvain. An- other pile of smoking ruins shows the world what every country on which the Kaiser sets his heel has to fear.</p>
        <p>The Press Bureau on Friday last week issued the following statement The Belgian Minister in London has re- ceived the following telegram from the Bel- gian Minister for Foreign Affairs:— On Tuesday evening a German corps, after receiving a check, withdrew in disorder into the town of Louvain. A German guard at the entrance to the town mistook the nature of this incursion and fired on their routed fellow-countrymen, mistaking them for Bel-</p>
        <p>gians. In spite of all denials from the authorities, the Germans, in order to cover their mistake,. pretended that it was the inhabitants who nad fired on them ,whereas the inhabitants, including the police, had been disarmed more than a week ago.</p>
        <p>INTELLECTUAL METROPOWS A HEAP</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>OF ASHES.</head>
        <p>OF ASHES.</p>
        <p>Without inquiry and without listening to any protests the German Commander-in-Chief announced that the town would be immedi- ately destroyed. The inhabitants were ordered to leave their dwellings; a party of the men were made prisoners and the women and children put into trains, the destination of which is unknown. Soldiers furnished with bombs set fire to all parts of the town. The splendid church of St. Pierre, the 'Uni- versity buildings, the library, and the scien- tific establishment were delivered to the flames. Several notable citizens were shot. A town of 45,000 inhabitants, the intellectual metropolis of the Low Countries since the 15th century, is now no more than a heap of ashes.</p>
        <p>The following item is included in the official war news circulated by the German wireless stations and received by the Marconi Company: According to the "Berliner Tageblatt" Louvain has been bitterly punished for the civilian up- rising, which has now been almost quelled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>OUR ARMY IN FRANCE.</head>
        <p>OUR ARMY IN FRANCE.</p>
        <p>GLORIOUS BRITISH FIGIIT AGAINST GREAT ODDS. Germans Feel the Lion's Claws</p>
        <p>AND COME TO A STAND,</p>
        <p>The official account of the operations in which the British Army has been engaged in France, issued by the War Office on Sunday afternoon,, covered four days' fighting, from Sunday, August 23rd, when our Army was first attacked at Mons, to the evening of Wednesday, the 26th, when its retirement from the Cambrai-le-Cateau line had at last been successfully carried out. The following is the statement communicated by Lord Kitchener, Secretary of State for War: Although the official dispatches from Sir John French on the recent battles have not yet been received, it is possible now to state in general outline what the British share in the recent operations has been. There has, in effect, been a four days' battle— on the 23rd, 24th, 25th and 26th of August. Dur- ing the whole of this period the British troops, in conformity with the general movement of the French armies, were occupied in resisting and checking the German advance and in with- drawing to the new lines of defence. The battle began at Mons, on Sunday, during which day and part of the night the German attack, which was stubbornly pressed and re- peated, was completely checked on the British front.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>STEADY WITHDRAWAL.</head>
        <p>A STEADY WITHDRAWAL.</p>
        <p>On Monday, the 24th, the Germans made vigorous efforts in superior numbers to prevent the safe withdrawal of the British Army and to drive it into the fortress of Maubeuge. This effort was frustrated by the steadiness and skill with which the British retirement was con- ducted, and, as on the previous day, very heavy losses, far in excess of anything suffered by us, were inflicted upon the enemy, who, in dense formation and in enormous masses, marched forward again and yet again to storm the British lines.</p>
        <p>The British retirement proceeded on the 25th with continuous fighting though not on the scale of the previous two days, and by the night of the 25th the British Army occupied the line Cambrai-Landrecies-le-Cateau. It had been intended to resume the retirement at daybreak on the 26th, but the German at- tack, in which no . less than five corps were engaged, was so close and fierce that it was not Possible to carry out this intention until the afternoon.</p>
        <p>The battle on this day, August 26th, was of the most severe and desperate character. T'he troops offered a superb and most stubborn re- sistance to the tremendous odds with which they were confronted, and at length extricated themselves in good order, though with serious losses and under the heaviest artillery fire. No guns were taken by the enemy except those the horses of which were killed, or which were shattered by high explosive shells.</p>
        <p>OUR LOSSES HEAVY; ENEMY'S LOSSES ENORMOUS. Germans Pay the Extreme Penalty. Sir John French estimates that during the whole of these operations, from the 23rd to the 26th inclusive, his losses amount to 5,000 or 6,000 men. On the other hand the losses suf- fered by the Germans in their attacks across the open, and through their dense formations, are out of all proportion to those which we have suffered.</p>
        <p>In Landrecies alone, on the 26th as an in- stance, a German Infantry Brigade advanced in the closest order into the narrow street, which they completely filled. Our machine guns were brought to bear on this target from the end of the town. The head of the column was swept away, and frightful panic ensued, and it is estimated that no less than 800 to 900 dead and wounded Germans were lying in this street alone.</p>
        <p>Another incident which may be chosen from many like it was the charge of the German 'Guard Cavalry Division upon the British 12th Infantry Brigade, when the German Cavalry were thrown back with great loss and in abso- lute disorder. These are notable examples of what has taken place over practically the whole ' front during these engagements, and the Ger- mans have been made to pay the extreme pricel for every forward march they have made. Since the 26th, apart from cavalry fighting, the British Army has not been molested. It has rested and re-fitted after its exertions and glorious achievements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>READY FOR THE NEXT ROUND.</head>
        <p>READY FOR THE NEXT ROUND.</p>
        <p>Reinforcements amounting to double the loss suffered have already joined. Every gun has been replaced and the Army is now ready to take part in the next great encounter with un- diminished strength. and undaunted spirit. To-day the news is again favourable. The British have not been engaged, knit the French armies, acting vigorously. on their right and left, have for the time being brought the Ger- man attack to a standstill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>GERMAN_ CAVALRY ROUTED.</head>
        <p>GERMAN_ CAVALRY ROUTED.</p>
        <p>Sir John French also reports that on the 28th the sth British Cavalry Brigade, under General Chetwode, fought a brilliant action with the German Cavalry, in the course of which the 12th Lancers and Royal Scots Greys routed the enemy, aml speared large numbers in flight,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>BRITISH</head>
        <p>BRITISH</p>
        <p>CASUALTIES.</p>
        <p>LARGE NUMBER OF MEN "MISSING."</p>
        <p>On Tuesday night the War Office gave the first details of the British losses in Southern Belgium and Northern France. From these it appears that three divisions, less one brigade, and one cavalry brigade lost 36 officers killed, 57 wounded, and 95 missing, 188 all told; and 127 men killed, 629 wounded, and 4,183 missing, making 4,939 men, or a full total of 5,127 casual- ties. The high percentage of missing will be re- marked at once, but it must be remembered that the troops were fighting rearward actions, which always mean an indeterminate loss classified as missing, and including killed, wounded, pris- oners, and stragglers. Many of the British miss- ing are known to be amongst hitherto untraced wounded sent down country during the fighting. LOSSES AMONG CHESHIRE OFFICERS. The War Office issued on Wednesday night a report received from the General Headquar- ters of the Expeditionary Force, on September 1, which included the following casualties in the Cheshire Regiment :</p>
        <p>Officers Missing. Boger, Lieut.-Col. D. C. Bolton, Sec. Lieut. R. H. Campbell, Lieut. C. A. Chetwsnd-Stapylton, Major B. H. Dugmore, Capt. W. L. E. R. Dyer, Capt. A. J. L. Elliott, Lieut. W. G. It. Fairweather, Sec. Lieut. I. Frost, Lieut. T. L. Jackson, Capt. E. A. Jacobs, Lieut. G. S. Joliffe, Capt. C. E. Jones, Capt. E. R. Massey, Capt. B. E. Matterson, Lieut. C. A. B. Randall, Lieut. H. C. Stewart, Sec. Lieut. W. L. Tahourdin, Capt. V. R.</p>
        <p>LOSSES TOTAL 10,345. A second statement of British losses in France has been received from the army headquarters. The casualties number 5,218, of whom 182 are officers. Once more the immense majority are " missing," 4,758 officers and men coming under that heading. As regards the men so returned, the official statement says many of them are among 2,682 soldiers sent back to the base as unfit. The others are prisoners, stragglers, wounded, and dead left on the field. With the 5,127 casualties previously reported, the British losses now total 10,345, less those of the missing now at the base.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>ALSACE-LORRAINE</head>
        <p>ALSACE-LORRAINE</p>
        <p>FRENCH RIGIIT 'WING ADVANCES</p>
        <p>IN LORRAINE.</p>
        <p>Last week-end in Lorraine the progress of the French forces was accentuated. We are mas- ters of La Montane and our right is advancing. A severe action has been in progress since the 28th in the Launoy (? Launois), Signy P Abbaye region, south-west of Mezieres. On our left wing a battle has been carried on by four Army Corps. The right of these corps took the offensive and drove back to Guise and to the west the 10th German Army Corps and the Guard, which had considerable losses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>NORTH SEA VICTORY. –</head>
        <p>NORTH SEA VICTORY. -</p>
        <p>GERMAN 'CRUISERS AND DESTROYERS SUNK.</p>
        <p>A Brilliant Exploit.</p>
        <p>The British .naval victory—which f` The Examiner" was the only local paper to report last Saturday—which took place in the North Sea last Friday, Was one of the most brilliant of the exploits that stand to the credit of the British Navy. The official account of the action, as issued at nine o'clock on Friday night, stated : Early this morning a concerted operation of some consequence was attempted against the Germans in the Heligoland Bight. Strong forces of destroyers, supported by light cruisers and battle cruisers, and working in conjunction with submarines, intercepted and attacked the Ger- man destroyers and cruisers guarding the ap- proaches to the German coast. According to the information that has reached the Admiralty so far, the operation has been fortunate and fruitful. The British destroyers have been heavily engaged with the enemy's destroyers 4 All British destroyers are reported afloat, and returning in good order. Two German destroyers were sunk and many damaged. The enemy's cruisers were engaged by the British cruisers and battle cruisers. The First Light Cruiser Squadron sank the Mainz, receiv- ing only slight damage. The First Battle- Cruiser Squadron sank one cruiser, Kola class, and another cruiser disappeared in the mist, heavily on fire, and in a sinking condition. All the German cruisers which engaged were thus disposed of. _ _</p>
        <p>The Battle-Cruiser Squadron, although attacked by submarines and floating mines, suc- cessfully evaded them, and is undamaged. The Light Cruiser Squadron suffered no cas- ualties.</p>
        <p>The Flotilla cruiser Amethyst [since ascer- tained to be the Arethusa] and the destroyer Laertes are damaged. No other vessels are seri- ously injured. .British loss of life reported not heavy. The Commanding Officers concerned in this skilfully-handled operation were Rear-Admirals Beatty, Moore, and Christian, and Commodores Keyes, Tyrwhitt, and Goodenough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>ARETHUSA'S FINE QUALITIES.</head>
        <p>ARETHUSA'S FINE QUALITIES.</p>
        <p>German Sailors Shot By Their Officers.</p>
        <p>The Press Bureau has since issued the follow- ing account of the engagement in the North Sea In the action of the 28th instant off Heligo- land the light armoured 'cruiser Arethusa, and not the Amethyst, as previously stated, played the principal part. This vessel, which is the first of the 20 built under the present Board of Admiralty, carried the broad pennant of Com- modore Tyrwhitt, commanding the flotillas of the First Fleet. The principle of the operation was a scooping movement by a strong force of destroyers headed by the Arethusa to cut the German light craft from home and engage them I at leisure in the open sea. The Arethusa, lead- ing the line of destroyers, was first attacked by two German cruisers and was sllarply engaged for 35 minutes at a range of about 3,000 yards, with the result that she sustained some damage and casualties, but drove off the two German cruisers, one of which she seriously injured with her 6-inch guns. Later in the morning she engaged at intervals</p>
        <p>..</p>
        <p>two other German vessels, which were encoun- tered in the confused fighting which followed, and, in company with the Fearless and the Light Cruiser Squadron, contributed to the sinking ot the cruiser Mainz.</p>
        <p>OUR SUPERIORITY DEMONSTRATED.</p>
        <p>- -...-.... In these encounters the Arethusa's speed was reduced to 10 knots, and many of her guns were disabled, and at 1 o'clock she was about to be attacked by two other cruisers of the German ' Town class When , the Battle Cruiser Squadron most opportunely arrived and pursued and sank these new antagonists. The armoured protec- tion, speed, and fighting qualities of the Arethusa class have now been vindicated, and this is satisfactory in view of the fact that a large number of these valuable and unique vessels will join the Fleet in the next few months. It musk be remembered that the Arethusa had only been commissioned a few days before as an emergency ship and that 4e officers and crew were new to each other and to her. In these circumstances the series of actions which they fought during the morning is extremely creditable and adds another page to the annals of a famous ship. _ _</p>
        <p>Although only two of the enemy's destroyers were actually observed to sink, most of the 18 or 20 boats rounded up and attacked were well punished and only saved themselves by scattered flight. The superior gun-power and strength of the British destroyers ship for ship was conclu- sively demonstrated. The destroyers themselves did not hesitate to engage the enemy's cruisers both With guns and torpedoes with hardihood, and two of them—the Laurel and Liberty—got knocked about in the process. Intercepted German signals and other in- formation from German sources confirms the report of Rear-Admiral Beatty as to the sinking of the third German cruiser, which now appears to have been the Ariadne.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>RUSSIANS ARE COMING.</head>
        <p>THE RUSSIANS ARE COMING.</p>
        <p>It must be remembered throughout that the operations in France, vast though they are, are only one wing of the whole field of battle. The strategic position of ourselves and our Allies is such that whereas a decisive victory to our arms in France would probably be fatal to the enemy, the continuance of resistance by the Anglo-French amides upon such a scale as to keep in the closest grip the enemy's best troops can, if prolonged, lead only to one conclusion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>GERMAN ADVANCE. BRITISH BLOCK THE WAY TO</head>
        <p>GERMAN ADVANCE. BRITISH BLOCK THE WAY TO</p>
        <p>PARIS.</p>
        <p>A Foot-by-Foot Struggle.</p>
        <p>Lord Kitchener's message to the nation as to the state of •affairs up to last Sunday was followed by a long silence, but we learnt enough indirectly to know that the German right wing was making considerable progress towards Paris, if at a terrible cost. An offi- cial communique issued on Monday in Paris stated that the French were slowly advancing in the region of the Vosges and in Lorraine, where a regular siege had been in progress for two days. We beat the' Crown Prince's Army in the regions of Spincourt and Longuyon, said the document. We suffered partial checks in the region of Neufchatetti. and Paliseul, which obliged us to withdraw towards the Meuse.</p>
        <p>A general engagement has now begun in the region between the Meuse and Bethel.. The Franco-British have been attacked by the enemy in greatly superior numbers and in the Cateau-Cambrai district have retired to- wards the south.</p>
        <p>A general battle began the day before yester- day in the region of St. Quentin and Vervins, and simultaneously in the region of Horn and Peronne. Our right drove back the Prussian Guard and Tenth Army Corps in the Oise, but on account of the progress of the German right wing we again retired. Our armies are nowhere broken through. The morale of the troops is excellent. The losses •sustained are filled up by the dispatch of re- serves.</p>
        <p>From this and other indications it was ob- vious that the critical section of this tremen- dous battle line was that which lies in the neighbourhood of La Fere, Leon, and Reims, the fortified positions which form the base of a triangle whose apex is Paris. Here the finest corps of the Kaiser's Army were making con- tinuous efforts to turn the Allies' left. But they are losing heavily, says the "Daily Chronicle," and so far as can be ascertained from the limited information available are mak- ing but slow progress. On more the British troops were in the forefront of the battle, and once more they are fighting stubbornly against tremendous odds, holding the road to Paris.</p>
        <p>• TEN GUNS CAPTURED. Paris, Wednesday (4 p.m.).</p>
        <p>An official communique issued this afternoon says that yesterday a German Cavalry Corps marching on the forest of Compiegne had an engagement with a British force, which cap- tured ten guns from the Germans._</p>
        <p>Another German Cavalry Corps has advanced to the line of Soissons—Anizy—Le Cateau. In the Rethel and Meuse districts the enemy has shown no activity.</p>
        <p>11 p.m. On our left wing, in conseqtience of the con- tinuation of the German enveloping movement, and in order to avoid accepting a decisive action, which might have been engaged in under unfavourable conditions, our troops have fallen back partly towards the south-west. The action in the Rethel district has resulted in checking the enemy for the time being. As Compiegne is only 45 miles from Paris this "success" was' looked upon as somewhat ominous.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>GALLANT SOUTH LANCASHIRES HARD</head>
        <p>GALLANT SOUTH LANCASHIRES HARD</p>
        <p>HIT.</p>
        <p>" The Times" on il;i;;sday published a dis- patch from its Paris correspondent, who de- scribed typical incidents of the fighting in which British troops have been engaged, and records many evidences of their gallantry in action.</p>
        <p>As tar as I can learn, said the correspondent in question, by. stories which are corroborated, the Lancashire regiments have suffered most. The South and East Lancashires were particu- larly tried. At Mons in a three days' battle two regiments lost a considerable number of men.</p>
        <p>The South Lancashires were most hardly hit. Placed in an exposed position on the left flank, they were raked by machine guns firing under cover of a wood. There was a momentary wav- ering, but the major rallied the men by calling "Be Englishmen," and Englishmen they were. One fellow carried on his back for eight hun- dred yards his wounded officer, a young sub- altern who had been struck in the region of the heart. The stretcher-bearers then bore the wounded man to the hospital, but he died on the way. _ . _</p>
        <p>At the great battle of Solemes, fought on Wednesday, a division appeared to have been practically encircled by the Germans. This was due to failure of the support to turn up until too late. Literally the men had to kew their way out with the bayonet under a ter- rible shell fire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>GERMANS NEAR PARIS.</head>
        <p>GERMANS NEAR PARIS.</p>
        <p>FRENCH GOVERNMENT "REMOVE" TO BORDEAUX.</p>
        <p>Unconquerable Determination.</p>
        <p>Yesterdays reports showed that the Germans were near Paris and that President Poincare and the French Government had left Paris for Bordeaux. General Gallieni, Military Governor of the capital, remains in complete command. He has issued a declaration of his purpose to defend the city until the last. A Proclamation issued in Paris by the Presi- dent of the Rertublic and the Ministry assured the French nation that provision had been made, for the defence of the city; and that the Govern- ment's action has been taken "on the demand of the military authorities." The nation was exhorted "to endure and fight," relying on its own unconquerable determination, and on the assistance of the Allied British, Russian and Belgian forces.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>GERMANS CLAIM SUCCESSES,</head>
        <p>GERMANS CLAIM SUCCESSES,</p>
        <p>But Our Position Remains Strong.</p>
        <p>Berlin official reports said important successes had been won over the Allied armies in North- ern and Eastern France. According to these re- ports, between Verdun arid Rheims ten French army corps have been defeated, and on the northern front also the defenders have been driven back. In one place, it is alleged a Brit- ish battalion was captured. Though the invader has,succeeded in advanc- ing almost to the outskirts of Paris, there is every reason to believe that his prospects are less favourable than they have been hitherto. The defences of Paris are strong. On the west the Allies hold the line of the Seine, on the east that of the Marne. Nowhere, we are assured, has their front been broken. The spirit of the British and French Armies remains excellent. Their long retreat from the</p>
        <p>Belgian frontier has been carried out with a stubborn determination which is the greatest hope for the future. There is no doubt whatever that the losses of the Germans have been far heavier than those suffered by the Allies, and there are indications, says "The Times," that it will not be possible much longer for the German commander to carry out the plan of massed attack in close formation which has been so successful up to the present, but has exacted so heavy a toll in Ger- man lives.</p>
        <p>PARIS THE PIVOT OF OPERATIONS. Paris, September 3. In the course of an interview an important personage in French military circles deplared that the decision to remove the Government to Bordeaux was not arrived at because there was any fear of Paris being cut off from the rest of France. As a matter of fact, no one believes for a moment that there is even a possibility of Paris being invested at the present time. It is clear that the entrenched camp outside Paris will in all probability become the pivot of mili- tary movements. The troops will pass through the capital, making sure at frequent intervals that communication with the left wing, the centre, and the right is intact.—" The Times."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>SAVING GERMANS.</head>
        <p>SAVING GERMANS.</p>
        <p>The British destroyers exposed themselves to considerable risk in endeavouring to save as many as possible of the drowning German sailors. The British officers present vouch for the fact that German officers were observed fir- ing at their own men in the water with pistols, and that several were shot before their eyes under these peculiar circumstances. The destroyer Defender was actually picking up wounded with her boats when she was driven off by the approach of another German cruiser and had to leave two of her boats, con- taining one officer and nine men, behind. It was feared that these had been made prisoners, but happily Submarine "E 4" arrived and brought the British party home. As it was not possible to accommodate the 30 Germans in the submarine, they were allowed to return to Ger- many in the boat under the charge of an ober- lieutenant, who was Unwounded. THE ENEMY'S LOSS.</p>
        <p>The complements of the five German vessels known to have been sunk aggregated about 1,200 officers and men, all of whom, with the excep- tion of these 30, and about 300 wounded and ,1 unwounded prisoners, perished. Besides this there is the loss, which must have been severe,. on board the German torpedo-boats and other cruisers which did not sink during the action. The total British casualties amounted to 69 killed and wounded, among whom must, how- ever, be included killed two officers of excep- tional merit—Lieutenant-Commander Nigel K. W. Barttelot and Lieutenant Eric W. P. West- macott. All the British ships will be fit for ser- vice in a week or ten days. The success of this operation was due in the first instance to the information brought to the Admiralty by the submarine officers, who have during the past three weeks showed extraordin- ary daring and enterprise in penetrating the enemy's .waters.. _ .</p>
        <p>The First Lord has telegraphed to the Ameri- can Ambassador at Berlin desiring him to in- form Grand Admiral von Tirpitz that his son has been saved and is unwounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>GERMAN ACCOUNT. "British Sent Out Lifeboats to Save</head>
        <p>GERMAN ACCOUNT. "British Sent Out Lifeboats to Save</p>
        <p>Our Men."</p>
        <p>A striking story of the naval action off Heligo- land was sent out on Tuesday in the official wire- less message circulated from Berlin and received in London by the Marconi Company. It de- scribes the end of the Ariadne—the German cruiser which in the first British account was mentioned as "disappearing in the mist on fire and in a sinking condition." The message rtiriS</p>
        <p>Berlin, Monday. The following is an official account of the sink- ing of the torpedo boat V 187:— During a fog the vessel was unexpectedly at- tacked on all sides by British torpedo destroyers and submarines. She defended herself with all her might, but the sharp firing at close range reduced her moving capacity. There was no possibility of escaping from the enemy's fire, and the vessel turned on her enemies either to fight a passage through or to engage them in battle to the end. When she was no longer able to move she was blown up to prevent her from falling into the hands of the enemy, and sank quickly. The chief of the flotilla and captain of the corvette Wallis and Captain Lieutenant Lechler died like heroes.</p>
        <p>It must be adinitieCl-t-Q--tlieliritish, without stopping to consider their own danger, sent out lifeboats in order to save our men.</p>
        <p>The Fighting Spirit of the German Navy. Summoned by the thunder of the guns, the small cruiser A riadne rushed to the assistance of the outpost fighting forces, and the guns were in the meanwhile silenced. Retreat was not in accordance with the fighting spirit of the Ger- man navy, and the Ariadne began to pursue the enemy, whose vessels were, however, hidden by the fog. ' Suddenly new gun firing was heard, and two English ,armoured cruisers of the Lion class were bombarding a German vessel to NI-hose assistance- the Ariadne was sailing. A shell struck the boiler-room and put half of the boilers out of action, and reduced her speed to 16 miles. The unequal battle raged for another half-hour, the stern of the ship being at the time ablaze, but the other guns continued to fire. The enemy in the meantime turned to- wards the west.</p>
        <p>The brave Ariadne was doomed to destruc- tion ; and, with three "Hurrahs" for the Kaiser and the singing of the Song of the Colours and "Deutschland über alles," the ship was aban- doned in perfect order, and sank shortly after- wards. The chief officer, Captain Franck; the ship's doctor, Ritter Roxberger; the officer of the watch, Helbing; and about 70 members of the crew have fallen, and many have been in- jured.</p>
        <p>Although this engagement with a superior op- posing force was not successful, it furnished a proof of the eagerness of our men to fight, their tough endurance, and their great courage. The enemy on their own admission have suffered heavy losses.</p>
        <p>GERMANY'S SEA LOSSES. When war began Germany had six ships with a speed of 28 knots or over. One, the Goeben, has been sold to Turkey, and one, the Mainz, sunk by the British. All three German cruisers destroyed behind Heligoland were apparently of the same type, vessels of 4,500 tons burden; armed with twelve 4in. guns, and manned by crews 380 strong. Their highest speed varies from 26 to 28 knots. Germany's modern de- stroyers vary in displacement between 330 tons and 650, and their crews between 100 and- 180 men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-05_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>BRITISH GUNBOAT SUNK. Strikes Mine in North Sea.</head>
        <p>BRITISH GUNBOAT SUNK. Strikes Mine in North Sea.</p>
        <p>Official Press Bureau, Thursday, A report from the commanding officer of H.M.S. Speedy states that the steam drifter Linsdell struck a mine yesterday morning 30 miles off the East Coast and sank. A quarter of an hour later H.M.S. Speedy also struck a mine and sank. The casualties reported are as follows The Speedy. Missing: E. A. Claxton, officers' Steward, second-class pensioner, 157,283. Seriously injured: Mr. Alfred S. Bright; Gun- ner Frederick William Lee, petty officer, official number 200,081.</p>
        <p>The Linsdell. Missing : The skipper, Mr. Woodgate, R.N.R., and four members of the crew. The remainder of the crew had been picked up by the Speedy before she struck the second mine. The Speedy was an old torpedo gunboat of 810 tons and moderate speed, and was built in 1893. Her armament consisted of two 4.7 in. guns and four three-pounders. Previous to the declara- tion of war she was engaged on fishery protec- tion duties in the North Sea.</p>
        <p>%.!</p>
