        <p>of our peace force and the reinforcement of our League of Peace." Sincerely, or otherwise, every European statesman has preached the doctrine that vast armaments stand for peace. How stands the doctrine in the light of present events? Ger- many, where this teaching had its fullest practical expression, has been the State, if not alone, at least more than any other, which has brought about this European cataclysm. Ido not see that any unprejudiced investigator can come to any other conclusion, as he reads the history of the negotiations preceding this war as revealed in the White Paper.</p>
        <p>ing to the Primitive Methodists, whose team they occasionally played at cricket. Some- times they " licked them," and sometimes they did not, but that was his relationship with , Mr. Chessum. (Laughter.) He thanked his own people for the kindness with which they had received him, as well as his mother and sister. During his ministry he would try to treat all his people alike. (Loud cheers.) VOTE OF THANKS. Mr. J. W. SLACK moved, and Mr. EDWARD WALLINGTON seconded, a comprehensive vote of thanks to all who had taken part in the wel- come meeting. This was carried with accla- mation. During the evening the following songs were given: "Zurnmerzetzhire" and "The moun- tains of Mourn," by Mr. Arthur Davies; "Little grey home in the West," by Mrs. Smallshaw; and a duet, "The spider and the fly," by Miss M. Senior and Mr. W. H. Wallington. Mish Ethel Wells gave a recitation.</p>
